WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.250
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Welcome to
the Huberman Lab podcast,

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:04.920
where we discuss science
and science-based tools

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:05.970
for everyday life.

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:09.030
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:10.860
I'm Andrew Huberman,
and I'm a professor

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:12.900
of neurobiology
and ophthalmology,

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.520
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.650
Today, my guest is Rick Rubin.

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:18.390
Rick Rubin is
credited with being

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:20.190
one of the most creative
and prolific music

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:21.910
producers of all time.

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:24.660
The range of artists with whom
he's worked with and discovered

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:26.670
is absolutely
staggering, ranging

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:30.150
from artists such as LL Cool
J, Public Enemy, Minor Threat,

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:34.050
Fugazi, Beastie Boys, Jesus
and Mary Chain, Jay-Z, Red Hot

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:37.260
Chili Peppers, Metallica,
Green Day, Tom Petty, System

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:40.830
of a Down, Joe Strummer, Kanye
West, Johnny Cash, Adele,

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:42.570
and many, many more.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:44.520
Not surprisingly,
therefore, Rick

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.830
is considered
somewhat of an enigma.

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:50.310
That is, people want to know
how it is that one individual is

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:53.490
able to extract the best
creative artistry from so

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:56.940
many different people in so
many different genres of music.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:59.640
Well, as today's
discussion reveals,

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.980
Rick's expertise in
the creative process

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:03.640
extends well beyond music.

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:05.099
In fact, our
conversation takes us

00:01:05.099 --> 00:01:07.680
into the realm of what
the creative process is

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:11.460
specifically and generally
across domains, including

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:15.390
music, of course, but also
writing, film, science,

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:18.910
and essentially, all domains
in which new, original thought,

00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:22.710
ideas, and production of
anything becomes important.

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:25.140
Our conversation ventures
from abstract themes,

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:26.820
such as what is
creativity, and where

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:29.400
does it stem from,
to the more concrete,

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:31.650
everyday, tool-based
approaches to creativity,

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:33.840
including those that
Rick himself uses

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:37.050
and that he's seen other
people use to great success.

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:39.150
That took us down some
incredible avenues,

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:41.640
ranging from a discussion
about the subconscious,

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:43.260
to how the
subconscious interacts

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:45.390
with our conscious mind,
and how the subconscious

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:49.170
and conscious mind interact with
nature around us and within us.

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:52.500
Indeed, our conversation got
rather scientific at times,

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.900
but all with an eye and an
ear toward understanding

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:57.000
the practical tools
that any and all of us

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.300
can use in order to access
the creative process.

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:02.940
We also spent some time talking
about Rick's new book, which

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:06.420
is all about creativity and
ways to access creativity.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:09.750
The title of the book is The
Creative Act: A Way of Being,

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:10.800
by Rick Rubin.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:12.570
This is a book
that I've now read

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:15.420
three times from cover
to cover, and I'm now

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:16.590
reading it a fourth time.

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:19.680
Because it is so rich with
wisdom and information

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.970
that I'm applying in
multiple domains of my life,

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:24.690
not just my work but
my everyday life.

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:26.760
I cannot recommend
it highly enough.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:30.540
Rick has an incredible ability
to translate his understanding

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:34.720
of the creative process in a way
that is meaningful for anybody.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:36.793
So if you're in music,
if you're a musician,

00:02:36.793 --> 00:02:38.460
it will certainly be
meaningful for you,

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:40.140
but it is not about music.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:42.040
It is about the
creative process.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.310
And so whether or not you
consider yourself somebody

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:46.110
creative or not, or
whether or not you

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:49.230
seek to be more creative, Rick's
book and today's conversation

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:52.620
sheds light on what I believe
to be the fundamental features

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:54.510
of what makes us human beings.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:57.450
That is what allows us,
unlike other animals,

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:59.610
to look out on the
landscape around us,

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:02.040
to examine our inner
landscape, and to come up

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.800
with truly novel
ideas that thrill us,

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:07.140
entertain us,
entertain other people,

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:10.320
scare us, make us
laugh, make us cry.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:12.420
All the things
that make life rich

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:15.090
are essentially contained
in the creative process.

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:18.180
And to be able to sit down
and learn from the Rick Rubin

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:20.370
how the creative
process emerges in him

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:23.400
and his observations about how
we can best emerge in others

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:25.320
is and was truly a gift.

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:27.623
So I'm excited to share his
knowledge with you today.

00:03:27.623 --> 00:03:29.040
One thing that
you'll quickly come

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:30.960
to notice about
today's conversation

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.630
is that Rick is incredibly
generous with his knowledge

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:35.040
about the creative process.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:38.280
In fact, he very graciously,
and spontaneously I

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:40.800
should add, offered to
answer your questions

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:41.978
about creativity.

00:03:41.978 --> 00:03:44.520
So if you have questions about
the creative process for Rick,

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:47.310
please, put those in the
Comment section on YouTube.

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.500
And in order to make those
questions a bit easier

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:52.860
for me to find, please, put
"Question for Rick Rubin"

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:56.610
in capitals, then colon or
dash, whichever you choose,

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:58.020
and then put your
question there.

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:00.270
I do ask that you keep the
questions relatively short,

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:03.030
so that I can ask Rick as many
of those questions as possible.

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:04.800
We will record
that conversation,

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:08.220
and we will post it as a clip on
the Huberman Lab Clips channel.

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:09.962
