00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:30.060
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So of
all the topics in science,

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:31.890
and in particular
in neuroscience,

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:36.299
I confess that creativity is the
most difficult one to capture.

00:08:36.299 --> 00:08:40.409
Because you can find
papers, scientific studies

00:08:40.409 --> 00:08:45.030
that is, on convergent thinking
versus divergent thinking,

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:46.620
and there are
definitions to these,

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:49.050
and they take on
different forms.

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:54.320
But in a strict
definition form, it

00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:56.360
seems that creativity
has something

00:08:56.360 --> 00:08:59.840
to do with either
rearranging existing elements

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:02.360
or coming up with new elements.

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:06.980
But as I went into your
book, which I've done twice.

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:08.780
I've read it twice,
and by the way,

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:11.000
I feel so blessed
and honored to have

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:17.660
gotten an early copy from you,
or a final copy early that is.

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:19.280
But having gone
through it twice,

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:24.240
I'm now convinced that
there may not actually

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:28.020
be an internal source
of creativity that

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:29.590
exists on its own right.

00:09:29.590 --> 00:09:32.238
And the example that you
give that, for me, really

00:09:32.238 --> 00:09:34.530
is serving as an anchor, and
tell me if I'm wrong here,

00:09:34.530 --> 00:09:38.340
is this idea that
ideas and creativity

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:39.730
are a little bit like a cloud.

00:09:39.730 --> 00:09:42.690
If you look at it
at one moment, you

00:09:42.690 --> 00:09:45.330
might think that it looks
like one thing, where it has

00:09:45.330 --> 00:09:46.905
a certain shape and texture.

00:09:46.905 --> 00:09:49.530
But then you look at it a moment
later, it could be quite a bit

00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:49.960
different.

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:51.510
And if you look at
it an hour later,

00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:54.000
it very well could be gone.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:57.470
And the reason I
think that serves

00:09:57.470 --> 00:10:00.740
as such a powerful hook for
me to think about creativity

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:03.380
and why I think neuroscientists
and scientists in general

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:06.140
have never actually
captured a way to even talk

00:10:06.140 --> 00:10:09.820
about creativity
stems from somebody

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:13.588
that you knew in person but,
as you know, I greatly admire.

00:10:13.588 --> 00:10:15.130
I don't have many
heroes, but I would

00:10:15.130 --> 00:10:17.890
put Joe Strummer among the short
list of heroes that I have.

00:10:17.890 --> 00:10:22.210
And I remember once an interview
with him fairly disjointed.

00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:26.110
He was off in different
tangents that I couldn't follow.

00:10:26.110 --> 00:10:29.680
But at one point,
he just blurted out

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:33.280
that, if you have an idea,
you have to write it down.

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:36.460
And you may end up throwing
it away, but if you wait,

00:10:36.460 --> 00:10:38.350
it will be gone.

00:10:38.350 --> 00:10:40.283
And I remember that,
and as a consequence,

00:10:40.283 --> 00:10:42.700
I have a whole system that I
use to try and capture ideas.

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:45.690
But what are your
thoughts on what

00:10:45.690 --> 00:10:49.380
Joe said, this cloud
idea that comes up in one

00:10:49.380 --> 00:10:50.790
form in one area of the book?

00:10:50.790 --> 00:10:53.220
But then I think it's
thread throughout the book

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:55.840
in different ways.

00:10:55.840 --> 00:10:58.680
How did that come
to you, and how

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:01.273
does it serve you in trying to--

00:11:01.273 --> 00:11:02.940
I don't want to say
extract-- but trying

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:03.902
to access creativity?

00:11:03.902 --> 00:11:05.610
RICK RUBIN: I think
the best way to think

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:08.500
about it is like a dream.

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:11.610
It's like, if you think
about your dreams,

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:14.400
they don't necessarily
make sense.

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:17.220
When you wake up, you
might remember part but not

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:19.280
the whole thing.

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:24.140
Then, if you start writing
them down, they'll come back,

00:11:24.140 --> 00:11:26.570
and they may not
make sense to you.

00:11:26.570 --> 00:11:31.720
There'll be a series
of abstract images,

00:11:31.720 --> 00:11:34.970
and maybe, someday
in the future,

00:11:34.970 --> 00:11:37.390
you'll be able to look back
and understand what they mean,

00:11:37.390 --> 00:11:38.950
and maybe not.

00:11:38.950 --> 00:11:44.920
And that's how the art
making process works

00:11:44.920 --> 00:11:47.200
is like we're making
things, and we're looking

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:49.420
for feeling in ourselves.

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:54.580
And it could be a feeling
of excitement or enthusiasm,

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:57.940
a feeling of interest,
a feeling of curiosity--

00:11:57.940 --> 00:11:59.395
I want to know more--

00:11:59.395 --> 00:12:02.810
a feeling of leaning forward.

00:12:02.810 --> 00:12:08.990
And we're following that
energy in our body, when

00:12:08.990 --> 00:12:11.452
we feel there's something here.

00:12:11.452 --> 00:12:12.410
There's something here.

00:12:12.410 --> 00:12:13.220
I want to know more.

00:12:13.220 --> 00:12:13.910
I want to know more.

00:12:13.910 --> 00:12:14.840
I want to know more.

00:12:17.660 --> 00:12:19.890
I'll say, it's not an
intellectual process.

00:12:19.890 --> 00:12:21.410
It's a different thing.

00:12:21.410 --> 00:12:23.870
That's why it's hard
even to talk about it,

00:12:23.870 --> 00:12:28.210
because it's so elusive.

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:29.960
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Recently, I was listening

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:34.040
to a podcast by our
friend Lex Fridman.

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:36.410
I think it was an episode
with Balaji Srinivasan,

00:12:36.410 --> 00:12:41.810
where Balaji, who's
an investor-type guy,

00:12:41.810 --> 00:12:44.012
thinker-type guy--

00:12:44.012 --> 00:12:45.470
this is like an
eight-hour episode.

00:12:45.470 --> 00:12:46.940
He says something
at the beginning

00:12:46.940 --> 00:12:49.700
that I'd love your thoughts on.

00:12:49.700 --> 00:12:51.860
He said, look, we
can train a rat

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:56.060
to lever press every
other time or to expect

00:12:56.060 --> 00:12:59.180
reward on every
even number press

00:12:59.180 --> 00:13:02.900
or every odd number press or
even every fifth number press.

00:13:02.900 --> 00:13:06.500
But a human and a
rat can't do that

00:13:06.500 --> 00:13:10.250
for like prime number presses.

00:13:10.250 --> 00:13:11.630
You can't actually train that.

00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:13.922
And then you think about the
reward systems and the way

00:13:13.922 --> 00:13:17.120
that we follow life, from when
we get up until we go to sleep,

00:13:17.120 --> 00:13:21.680
and what he said is the fact
that we can't do that means

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:26.720
that we may not actually
be in touch with the best

00:13:26.720 --> 00:13:28.250
schedules of doing things.

00:13:28.250 --> 00:13:30.080
Like every time I'm
thirsty, I take a sip.

00:13:30.080 --> 00:13:32.390
I assume that's the
right way to do it,

00:13:32.390 --> 00:13:36.710
but it might not be optimal,
for whatever purpose.

00:13:36.710 --> 00:13:41.960
When I was reading your book,
I was thinking about there's

00:13:41.960 --> 00:13:45.270
a set of things
to follow, things

00:13:45.270 --> 00:13:48.060
to pay attention to-- you talk
about this-- things to access,

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:50.280
that none of the
creative process

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:52.260
comes from just within us.

00:13:52.260 --> 00:13:55.450
It can, but it's always being
fed by things outside of it.

00:13:55.450 --> 00:13:57.830
And so what I started to
do is, the second time

00:13:57.830 --> 00:14:00.330
I read through the book, was
think about it through the lens

00:14:00.330 --> 00:14:03.000
of what Balaji was saying
was that there may not even

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:06.180
be a language for
this thing that we

00:14:06.180 --> 00:14:08.040
call accessing creativity.

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:11.610
There's a process, but that
language in the form of words

00:14:11.610 --> 00:14:16.920
is a little bit like trying
to use even numbers to try

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:18.593
and access prime numbers.

00:14:18.593 --> 00:14:19.260
RICK RUBIN: Yes.

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:22.500
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Like the
math becomes so convoluted

00:14:22.500 --> 00:14:25.080
that we end up in a
conversation like this,

00:14:25.080 --> 00:14:28.080
where I'm confident we can
get to the kernels of it.

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:31.470
Because what's remarkable
about the book is that you do.

00:14:31.470 --> 00:14:37.150
You show and inform the process.

00:14:37.150 --> 00:14:41.220
But there may not be a
English or any other language

00:14:41.220 --> 00:14:44.100
for saying, do this, then
this, then this, then this,

00:14:44.100 --> 00:14:47.000
and you'll have something
of creative value.

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:48.030
Does that capture it?

00:14:48.030 --> 00:14:48.697
RICK RUBIN: Yes.

00:14:48.697 --> 00:14:55.810
I think language is insufficient
to drill down on creativity.

00:14:55.810 --> 00:15:01.900
It's closer to magic
than it is science.

00:15:01.900 --> 00:15:05.600
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So when
kids come into the world,

00:15:05.600 --> 00:15:07.390
do you think that
they have better

00:15:07.390 --> 00:15:11.770
access to this creative
process than we do as adults?

00:15:11.770 --> 00:15:15.220
Because we start to impart
rule plays and books.

00:15:15.220 --> 00:15:16.420
Like will it get likes?

00:15:16.420 --> 00:15:17.710
Will people like it?

00:15:17.710 --> 00:15:22.443
But also all the things
that are available to us

00:15:22.443 --> 00:15:24.610
that we're not paying
attention to, like the texture

00:15:24.610 --> 00:15:29.950
of this table, we're discarding
things, systematically.

00:15:29.950 --> 00:15:31.900
We get, quote, unquote,
set in our ways.

00:15:31.900 --> 00:15:34.930
Do you think kids are, just
by definition and by design,

00:15:34.930 --> 00:15:36.460
more creative than adults?

00:15:36.460 --> 00:15:37.360
RICK RUBIN: Yes.

00:15:37.360 --> 00:15:41.560
Kids, they're open, and
they have no baggage.

00:15:41.560 --> 00:15:43.550
They don't have
any belief system.

00:15:43.550 --> 00:15:46.730
They don't know how things
are supposed to work.

00:15:46.730 --> 00:15:50.470
They just see what is, and if
we pay attention to what is,

00:15:50.470 --> 00:15:55.372
we learn much more than if we--

00:15:55.372 --> 00:16:02.590
Most of us select from an
endless number of data points

00:16:02.590 --> 00:16:07.150
available to us to,
well, as a species,

00:16:07.150 --> 00:16:10.870
to make sure that we
don't die and to procreate

00:16:10.870 --> 00:16:12.760
and to feed ourselves
are probably

00:16:12.760 --> 00:16:17.088
the primary functions first.

00:16:17.088 --> 00:16:19.630
And then we learn things about
what's right and what's wrong,

00:16:19.630 --> 00:16:24.520
and we learn things about
how to do certain things.

00:16:24.520 --> 00:16:28.870
Or we're inspired by someone
who makes something we love,

00:16:28.870 --> 00:16:31.120
and we want to do it
the way they do it.

00:16:31.120 --> 00:16:34.780
And all of those things
undermine the purity

00:16:34.780 --> 00:16:36.380
of the creative process.

00:16:36.380 --> 00:16:41.790
They can be tools to
build your skill set

00:16:41.790 --> 00:16:43.240
to be able to do it yourself.

00:16:43.240 --> 00:16:46.920
Like if you're a
singer, you might

00:16:46.920 --> 00:16:51.750
imitate a singer you really like
for a while to get good at it

00:16:51.750 --> 00:16:54.630
and then eventually come
to find your own voice.

00:16:54.630 --> 00:16:57.930
It doesn't always start
with your own voice.

00:16:57.930 --> 00:17:02.060
But if you're three years
old or five years old,

00:17:02.060 --> 00:17:04.829
and you try singing, you're
not singing like anyone else.

00:17:04.829 --> 00:17:07.170
You're singing with
your own voice.

00:17:07.170 --> 00:17:09.000
And when you make
something, you're

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:13.530
making it based on not knowing.

00:17:13.530 --> 00:17:16.579
And I think I had the
advantage, early in my career,

00:17:16.579 --> 00:17:20.540
of starting making music
without any experience, which

00:17:20.540 --> 00:17:24.200
was helpful, because I didn't
know what rules I was breaking.

00:17:24.200 --> 00:17:28.580
And so it wasn't intentional
breaking of rules.

00:17:28.580 --> 00:17:30.980
I just did what
seemed right to me,

00:17:30.980 --> 00:17:33.800
but I didn't realize
that I was doing things

00:17:33.800 --> 00:17:35.702
that other people wouldn't do.

00:17:35.702 --> 00:17:37.160
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
There is this idea

00:17:37.160 --> 00:17:39.980
that there are no new ideas.

00:17:39.980 --> 00:17:42.530
I disagree, because
every once in a while,

00:17:42.530 --> 00:17:44.600
I'll see or hear something
that at least seems

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:47.270
different enough.

00:17:47.270 --> 00:17:50.780
RICK RUBIN: I think
it's a combination of--

00:17:50.780 --> 00:17:55.410
a new combination of existing
ideas presented in a new way.

00:17:55.410 --> 00:17:58.080
I think that's how it works.

00:17:58.080 --> 00:17:59.400
I don't know.

00:17:59.400 --> 00:18:01.490
But I will say, it does
seem like the things that

00:18:01.490 --> 00:18:07.090
are most interesting to me have
a series of familiar elements

00:18:07.090 --> 00:18:09.490
joined together in a way
that it's creating something

00:18:09.490 --> 00:18:11.440
that I've never seen before.

00:18:11.440 --> 00:18:16.210
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You mentioned
that when you are close to

00:18:16.210 --> 00:18:19.720
or you see hints of creativity
that is of real value

00:18:19.720 --> 00:18:24.400
that it's a feeling, and I
also believe that the body is

00:18:24.400 --> 00:18:26.290
a great source of information.

00:18:26.290 --> 00:18:30.293
Which once people will realize
that the brain, of course,

00:18:30.293 --> 00:18:32.710
is in the skull, but the nervous
system extends everywhere

00:18:32.710 --> 00:18:37.357
in the body, the whole
mind-body thing just falls away.

00:18:37.357 --> 00:18:39.190
Philosophers have argued
about this forever,

00:18:39.190 --> 00:18:41.420
but it's a silly argument.

00:18:41.420 --> 00:18:44.180
It's also true
that, God forbid, I

00:18:44.180 --> 00:18:47.870
were to amputate all my
limbs, have them amputated,

00:18:47.870 --> 00:18:50.230
I'd fundamentally still be me.

00:18:50.230 --> 00:18:50.730
Right?

00:18:50.730 --> 00:18:52.490
The same is not
true if we took out

00:18:52.490 --> 00:18:54.990
a big enough chunk of my
brain, and I still survived.

00:18:54.990 --> 00:18:57.300
I would be a fundamentally
different human being.

00:18:57.300 --> 00:19:00.270
I'd still have the same name
and identity and social security

00:19:00.270 --> 00:19:03.800
number, but I would
behave very differently.

00:19:03.800 --> 00:19:04.800
Who knows, maybe better?

00:19:07.380 --> 00:19:14.070
The signals from the body we
know, or at least we assume,

00:19:14.070 --> 00:19:15.360
are pretty generic.

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:18.870
Like I can think of 50
different ways or 100 ways

00:19:18.870 --> 00:19:20.670
that we could talk
about creativity today.

00:19:20.670 --> 00:19:23.070
And we could define it and
redefine it and carve it up

00:19:23.070 --> 00:19:25.740
and serve it up like sushi
in a bunch of different ways.

00:19:25.740 --> 00:19:31.280
But the body sends signals
that most of us are--

00:19:31.280 --> 00:19:33.603
we have a course
understanding of.

00:19:33.603 --> 00:19:36.020
It's like, oh, my stomach
hurts, or my stomach feels good,

00:19:36.020 --> 00:19:38.000
or I'm not sensing my stomach.

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:40.710
Or oh, that feels good.

00:19:40.710 --> 00:19:41.317
It feels warm.

00:19:41.317 --> 00:19:41.900
It feels cold.

00:19:41.900 --> 00:19:44.450
Like most of us aren't
trained in understanding

00:19:44.450 --> 00:19:45.870
how to interpret those signals.

00:19:45.870 --> 00:19:48.740
So it's almost like you have
a few vowels, a few syllables,

00:19:48.740 --> 00:19:50.140
and there isn't a lot more.

00:19:50.140 --> 00:19:52.473
Whereas, we talk about our
thoughts and our experiences,

00:19:52.473 --> 00:19:54.860
depending on how hyper
verbal somebody is

00:19:54.860 --> 00:19:57.260
and how much emphasis they
put on different sounds,

00:19:57.260 --> 00:20:00.260
it's near infinite.

00:20:00.260 --> 00:20:01.970
Not infinite, but near infinite.

00:20:01.970 --> 00:20:07.100
So for you personally,
when you know

00:20:07.100 --> 00:20:11.030
that you're on the end of
a thread of creativity--

00:20:11.030 --> 00:20:12.530
maybe you're listening
to an artist,

00:20:12.530 --> 00:20:14.450
or you're hearing something.

00:20:14.450 --> 00:20:17.450
And you're like there
and the your antennae

00:20:17.450 --> 00:20:19.280
start to deflect
in a certain way.

00:20:19.280 --> 00:20:20.330
Right?

00:20:20.330 --> 00:20:25.520
Do you feel that in your body
as a recognizable sensation,

00:20:25.520 --> 00:20:29.090
or is it a thought
and a sensation?

00:20:29.090 --> 00:20:31.190
RICK RUBIN: It's a
feeling in my body.

00:20:31.190 --> 00:20:33.705
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Is it localized?

00:20:33.705 --> 00:20:34.330
RICK RUBIN: No.

00:20:34.330 --> 00:20:39.220
It's a feeling of--

00:20:39.220 --> 00:20:41.762
I would say it's like
a surge of energy.

00:20:41.762 --> 00:20:43.720
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Do you
remember the first time

00:20:43.720 --> 00:20:45.580
you experienced that?

00:20:45.580 --> 00:20:47.710
RICK RUBIN: Probably
hearing the Beatles, when

00:20:47.710 --> 00:20:49.062
I was three or four years old.

00:20:49.062 --> 00:20:50.770
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Three
or four years old?

00:20:50.770 --> 00:20:51.478
RICK RUBIN: Yeah.

00:20:51.478 --> 00:20:52.372
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Wow.

00:20:52.372 --> 00:20:53.080
RICK RUBIN: Yeah.

00:20:53.080 --> 00:20:54.160
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Is there
something wrong with me

00:20:54.160 --> 00:20:56.050
that the Beatles have
never done it for me?

00:20:56.050 --> 00:20:56.350
RICK RUBIN: No.

00:20:56.350 --> 00:20:58.350
Maybe you just weren't
exposed at the right time

00:20:58.350 --> 00:20:59.080
in the right way.

00:20:59.080 --> 00:21:03.467
There's no right or
wrong way, and everyone--

00:21:03.467 --> 00:21:06.050
I can love the Beatles, and you
can not, and we're both right.

00:21:06.050 --> 00:21:07.090
There's not--

00:21:07.090 --> 00:21:08.350
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'm glad
we can still be friends.

00:21:08.350 --> 00:21:09.130
I was a little concerned.

00:21:09.130 --> 00:21:11.140
I was a little scared to
ask you that question.

00:21:11.140 --> 00:21:12.850
I know my taste in
music is a little bit

00:21:12.850 --> 00:21:16.773
obscure and
fragmentary, but good.

00:21:16.773 --> 00:21:18.190
I've always felt
like, gosh, there

00:21:18.190 --> 00:21:19.523
must be something wrong with me.

00:21:19.523 --> 00:21:21.490
I like their songs,
but they don't--

00:21:21.490 --> 00:21:23.290
there's no juice for me there.

00:21:23.290 --> 00:21:26.320
RICK RUBIN: I think
maybe we'll watch--

00:21:26.320 --> 00:21:30.713
there was an eight-part series
called The Beatles Anthology,

00:21:30.713 --> 00:21:31.630
which is out of print.

00:21:31.630 --> 00:21:33.220
But I can try to
find it somewhere,

00:21:33.220 --> 00:21:35.980
and we can watch that
together, and maybe that'll

00:21:35.980 --> 00:21:38.050
make the case for the Beatles.

00:21:38.050 --> 00:21:39.350
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

00:21:39.350 --> 00:21:41.980
Nothing against them, it's
just I'm always bothering you

00:21:41.980 --> 00:21:43.360
for a story, but like Ramones.

00:21:43.360 --> 00:21:45.797
I saw that, and I was like,
wow, like jeans, aviators,

00:21:45.797 --> 00:21:47.380
everyone had to
change their last name

00:21:47.380 --> 00:21:51.050
to Ramone, a lot of
them hated each other.

00:21:51.050 --> 00:21:54.010
There's so much drama in
there, and three chords

00:21:54.010 --> 00:21:55.900
and just-- but to me,
it just was like, wow,

00:21:55.900 --> 00:21:58.118
like kids from New
York, that energy.

00:21:58.118 --> 00:22:00.160
So I think different things
for different people.

00:22:00.160 --> 00:22:00.660
Right?

00:22:00.660 --> 00:22:01.630
RICK RUBIN: Absolutely.

00:22:01.630 --> 00:22:03.047
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
So that brings me

00:22:03.047 --> 00:22:08.380
to a question of when something
feels creatively right,

00:22:08.380 --> 00:22:11.407
and you're sensing
it, and you're there,

00:22:11.407 --> 00:22:13.240
let's say in the studio
or maybe even you're

00:22:13.240 --> 00:22:16.300
listening to something
that somebody sent you,

00:22:16.300 --> 00:22:22.830
how do you translate that,
given the absence of language?

00:22:22.830 --> 00:22:27.210
How do you translate that into
a conversation with the artist?

00:22:27.210 --> 00:22:30.300
And again, this could be about
writing or comedy or science

00:22:30.300 --> 00:22:31.680
or podcasting, for that matter.

00:22:31.680 --> 00:22:35.820
How do you say that,
keep going that way,

00:22:35.820 --> 00:22:38.490
when they might not even
recognize that they did it?

00:22:38.490 --> 00:22:40.602
And I'm guessing a lot
of times, they don't.

00:22:40.602 --> 00:22:41.310
RICK RUBIN: Yeah.

00:22:41.310 --> 00:22:42.060
Sometimes, they don't.

00:22:42.060 --> 00:22:42.660
It depends.

00:22:42.660 --> 00:22:45.460
When we're in the--

00:22:45.460 --> 00:22:49.300
I'll try to be in
a setting where,

00:22:49.300 --> 00:22:51.790
as we're talking
about it, we can

00:22:51.790 --> 00:22:53.900
engage with it in that moment.

00:22:53.900 --> 00:22:57.600
So it's not much good.

00:22:57.600 --> 00:22:59.350
Let's say I was producing
your new record,

00:22:59.350 --> 00:23:02.200
and you played me something, and
I had some thoughts about it.

00:23:02.200 --> 00:23:05.332
It wouldn't be so helpful for
me to tell you what those were.

00:23:05.332 --> 00:23:07.540
It'd be better for us to
wait till we were in a place

00:23:07.540 --> 00:23:11.420
where we could try things
and see where it goes.

00:23:11.420 --> 00:23:17.020
So the first thing is I wouldn't
rely on language to do it.

00:23:17.020 --> 00:23:22.880
It would be more of making a
suggestion of something that's

00:23:22.880 --> 00:23:23.510
actionable.

00:23:23.510 --> 00:23:25.760
We try it, and then
we have more data.

00:23:25.760 --> 00:23:27.770
And either we're moving
in a good direction,

00:23:27.770 --> 00:23:30.350
or we're moving away from--
we're moving towards it or away

00:23:30.350 --> 00:23:32.370
from it, and we never know.

00:23:32.370 --> 00:23:33.830
And so it's always
an experiment.

00:23:33.830 --> 00:23:37.460
And maybe a simple way to
talk about it would be like,

00:23:37.460 --> 00:23:40.670
if I gave you two
dishes of food and asked

00:23:40.670 --> 00:23:43.778
you to taste them and tell
me which one you like better.

00:23:43.778 --> 00:23:45.320
Usually, it's pretty
straightforward,

00:23:45.320 --> 00:23:47.930
when you have two choices,
which you like better,

00:23:47.930 --> 00:23:53.130
and I think most creativity
can be boiled down to that.

00:23:53.130 --> 00:23:56.150
That's very different
than I wonder

00:23:56.150 --> 00:23:59.990
how this is going to perform on
certain social media platforms?

00:23:59.990 --> 00:24:02.682
That's different
than what is it--

00:24:02.682 --> 00:24:04.890
when I'm tasting these two
things, which is the one I

00:24:04.890 --> 00:24:06.750
want to finish eating?

00:24:06.750 --> 00:24:09.918
And if I were to say,
I like this one better,

00:24:09.918 --> 00:24:12.460
but it needs a little salt, and
then put a little salt on it.

00:24:12.460 --> 00:24:15.000
It's like, maybe I
put too much salt,

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:16.360
and you know when you taste it.

00:24:16.360 --> 00:24:18.375
It's like it's that simple.

00:24:21.230 --> 00:24:25.970
Being in tune enough
with ourselves

00:24:25.970 --> 00:24:30.680
to really know how we feel
in the face of knowing

00:24:30.680 --> 00:24:32.870
that other people might
feel very differently,

00:24:32.870 --> 00:24:34.460
which is part of the challenge.

00:24:34.460 --> 00:24:40.780
It's like, if everyone
tells you A, A, A, A, A, A,

00:24:40.780 --> 00:24:48.630
and you listen, and you're
like that's B, as an artist,

00:24:48.630 --> 00:24:55.310
it's important to be able
to say, to me, it's B.

00:24:55.310 --> 00:24:59.450
And it's a disconnect,
because so much of,

00:24:59.450 --> 00:25:04.570
when we go to school, it's to
get us to follow the rules.

00:25:04.570 --> 00:25:09.070
And in art, it's different,
because the rules are there

00:25:09.070 --> 00:25:14.770
as a scaffolding to be
chipped away, as need be.

00:25:14.770 --> 00:25:15.910
Sometimes, they're helpful.

00:25:15.910 --> 00:25:18.520
Sometimes, they're not,
and sometimes, we'll

00:25:18.520 --> 00:25:23.270
even impose our own rules
to give something its shape.

00:25:23.270 --> 00:25:26.743
So we can decide to make a--

00:25:26.743 --> 00:25:28.660
we're going to make a
painting, but we're only

00:25:28.660 --> 00:25:31.900
going to use green and
red are the only colors

00:25:31.900 --> 00:25:33.250
we're allowed to use.

00:25:33.250 --> 00:25:36.040
We decide that in
advance, and then how

00:25:36.040 --> 00:25:40.060
do we solve the problem knowing
all we have is green and red?

00:25:40.060 --> 00:25:43.570
It can-- because
otherwise, if there's

00:25:43.570 --> 00:25:48.000
an infinite number of choices,
anything can be anything.

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:53.070
It's like, sometimes more
choices is not better.

00:25:53.070 --> 00:25:58.620
So limiting your palate
to something manageable

00:25:58.620 --> 00:26:03.030
forces you to solve
problems in a different way.

00:26:03.030 --> 00:26:06.990
Now, in our digital
age music-wise,

00:26:06.990 --> 00:26:12.620
you can make anything digitally.

00:26:12.620 --> 00:26:15.220
There's no-- there
was a time when,

00:26:15.220 --> 00:26:16.970
if you didn't have a
guitar in the studio,

00:26:16.970 --> 00:26:18.140
you couldn't record guitar.

00:26:18.140 --> 00:26:20.633
Or if you couldn't
hire an orchestra,

00:26:20.633 --> 00:26:22.550
there couldn't be orchestra
on your recording.

00:26:22.550 --> 00:26:25.200
Now, you can just call
any of those things up.

00:26:25.200 --> 00:26:31.460
So there's infinite choices,
and infinite choices

00:26:31.460 --> 00:26:37.820
don't necessarily lead to
better compositions or better

00:26:37.820 --> 00:26:39.750
final works.

00:26:39.750 --> 00:26:48.710
Understanding how you feel
in the face of other voices,

00:26:48.710 --> 00:26:50.720
without second
guessing yourself,

00:26:50.720 --> 00:26:56.560
is probably the single
most important thing

00:26:56.560 --> 00:26:58.870
to practice as an artist.

00:26:58.870 --> 00:27:03.490
Or a skill set to develop as
an artist is to I know how you

00:27:03.490 --> 00:27:05.860
feel, and own your feelings.

00:27:05.860 --> 00:27:08.860
And the key to
that is not I know,

00:27:08.860 --> 00:27:10.790
so I know what's right for you.

00:27:10.790 --> 00:27:11.990
It doesn't work that way.

00:27:11.990 --> 00:27:17.350
It's just I know for me, and the
reason I chose to be an artist

00:27:17.350 --> 00:27:21.720
is to demonstrate
this is how I see it.

00:27:21.720 --> 00:27:30.090
If I'm undermining my taste
for some commercial idea,

00:27:30.090 --> 00:27:32.910
it defeats the whole
purpose of doing this.

00:27:32.910 --> 00:27:35.370
That's not what this
process is about.

00:27:35.370 --> 00:27:41.550
This process is I'm doing me,
and I'm showing you who I am.

00:27:41.550 --> 00:27:44.070
And you can like it or
not, but either way, this

00:27:44.070 --> 00:27:46.660
is still how I see it.

00:27:46.660 --> 00:27:49.245
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I love
that, because in science,

00:27:49.245 --> 00:27:51.120
having trained graduate
students, having been

00:27:51.120 --> 00:27:53.670
a graduate student,
I was very blessed

00:27:53.670 --> 00:27:56.640
to have mentors, one of
who is a real iconoclast.

00:27:56.640 --> 00:27:58.410
He's dead now.

00:27:58.410 --> 00:28:00.600
Actually, all my
advisors are dead--

00:28:00.600 --> 00:28:01.830
suicide, cancer, cancer.

00:28:01.830 --> 00:28:03.747
The joke is you don't
want me to work for you.

00:28:03.747 --> 00:28:05.610
So they all had a
morbid sense of humor,

00:28:05.610 --> 00:28:08.230
so they're laughing about
this, someplace, right now.

00:28:08.230 --> 00:28:10.230
RICK RUBIN: I thought you
were going to say they

00:28:10.230 --> 00:28:11.438
all ate the poison mushrooms.

00:28:11.438 --> 00:28:15.180
ANDREW HUBERMAN: No, but
the last one said to me,

00:28:15.180 --> 00:28:17.790
you're the common
denominator, Andrew.

00:28:17.790 --> 00:28:19.560
And I thought, oh
my goodness, and he

00:28:19.560 --> 00:28:22.500
said, kind of just
kidding, but not really.

00:28:22.500 --> 00:28:24.180
So it's a little bit eerie.

00:28:24.180 --> 00:28:28.860
But in any case,
he always said--

00:28:28.860 --> 00:28:31.150
his name was Ben--
he always said,

00:28:31.150 --> 00:28:34.140
the one thing I
can't teach is taste,

00:28:34.140 --> 00:28:36.960
and the one predictor I
have of the people who

00:28:36.960 --> 00:28:41.250
will never develop it are the
ones who are perfectionists.

00:28:41.250 --> 00:28:43.950
Because they're
filtering their--

00:28:43.950 --> 00:28:46.140
perfectionists that
filter their perfection

00:28:46.140 --> 00:28:47.520
through the feedback of others.

00:28:47.520 --> 00:28:49.530
He was always looking
for the person

00:28:49.530 --> 00:28:53.100
that was putting up a little bit
of a middle finger to feedback.

00:28:53.100 --> 00:28:56.042
Not so much that they
would get things wrong,

00:28:56.042 --> 00:28:57.750
because it can be
badly wrong in science.

00:28:57.750 --> 00:28:59.375
You can be wrong for
the right reasons,

00:28:59.375 --> 00:29:01.740
but you can also be wrong
for the wrong reasons.

00:29:01.740 --> 00:29:06.060
But people that just had almost
a compulsion to do it their way

00:29:06.060 --> 00:29:09.390
or to believe in
what they were doing.

00:29:09.390 --> 00:29:12.480
And I'm hearing some
of that, or I'm hearing

00:29:12.480 --> 00:29:13.902
that in what you're describing.

00:29:13.902 --> 00:29:15.360
I also think that
there's something

00:29:15.360 --> 00:29:18.480
about the human empathic process
or the emotional process,

00:29:18.480 --> 00:29:21.030
where when we see
somebody doing something

00:29:21.030 --> 00:29:24.150
and they seem to really
not be paying attention

00:29:24.150 --> 00:29:26.580
to what anyone else is doing--

00:29:26.580 --> 00:29:29.160
I guess the crazy person on the
street is one version of it,

00:29:29.160 --> 00:29:31.035
where we go they're just
in their experience,

00:29:31.035 --> 00:29:31.890
and it's just crazy.

00:29:31.890 --> 00:29:35.550
But when somebody seems
to be enjoying themselves

00:29:35.550 --> 00:29:39.240
or the emotion
seems to be real, I

00:29:39.240 --> 00:29:42.000
think there are a good
fraction of people

00:29:42.000 --> 00:29:46.470
who feel a gravitational
pull, and they go, yeah, that.

00:29:46.470 --> 00:29:48.000
And the best example
I have of this

00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:50.340
is I remember growing up
in the skateboard thing

00:29:50.340 --> 00:29:53.790
we were the first-- we were the
first to start doing the baggy,

00:29:53.790 --> 00:29:56.640
sagging clothes thing, and
we got teased endlessly

00:29:56.640 --> 00:29:57.988
one year in school.

00:29:57.988 --> 00:30:00.030
Then, there was a bunch
of hip hop that came out,

00:30:00.030 --> 00:30:04.018
and guys were wearing sagging
their jeans or their shorts.

00:30:04.018 --> 00:30:06.060
Next year, we come back,
and the very same people

00:30:06.060 --> 00:30:08.250
who were making fun of
us we're all doing it,

00:30:08.250 --> 00:30:10.262
and that's when
it clicked for me.

00:30:10.262 --> 00:30:12.720
I was like, most people don't
actually know what they like.

00:30:12.720 --> 00:30:13.080
RICK RUBIN: No.

00:30:13.080 --> 00:30:14.580
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
They like what they

00:30:14.580 --> 00:30:18.490
like because of the certainty
of the people that they like.

00:30:18.490 --> 00:30:23.670
And so the question then
is, in this landscape

00:30:23.670 --> 00:30:27.280
of creative stuff, what's real?

00:30:27.280 --> 00:30:30.060
What's not real?

00:30:30.060 --> 00:30:31.530
It's almost like
whoever can create

00:30:31.530 --> 00:30:33.870
the most convincing
story at least

00:30:33.870 --> 00:30:35.950
captures a good number of--

00:30:35.950 --> 00:30:38.700
a good fraction of
audiences, but that's

00:30:38.700 --> 00:30:40.530
not what the creative
artist needs to do.

00:30:40.530 --> 00:30:42.155
They need to actually
depart from that.

00:30:42.155 --> 00:30:43.060
Do I have that right?

00:30:43.060 --> 00:30:45.185
RICK RUBIN: Well they're
just two different things.

00:30:45.185 --> 00:30:49.270
Like coming up with a story
with the purpose of pleasing

00:30:49.270 --> 00:30:52.150
someone else is a
skill set, but it's

00:30:52.150 --> 00:30:57.740
more of a commercial endeavor
than an artistic endeavor.

00:30:57.740 --> 00:30:59.240
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
It's like tactical.

00:30:59.240 --> 00:30:59.650
Yeah.

00:30:59.650 --> 00:31:00.100
RICK RUBIN: Yeah.

00:31:00.100 --> 00:31:00.880
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

00:31:00.880 --> 00:31:01.150
RICK RUBIN: Yeah.

00:31:01.150 --> 00:31:02.180
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I was
thinking, in your book,

00:31:02.180 --> 00:31:03.888
you describe, again,
when you're thinking

00:31:03.888 --> 00:31:07.210
about the creative
process as a cloud,

00:31:07.210 --> 00:31:10.240
for me, again, it serves
as such a powerful anchor.

00:31:10.240 --> 00:31:13.510
And then I think about the
biology, the neurobiology,

00:31:13.510 --> 00:31:17.800
of like strategy
formation or strategy

00:31:17.800 --> 00:31:22.810
implementation and then
almost by sheer luck,

00:31:22.810 --> 00:31:26.560
or miraculously, I turn a few
pages later into the book,

00:31:26.560 --> 00:31:29.500
and there's a description of
how animals that are trying

00:31:29.500 --> 00:31:32.470
to accomplish something--

00:31:32.470 --> 00:31:36.010
eat, mate, find
water, accomplish

00:31:36.010 --> 00:31:39.800
the requirements of living--

00:31:39.800 --> 00:31:41.990
it requires a
narrow visual focus.

00:31:41.990 --> 00:31:43.760
This is something my
lab is obsessed with,

00:31:43.760 --> 00:31:44.927
and I've been obsessed with.

00:31:44.927 --> 00:31:47.240
And in that more
narrow visual focus,

00:31:47.240 --> 00:31:51.140
we know that the playbook
becomes more narrow.

00:31:51.140 --> 00:31:54.230
The rule set is more narrow.

00:31:54.230 --> 00:31:57.230
Now, at some point,
in order to come up

00:31:57.230 --> 00:31:59.520
with a new creative
idea, that means

00:31:59.520 --> 00:32:02.630
that broadening vision is
essential, in some way,

00:32:02.630 --> 00:32:03.860
or broadening thinking.

00:32:03.860 --> 00:32:05.235
RICK RUBIN: Well,
it could either

00:32:05.235 --> 00:32:08.570
be a broadening or a narrowing,
but it's changing the aperture

00:32:08.570 --> 00:32:11.750
from the standard.

00:32:11.750 --> 00:32:16.820
The reason we do this
is to present something

00:32:16.820 --> 00:32:24.300
new that maybe you already knew
but didn't know you knew it.

00:32:24.300 --> 00:32:27.950
And for that to be the case,
you have to be looking at it.

00:32:27.950 --> 00:32:29.720
It's not unlike what
a comedian does.

00:32:29.720 --> 00:32:30.830
Comedian makes you laugh.

00:32:30.830 --> 00:32:33.260
Usually, what they're
saying, it's outrageous,

00:32:33.260 --> 00:32:35.510
but you know that it's right.

00:32:35.510 --> 00:32:37.640
Just no one says it
that way, or no one

00:32:37.640 --> 00:32:39.230
has said it that way before.

00:32:39.230 --> 00:32:44.280
But it's always the truth
in it that makes it funny.

00:32:44.280 --> 00:32:45.300
It's like that.

00:32:45.300 --> 00:32:50.100
It's the same idea as
recognizing something

00:32:50.100 --> 00:32:55.690
that seems really
obvious, once you see it,

00:32:55.690 --> 00:32:58.870
but it seems like nobody else
sees it, or no one else points

00:32:58.870 --> 00:32:59.420
it out.

00:32:59.420 --> 00:33:01.150
And I feel like science
is like that too,

00:33:01.150 --> 00:33:07.930
because how much of science,
once the light flashes

00:33:07.930 --> 00:33:13.210
over your head, it's like I
got it, it just seems like,

00:33:13.210 --> 00:33:14.690
well, we knew that forever.

00:33:14.690 --> 00:33:16.690
No one knew it, but do
you know what I'm saying?

00:33:16.690 --> 00:33:17.785
It's like it's so obvious.

00:33:17.785 --> 00:33:18.680
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Absolutely.

00:33:18.680 --> 00:33:19.847
RICK RUBIN: It's so obvious.

00:33:19.847 --> 00:33:22.750
And I think another
superpower of artists

00:33:22.750 --> 00:33:26.260
is this accepting we
don't know anything.

00:33:26.260 --> 00:33:30.310
When we think we know things,
that also limits our world.

00:33:30.310 --> 00:33:33.820
We think we know
it's only like this.

00:33:33.820 --> 00:33:39.220
This is all that's possible,
where nice in this little box.

00:33:39.220 --> 00:33:43.570
But in reality, who's
to say that's the case?

00:33:43.570 --> 00:33:45.790
Who's to say any
of the-- we could

00:33:45.790 --> 00:33:49.420
take all of what we
believe in science now

00:33:49.420 --> 00:33:52.400
and decide to throw all of that
away and start from scratch.

00:33:52.400 --> 00:33:54.992
And we'd probably create
a whole different one.

