00:35:04.150 --> 00:35:08.320
In an offline
conversation one time,

00:35:08.320 --> 00:35:10.810
you asked a good
friend of mine, who's

00:35:10.810 --> 00:35:12.940
been a guest on this
podcast, Eddie Chang, who's

00:35:12.940 --> 00:35:15.850
chair of neurosurgery,
and I would place him

00:35:15.850 --> 00:35:19.510
in the top 1% of
neuroscientists.

00:35:19.510 --> 00:35:21.122
He's pulling speech
out of people

00:35:21.122 --> 00:35:23.330
who are completely paralyzed
with locked-in syndrome,

00:35:23.330 --> 00:35:23.830
et cetera.

00:35:23.830 --> 00:35:27.700
And you asked him, what
percentage of what's

00:35:27.700 --> 00:35:30.022
contained in medical
textbooks and training--

00:35:30.022 --> 00:35:30.730
RICK RUBIN: Today

00:35:30.730 --> 00:35:31.360
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Today.

00:35:31.360 --> 00:35:32.068
RICK RUBIN: Yeah.

00:35:32.068 --> 00:35:33.970
If you went to
medical school today,

00:35:33.970 --> 00:35:36.160
and you learned what was
in the textbook, what

00:35:36.160 --> 00:35:38.290
percentage of that
information is accurate,

00:35:38.290 --> 00:35:39.740
and what percentage is not?

00:35:39.740 --> 00:35:41.657
And he said, maybe half.

00:35:41.657 --> 00:35:43.240
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Right, and you asked,

00:35:43.240 --> 00:35:45.020
and what is the
consequence of that?

00:35:45.020 --> 00:35:50.058
And he said, incalculable,
and I completely agree.

00:35:50.058 --> 00:35:51.850
And I asked him a second
time, and he still

00:35:51.850 --> 00:35:53.058
came up with the same answer.

00:35:53.058 --> 00:35:54.460
So that's a good sign.

00:35:54.460 --> 00:35:57.140
Reliability from experiment
to the next is good.

00:35:57.140 --> 00:35:57.640
Yeah.

00:35:57.640 --> 00:36:03.880
I think that there is this idea
that we really know things.

00:36:03.880 --> 00:36:07.990
In science, we've observed
amazing discoveries

00:36:07.990 --> 00:36:08.920
from chance.

00:36:08.920 --> 00:36:12.190
We've observed
amazing discoveries

00:36:12.190 --> 00:36:15.052
from incredible
bouts of hard work.

00:36:15.052 --> 00:36:16.510
In both cases,
people were spending

00:36:16.510 --> 00:36:18.370
a lot of time in the lab.

00:36:18.370 --> 00:36:21.550
Like no one walked into the
lab, saw something one day,

00:36:21.550 --> 00:36:24.070
and had a Nobel Prize
winning discovery

00:36:24.070 --> 00:36:25.840
or fundamental discovery.

00:36:25.840 --> 00:36:27.790
They were all hanging
out in lab a lot.

00:36:27.790 --> 00:36:30.760
Just some of them
came up with something

00:36:30.760 --> 00:36:31.990
that they didn't expect.

00:36:31.990 --> 00:36:34.090
Others were drilling
toward an answer.

00:36:34.090 --> 00:36:35.768
RICK RUBIN: And in
all those cases,

00:36:35.768 --> 00:36:37.060
when the breakthroughs happen--

00:36:37.060 --> 00:36:37.750
I'm guessing.

00:36:37.750 --> 00:36:38.740
I don't know this--

00:36:38.740 --> 00:36:48.690
that considering we
assume this information,

00:36:48.690 --> 00:36:52.860
then this discovery is
true, based on everything

00:36:52.860 --> 00:36:54.180
that came before it.

00:36:54.180 --> 00:36:57.090
But if everything that
came before it is wrong,

00:36:57.090 --> 00:36:59.973
then the discoveries are
probably built on a--

00:36:59.973 --> 00:37:01.140
do you know what I'm saying?

00:37:01.140 --> 00:37:04.920
It's like the context,
everything that happens

00:37:04.920 --> 00:37:09.330
takes into account that the
context that it's sitting in it

00:37:09.330 --> 00:37:11.340
fits in that context.

00:37:11.340 --> 00:37:12.720
Maybe that context isn't right.

00:37:12.720 --> 00:37:13.420
Who knows?

00:37:13.420 --> 00:37:14.160
We don't know.

00:37:14.160 --> 00:37:19.770
So I'm saying, we're too close
to most things in thinking,

00:37:19.770 --> 00:37:24.010
when we think we things,
where there are a lot

00:37:24.010 --> 00:37:27.280
of assumptions that go into it.

00:37:27.280 --> 00:37:29.980
And that any new
discoveries are essentially

00:37:29.980 --> 00:37:36.100
built on top of these
beliefs, but they're beliefs.

00:37:36.100 --> 00:37:38.410
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I remember--

00:37:38.410 --> 00:37:42.190
of course, I listened to
the Beastie Boys growing up.

00:37:42.190 --> 00:37:42.710
Who didn't?

00:37:42.710 --> 00:37:46.180
I was a child of the 90s,
and they were in the--

00:37:46.180 --> 00:37:49.600
Sabotage was an outgrowth
of a skateboarding movie,

00:37:49.600 --> 00:37:51.970
like Spike Jones and
like the girl movies.

00:37:51.970 --> 00:37:54.760
And those worlds, the Beastie
Boys and skateboarding

00:37:54.760 --> 00:37:56.783
were really closely
interwoven for a while.

00:37:56.783 --> 00:37:57.700
Some people know that.

00:37:57.700 --> 00:37:58.450
Some people don't.

00:37:58.450 --> 00:38:00.430
And Spike formed the
bridge, and then Spike

00:38:00.430 --> 00:38:04.270
went off and started
making more bigger movies

00:38:04.270 --> 00:38:06.140
that more people watch.

00:38:06.140 --> 00:38:08.170
But let's just use
them as an example.

00:38:08.170 --> 00:38:12.040
I heard you say once before that
you guys were joking around,

00:38:12.040 --> 00:38:14.958
like Beastie Boys, like
these guys doing hip hop,

00:38:14.958 --> 00:38:16.750
but it was kind of like
the hardcore scene,

00:38:16.750 --> 00:38:19.750
in New York, punk rock
scene, and it was a joke.

00:38:19.750 --> 00:38:21.490
There were a lot
of inside jokes.

00:38:21.490 --> 00:38:26.950
When you were working
together, was there the thought

00:38:26.950 --> 00:38:29.890
that people might love
it, might hate it,

00:38:29.890 --> 00:38:31.810
or you just weren't
paying attention at all?

00:38:31.810 --> 00:38:33.643
RICK RUBIN: Weren't
paying attention at all.

00:38:33.643 --> 00:38:35.100
Never considered it.

00:38:35.100 --> 00:38:37.290
There were no-- at
that point in time,

00:38:37.290 --> 00:38:40.620
when we were making
Licensed to Ill,

00:38:40.620 --> 00:38:44.980
hip hop music was a
tiny underground thing.

00:38:44.980 --> 00:38:49.890
And no one making
hip hop at that time

00:38:49.890 --> 00:38:52.080
thought it would
ever mean anything.

00:38:52.080 --> 00:38:54.660
It was not a realistic thought.

00:38:54.660 --> 00:38:58.080
So we were making it really
for our crazy friends,

00:38:58.080 --> 00:38:59.288
and that's it.

00:38:59.288 --> 00:39:01.080
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So do
you think, nowadays,

00:39:01.080 --> 00:39:05.100
the fact that one can create
something and, quote, unquote,

00:39:05.100 --> 00:39:05.970
release it quickly.

00:39:05.970 --> 00:39:10.890
I can put something out onto
Twitter or Instagram now.

00:39:10.890 --> 00:39:12.870
We could do it in
10 seconds from now,

00:39:12.870 --> 00:39:15.518
and I will get immediate
feedback, which

00:39:15.518 --> 00:39:17.310
is external feedback,
of course, but then I

00:39:17.310 --> 00:39:19.260
can iterate on the
basis of that feedback.

00:39:19.260 --> 00:39:23.160
Do you think that's
problematic for the larger

00:39:23.160 --> 00:39:24.520
opportunity for creativity?

00:39:24.520 --> 00:39:29.730
In other words, if we were to go
back 20 years or even 15 years,

00:39:29.730 --> 00:39:32.880
when the opportunity to create
was certainly still there,

00:39:32.880 --> 00:39:37.200
but you really didn't know how
it was going to land until you,

00:39:37.200 --> 00:39:39.242
quote, unquote, released it.

00:39:39.242 --> 00:39:40.950
It seems to me there
was more opportunity

00:39:40.950 --> 00:39:44.790
to stay in that
magical rainforest that

00:39:44.790 --> 00:39:46.713
is the creativity itself.

00:39:46.713 --> 00:39:48.630
RICK RUBIN: I don't think
it's wrong or right.

00:39:48.630 --> 00:39:52.740
It's just more information that
you can use or not use and use

00:39:52.740 --> 00:39:53.940
it in a useful way.

00:39:53.940 --> 00:39:57.082
And you can make
something and put it out,

00:39:57.082 --> 00:39:58.290
and people could not like it.

00:39:58.290 --> 00:40:00.165
And you're like, oh,
they still don't get it.

00:40:00.165 --> 00:40:01.590
I'm going to I got to go harder.

00:40:01.590 --> 00:40:05.103
Like I got to go harder
in that direction not--

00:40:05.103 --> 00:40:06.270
do you know what I'm saying?

00:40:06.270 --> 00:40:10.680
It's like not to react
away from information.

00:40:10.680 --> 00:40:11.770
It can be helpful.

00:40:11.770 --> 00:40:12.945
It can be helpful when--

00:40:15.740 --> 00:40:18.350
there could be different stories
that happen at the same time.

00:40:18.350 --> 00:40:21.800
Where you're making something,
and you have an idea of what

00:40:21.800 --> 00:40:22.800
it is.

00:40:22.800 --> 00:40:25.850
And then other people
engage with it,

00:40:25.850 --> 00:40:27.975
and they have a different
idea of what it is.

00:40:27.975 --> 00:40:29.600
And they like it for
a different reason

00:40:29.600 --> 00:40:32.060
than you did or
dislike it for a reason

00:40:32.060 --> 00:40:33.860
different than the
reason you like it.

00:40:33.860 --> 00:40:37.250
We can't control
any of those things.

00:40:37.250 --> 00:40:41.450
The only part of it
that we can control

00:40:41.450 --> 00:40:44.550
is how we relate to
the thing that we make.

00:40:44.550 --> 00:40:49.050
And any external
information that

00:40:49.050 --> 00:40:53.700
undermines the clarity
of that connection

00:40:53.700 --> 00:40:56.580
is probably bad for
the art is my guess.

00:40:59.090 --> 00:41:01.730
And again, I'm only saying
this from my experience.

00:41:01.730 --> 00:41:03.680
I try to make things--

00:41:03.680 --> 00:41:07.850
all I've ever tried to
make was something I like

00:41:07.850 --> 00:41:10.940
or something that I felt
like was missing as a fan

00:41:10.940 --> 00:41:14.720
that I wanted, and
nobody was making it.

00:41:14.720 --> 00:41:19.350
So I'll make it, but it wasn't--

00:41:19.350 --> 00:41:23.310
it was always in the service
of I love this thing.

00:41:23.310 --> 00:41:24.750
I want something like this.

00:41:24.750 --> 00:41:26.070
No one else is making one.

00:41:26.070 --> 00:41:27.333
I have to make one.

00:41:27.333 --> 00:41:28.250
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

00:41:28.250 --> 00:41:32.540
It's beautiful, because the
word that keeps coming to mind

00:41:32.540 --> 00:41:35.180
is it's almost
like a compulsion.

00:41:35.180 --> 00:41:38.090
Like there are other
options of ways

00:41:38.090 --> 00:41:41.340
to be and to behave and to
function and work in life.

00:41:41.340 --> 00:41:43.410
But if something
is a compulsion,

00:41:43.410 --> 00:41:46.580
it yanks us away from
those other opportunities,

00:41:46.580 --> 00:41:48.650
just enough that we
have to get back to it.

00:41:48.650 --> 00:41:50.840
You've talked before
about, and you

00:41:50.840 --> 00:41:54.730
talk in the book, this
notion of the source.

00:41:54.730 --> 00:41:59.440
And to me, again, I can't help
but put my neuroscientist lens

00:41:59.440 --> 00:42:00.340
on this.

00:42:00.340 --> 00:42:06.310
I think of the source as not
one brain area but some function

00:42:06.310 --> 00:42:09.220
within the brain,
where we are in touch

00:42:09.220 --> 00:42:11.720
with our bodily signals.

00:42:11.720 --> 00:42:13.480
Like what feels
right, what doesn't?

00:42:13.480 --> 00:42:17.560
Or like tasting the two
foods, I love that example.

00:42:17.560 --> 00:42:22.930
And that it's a playbook
that is far more

00:42:22.930 --> 00:42:26.098
vast than the short
term adaptive playbook,

00:42:26.098 --> 00:42:28.390
like this how I'm going to
get from point A to point B.

00:42:28.390 --> 00:42:32.270
And yet, when I listen
to an album or a song,

00:42:32.270 --> 00:42:35.350
I have to assume that
there, at some point,

00:42:35.350 --> 00:42:40.700
it becomes not strategy
development or creativity

00:42:40.700 --> 00:42:42.320
but strategy implementation.

00:42:42.320 --> 00:42:44.210
Like there needs
to be the songs are

00:42:44.210 --> 00:42:46.010
going to come in
this order-- and I

00:42:46.010 --> 00:42:47.180
don't know much about music.

00:42:47.180 --> 00:42:51.380
My musician friends
are always laughing.

00:42:51.380 --> 00:42:54.892
RICK RUBIN: It's not
so much about music.

00:42:54.892 --> 00:42:55.850
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Right.

00:42:55.850 --> 00:42:59.750
Well put, but the
ordering of the sequence

00:42:59.750 --> 00:43:01.160
of the melodies, et cetera.

00:43:01.160 --> 00:43:06.320
So at what point
does one decide, OK,

00:43:06.320 --> 00:43:08.240
like now's the time
to get into that more

00:43:08.240 --> 00:43:11.330
narrow focus of effort?

00:43:11.330 --> 00:43:12.920
Like we've got it.

00:43:12.920 --> 00:43:13.813
Let's run with this.

00:43:13.813 --> 00:43:15.980
Because there is a component
of the creative process

00:43:15.980 --> 00:43:17.630
that involves packaging
and finishing.

00:43:17.630 --> 00:43:21.470
And is that part less
satisfying to you,

00:43:21.470 --> 00:43:23.387
or is it just all part
of the same larger arc?

00:43:23.387 --> 00:43:24.970
RICK RUBIN: It's all
part of the same.

00:43:24.970 --> 00:43:25.880
It's nice.

00:43:25.880 --> 00:43:26.840
There's a good feeling.

00:43:26.840 --> 00:43:29.360
There's usually a good feeling
when something is done.

00:43:29.360 --> 00:43:34.390
On the one hand,
it's a commitment,

00:43:34.390 --> 00:43:36.880
because up until the time
that you say it's done,

00:43:36.880 --> 00:43:39.650
you can keep experimenting
and changing it.

00:43:39.650 --> 00:43:42.850
If you think, well, maybe
tomorrow I can make it better,

00:43:42.850 --> 00:43:43.870
then it's not finished.

00:43:43.870 --> 00:43:45.790
And you keep thinking
that for a long time,

00:43:45.790 --> 00:43:49.790
you can do that forever
and never put out anything.

00:43:49.790 --> 00:43:53.830
So getting to the point where
you're ready to sign off

00:43:53.830 --> 00:43:57.550
is a good feeling,
and it allows you--

00:43:57.550 --> 00:44:00.760
one of the things I talk about
in the book is, because it

00:44:00.760 --> 00:44:03.290
is a difficult thing to do,
because it's fun to play,

00:44:03.290 --> 00:44:06.340
and it's fun to maybe it's
not the best it could be yet.

00:44:08.944 --> 00:44:12.440
To use whatever
the next project is

00:44:12.440 --> 00:44:17.180
going to be as motivation
to finish the one

00:44:17.180 --> 00:44:18.770
you're working on now.

00:44:18.770 --> 00:44:21.260
Like I'm working on this.

00:44:21.260 --> 00:44:23.990
I'm spending all of
my time on this thing.

00:44:23.990 --> 00:44:25.300
It's really good.

00:44:25.300 --> 00:44:28.450
I believe it can be better,
but there's this other thing

00:44:28.450 --> 00:44:30.260
that I really want to make.

00:44:30.260 --> 00:44:32.860
And if I keep tinkering
with this one,

00:44:32.860 --> 00:44:34.720
I'll never get to
make the other one.

00:44:34.720 --> 00:44:39.700
So using other
projects as an impetus

00:44:39.700 --> 00:44:42.340
to finish something and
release it into the world

00:44:42.340 --> 00:44:43.090
is a good one.

00:44:43.090 --> 00:44:49.670
And you said your description of
source is something within us.

00:44:49.670 --> 00:44:54.430
I don't know if I would
say that was accurate.

00:44:54.430 --> 00:45:00.010
It's definitely in us too,
but it's not only in us.

00:45:00.010 --> 00:45:06.280
And it's I think of
source as the organizing

00:45:06.280 --> 00:45:13.220
principle of everything, and
it's how everything exists.

00:45:13.220 --> 00:45:17.690
How the trees grow, and
why there are mountains.

00:45:17.690 --> 00:45:21.140
And anything that we can
see in the outside world,

00:45:21.140 --> 00:45:25.490
and every discovery
and every piece of art

00:45:25.490 --> 00:45:31.370
and every new design
and every machine

00:45:31.370 --> 00:45:35.180
are all outgrowths of
this source energy.

00:45:35.180 --> 00:45:39.140
Our part of it is the
antenna that connects to it,

00:45:39.140 --> 00:45:46.750
and maybe where the
vehicle for source

00:45:46.750 --> 00:45:50.903
to allow things to
happen in the world.

00:45:50.903 --> 00:45:52.570
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
thank you for that,

00:45:52.570 --> 00:45:55.330
because I did indeed misspeak.

00:45:55.330 --> 00:45:57.490
Because I recall very
distinctly in the book,

00:45:57.490 --> 00:45:59.500
you described how
the physical world

00:45:59.500 --> 00:46:02.920
is constrained by the laws of
physics and certain things.

00:46:02.920 --> 00:46:05.170
The imagination
is unconstrained.

00:46:05.170 --> 00:46:07.150
And I think I have
this right, that you

00:46:07.150 --> 00:46:08.950
said the work set
somewhere between those,

00:46:08.950 --> 00:46:10.930
it's neither of
one nor the other.

00:46:10.930 --> 00:46:14.740
That ultimately what
feeds into all of that

00:46:14.740 --> 00:46:17.290
are imagination and
the way, indeed,

00:46:17.290 --> 00:46:20.170
that our brain is
a physical entity.

00:46:20.170 --> 00:46:24.050
The nature in the
outside world provides

00:46:24.050 --> 00:46:25.880
at least what appears
to be near infinite

00:46:25.880 --> 00:46:27.230
if not infinite options.

00:46:27.230 --> 00:46:29.840
And I love the example
of the color palette.

00:46:29.840 --> 00:46:31.790
That if we restrict
me to whatever

00:46:31.790 --> 00:46:36.200
sorts of paints or medium I
have, then it's restricted.

00:46:36.200 --> 00:46:38.690
But in nature, there's
an infinite number

00:46:38.690 --> 00:46:41.450
of shades and tones
and combinations.

00:46:41.450 --> 00:46:44.300
RICK RUBIN: And even on
one, if you pick up a rock

00:46:44.300 --> 00:46:46.340
and look at the
color of the rock

00:46:46.340 --> 00:46:48.800
and tried to find a
paint to match that rock,

00:46:48.800 --> 00:46:50.090
it would never match.

00:46:50.090 --> 00:46:51.920
There's too much.

00:46:51.920 --> 00:46:56.360
There are too many
variations in nature

00:46:56.360 --> 00:47:02.390
within a single color
rock for us to get close.

00:47:02.390 --> 00:47:05.060
There's too much information.

00:47:05.060 --> 00:47:06.650
We scratch the surface.

00:47:06.650 --> 00:47:08.570
We're only scratching
the surface.

00:47:08.570 --> 00:47:10.010
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And we love when

00:47:10.010 --> 00:47:13.670
we are able to peer in
at different scales,

00:47:13.670 --> 00:47:17.307
spatial scales, time scales too,
but spatial scales, the delight

00:47:17.307 --> 00:47:19.640
that comes from that that
these nature pictures-- seemed

00:47:19.640 --> 00:47:20.780
like there were more
of these in the '80s,

00:47:20.780 --> 00:47:22.880
like where you'd see
a drop of oil shot

00:47:22.880 --> 00:47:24.810
at very high resolution.

00:47:24.810 --> 00:47:26.630
There's beauty in a drop of oil.

00:47:26.630 --> 00:47:28.820
And then you'd see the
Earth and the galaxy,

00:47:28.820 --> 00:47:31.670
there's beauty in that
too, these extremes.

00:47:31.670 --> 00:47:33.980
And of course, our
daily perception

00:47:33.980 --> 00:47:37.460
is mostly through the filter
of these kinds of interactions,

00:47:37.460 --> 00:47:39.830
walls, and sometimes outdoors.

00:47:39.830 --> 00:47:42.710
There's a brilliant
neuroscientist,

00:47:42.710 --> 00:47:45.170
and not surprisingly,
he has a Nobel.

00:47:45.170 --> 00:47:46.250
His name is Richard Axel.

00:47:46.250 --> 00:47:48.680
He's at Columbia University.

00:47:48.680 --> 00:47:51.770
He's outrageous personality.

00:47:51.770 --> 00:47:54.298
Chews Nicorette nonstop.

00:47:54.298 --> 00:47:56.840
You guys would get along great,
not because of the Nicorette,

00:47:56.840 --> 00:47:59.510
but because his
perspective on things

00:47:59.510 --> 00:48:04.430
is very abstract for
a guy who's solved--

00:48:04.430 --> 00:48:08.030
he won the Nobel for
solving a great problem

00:48:08.030 --> 00:48:12.920
within how we smell,
perception of odors and tastes.

00:48:12.920 --> 00:48:15.620
And he says that everything
that the brain does

00:48:15.620 --> 00:48:16.828
is an abstraction.

00:48:16.828 --> 00:48:18.620
Like I could take a
photograph of your face

00:48:18.620 --> 00:48:20.330
and show it to you, and
you'd say, yeah, that's me.

00:48:20.330 --> 00:48:23.120
Or let's say for the moment, I
call myself an abstract artist.

00:48:23.120 --> 00:48:25.078
Let's just play a game,
because I've never been

00:48:25.078 --> 00:48:26.390
accused of being an artist.

00:48:26.390 --> 00:48:28.820
And I do three dots
and a squiggly line,

00:48:28.820 --> 00:48:29.900
and I say that's you.

00:48:29.900 --> 00:48:31.670
And you say, well, that doesn't
look like me, and I say,

00:48:31.670 --> 00:48:33.290
but that's my
abstraction of you.

00:48:33.290 --> 00:48:33.620
RICK RUBIN: Yeah.

00:48:33.620 --> 00:48:34.453
ANDREW HUBERMAN: OK.

00:48:34.453 --> 00:48:37.100
Well, the brain essentially
does that, because

00:48:37.100 --> 00:48:38.510
or something in between that.

00:48:38.510 --> 00:48:41.180
Because there's no actual
photograph of you in my brain.

00:48:41.180 --> 00:48:42.800
It's just a bunch
of neurons playing

00:48:42.800 --> 00:48:45.890
what we call an ensemble, like
a different keys on a piano.

00:48:45.890 --> 00:48:49.520
And we go, Rick, I
recognize you, Rick Rubin.

00:48:49.520 --> 00:48:52.800
And so everything
is an abstraction,

00:48:52.800 --> 00:48:55.230
and it's only once we start
tinkering with the parts--

00:48:55.230 --> 00:48:56.688
and this is the
essence of science,

00:48:56.688 --> 00:48:58.220
to remove and add
and manipulate.

00:48:58.220 --> 00:49:02.090
And the best example I can
come up with would be Rothko.

00:49:02.090 --> 00:49:03.828
And I only we come
up with this example

00:49:03.828 --> 00:49:05.870
because I started off in
vision science and maybe

00:49:05.870 --> 00:49:07.620
this will make the
most sense to everyone,

00:49:07.620 --> 00:49:09.810
except the folks who've
been blind since birth,

00:49:09.810 --> 00:49:11.420
and they can swap
something in here.

00:49:11.420 --> 00:49:15.710
That if I show you a Rothko, and
I don't tell you it's a Rothko,

00:49:15.710 --> 00:49:18.080
you may or may not actually
think it's that impressive.

00:49:18.080 --> 00:49:19.430
It depends on your taste in art.

00:49:19.430 --> 00:49:22.610
But what Rothko did,
which was amazing,

00:49:22.610 --> 00:49:24.650
even if you don't like Rothkos--

00:49:24.650 --> 00:49:26.030
and I happened to--

00:49:26.030 --> 00:49:31.100
is that he removed all the
white and high contrast-y stuff.

00:49:31.100 --> 00:49:34.170
And when you do that
you alter color space,

00:49:34.170 --> 00:49:36.810
and so the colors
look very different.

00:49:36.810 --> 00:49:38.720
Some people saw that
dress a few years ago,

00:49:38.720 --> 00:49:41.360
is it orange, or is
a gold or whatever?

00:49:41.360 --> 00:49:44.150
That was a little bit
of the same phenomenon.

00:49:44.150 --> 00:49:46.970
I doubt-- in fact, I'd be
willing to bet my left arm

00:49:46.970 --> 00:49:50.600
that Rothko knew nothing about
the neuroscience of color

00:49:50.600 --> 00:49:51.500
perception.

00:49:51.500 --> 00:49:53.480
But somehow got to
this place where,

00:49:53.480 --> 00:49:56.690
if there was no canvas
showing and no high contrast

00:49:56.690 --> 00:49:58.610
and the paintings
were large enough

00:49:58.610 --> 00:50:01.220
and on the appropriate
wall, you saw them

00:50:01.220 --> 00:50:06.200
a certain way that tapped
into something fundamental.

00:50:06.200 --> 00:50:09.050
And this is where I think art
and science really converge,

00:50:09.050 --> 00:50:12.740
is that, every once in a
while, we see something that

00:50:12.740 --> 00:50:15.710
feels amazing to
enough people-- and not

00:50:15.710 --> 00:50:17.630
just like the baggy
pants phenomenon,

00:50:17.630 --> 00:50:20.220
not just because other
people think it's cool.

00:50:20.220 --> 00:50:23.630
But there's something there,
and again this defies language.

00:50:23.630 --> 00:50:29.600
And I have to imagine that in
your years of life and music

00:50:29.600 --> 00:50:34.840
and other creative endeavors,
that every once in a while,

00:50:34.840 --> 00:50:36.680
have you ever
encountered something

00:50:36.680 --> 00:50:38.500
where something
fundamental keeps

00:50:38.500 --> 00:50:40.390
showing up in different form?

00:50:40.390 --> 00:50:45.280
Or there's something like almost
like a rule or a principle,

00:50:45.280 --> 00:50:46.600
does that ever come about?

00:50:46.600 --> 00:50:48.100
Because in science,
we think of this

00:50:48.100 --> 00:50:51.370
as like this is reveals
something about our limitation

00:50:51.370 --> 00:50:53.620
to abstract the world.

00:50:53.620 --> 00:50:55.480
I hope I made that clear.

00:50:55.480 --> 00:50:58.660
RICK RUBIN: Not, exactly
but I have a thought.

00:50:58.660 --> 00:51:02.890
You talked earlier
about the drop

00:51:02.890 --> 00:51:04.810
of oil, the photograph
of the drop of oil

00:51:04.810 --> 00:51:08.650
and the photograph-- or we
could use, on the other side,

00:51:08.650 --> 00:51:12.100
like Hubble telescope
images of these vast things

00:51:12.100 --> 00:51:14.670
in high definition.

00:51:14.670 --> 00:51:18.450
What we see every day is as
impressive as those things,

00:51:18.450 --> 00:51:22.630
but where numb to them, because
we see them all the time.

00:51:22.630 --> 00:51:25.560
And if we were to look
at drops of oil every day

00:51:25.560 --> 00:51:29.880
in a microscope,
a month from now,

00:51:29.880 --> 00:51:32.850
we would not find
wonder in that image.

00:51:32.850 --> 00:51:41.570
So sometimes, it's the
novelty of not seeing it

00:51:41.570 --> 00:51:46.370
from that perspective before
that's really thrilling.

00:51:46.370 --> 00:51:48.200
And I could imagine,
and this probably

00:51:48.200 --> 00:51:52.670
relates to the Rothko idea,
that you could see something

00:51:52.670 --> 00:51:56.970
from a particular angle and
have this magical experience.

00:51:56.970 --> 00:51:59.397
And then walk three
feet to the side

00:51:59.397 --> 00:52:01.730
and see it from a different
way, and it just evaporates.

00:52:01.730 --> 00:52:09.050
It only works-- it only triggers
this thing in us when we

00:52:09.050 --> 00:52:12.960
look at it just the right way.

00:52:12.960 --> 00:52:16.310
There was an experiment
I just heard about,

00:52:16.310 --> 00:52:19.310
heard about the other day,
that sounds fascinating,

00:52:19.310 --> 00:52:22.730
that a painting
teacher recommended.

00:52:22.730 --> 00:52:26.510
Where instead of painting--

00:52:26.510 --> 00:52:28.310
having a model in
the room and painting

00:52:28.310 --> 00:52:32.870
the model, that you have
the model in the next room.

00:52:32.870 --> 00:52:35.390
And you go into the next
room without your equipment.

00:52:35.390 --> 00:52:38.270
You don't have your equipment,
and you can study the model

00:52:38.270 --> 00:52:40.910
for as long as you want.

00:52:40.910 --> 00:52:42.880
And then you go into
a different room,

00:52:42.880 --> 00:52:44.800
where you can't see
the model, and paint

00:52:44.800 --> 00:52:47.590
the model, instead of--

00:52:47.590 --> 00:52:51.550
and it changes
your relationship.

00:52:51.550 --> 00:52:54.910
We're not just
painting the lines.

00:52:54.910 --> 00:53:00.410
We're painting what
is interesting enough

00:53:00.410 --> 00:53:02.030
about what I saw--

00:53:02.030 --> 00:53:06.240
what are the data points
that stuck in my mind?

00:53:06.240 --> 00:53:09.650
And when I string those
together, what do I get?

00:53:09.650 --> 00:53:12.380
And what do I--

00:53:12.380 --> 00:53:16.220
how do I form it to get
as close to whatever

00:53:16.220 --> 00:53:20.710
the experience of
that person was, which

00:53:20.710 --> 00:53:24.700
the closest of getting
to the experience

00:53:24.700 --> 00:53:28.320
of that person in the
painting might not

00:53:28.320 --> 00:53:30.810
look like a photograph.

00:53:30.810 --> 00:53:38.620
It might look more
different then more the same

00:53:38.620 --> 00:53:43.320
to really see what you see.

00:53:43.320 --> 00:53:45.690
If we think about the
Picasso paintings that

00:53:45.690 --> 00:53:48.120
were inspired by African
art, where the eyes are

00:53:48.120 --> 00:53:54.727
on different levels, they
may give us more information

00:53:54.727 --> 00:53:56.060
than a photograph would give us.

00:53:59.200 --> 00:54:04.140
I'm thinking about when
you were describing

00:54:04.140 --> 00:54:07.440
the sensation of when something
takes your breath away,

00:54:07.440 --> 00:54:11.610
and we all have that when
we see a dramatic sunset.

00:54:11.610 --> 00:54:14.190
Anyone you know, when there's
a really dramatic sunset,

00:54:14.190 --> 00:54:18.120
or if there's a whale, and
if anyone's on the beach,

00:54:18.120 --> 00:54:21.090
and there's a whale,
everybody's really interested

00:54:21.090 --> 00:54:23.193
that there's a whale.

00:54:23.193 --> 00:54:24.360
Do you know what I'm saying?

00:54:24.360 --> 00:54:28.600
These feelings of wonder,
we get to experience them,

00:54:28.600 --> 00:54:30.690
depending on where we are.

00:54:30.690 --> 00:54:34.950
Or a dragonfly or a bird
flies into your space,

00:54:34.950 --> 00:54:36.340
these things happen.

00:54:36.340 --> 00:54:40.170
And when they happen,
it's like we're

00:54:40.170 --> 00:54:44.430
confronted with the
mystery of the world, when

00:54:44.430 --> 00:54:45.780
we change the perspective.

00:54:45.780 --> 00:54:48.810
Normally, we don't think of
whales in our backyard or birds

00:54:48.810 --> 00:54:52.500
in our house, flying
freely, but they

00:54:52.500 --> 00:54:54.510
do happen. these
things do happen,

00:54:54.510 --> 00:54:58.740
and they like break
us out of our trance

00:54:58.740 --> 00:55:00.600
when these things happen.

00:55:00.600 --> 00:55:04.500
It's like, oh, yeah, there are
birds like this everywhere.

00:55:04.500 --> 00:55:06.090
I'm just not paying attention.

00:55:06.090 --> 00:55:08.610
This guy is coming in to
tap me on the shoulder.

00:55:08.610 --> 00:55:09.540
It's like remember me?

00:55:09.540 --> 00:55:11.490
Here I am.

00:55:11.490 --> 00:55:14.130
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So I would
say that the whale example

00:55:14.130 --> 00:55:18.670
and what you're describing
is it's revealing to us how--

00:55:18.670 --> 00:55:21.070
in a delightful
way-- how deficient

00:55:21.070 --> 00:55:22.923
our perceptual
filters normally are.

00:55:22.923 --> 00:55:23.590
RICK RUBIN: Yes.

00:55:23.590 --> 00:55:25.007
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
It's a little bit

00:55:25.007 --> 00:55:26.507
like the Rothko
is revealing how--

00:55:26.507 --> 00:55:28.840
I've never thought about it
this way until this moment--

00:55:28.840 --> 00:55:31.540
is revealing to us how
color normally looks

00:55:31.540 --> 00:55:35.410
is actually, first of all,
not the only way it looks.

00:55:35.410 --> 00:55:37.897
Those colors we think are
one way, but all of color--

00:55:37.897 --> 00:55:39.730
this gets into the
biology of color vision--

00:55:39.730 --> 00:55:40.930
is all about contrast.

00:55:40.930 --> 00:55:43.660
What something is next to
dictates what it looks like,

00:55:43.660 --> 00:55:45.730
and that's the origin
of that dress meme

00:55:45.730 --> 00:55:47.230
or whatever you call it.

00:55:47.230 --> 00:55:49.270
I still can't figure out
exactly what a meme is.

00:55:49.270 --> 00:55:52.448
Someone will eventually tell me.

00:55:52.448 --> 00:55:56.280
In the same way, when you see
a whale, and it's delightful,

00:55:56.280 --> 00:55:58.410
I think it's revealing
to us the extent to which

00:55:58.410 --> 00:56:00.990
those whales are--
the ocean is vast.

00:56:00.990 --> 00:56:04.380
There's a whole universe
there, and we are blind to it

00:56:04.380 --> 00:56:05.020
all the time.

00:56:05.020 --> 00:56:08.295
And I think the misperception--
or the misconception,

00:56:08.295 --> 00:56:10.170
excuse me, is that we're
delighted because we

00:56:10.170 --> 00:56:10.830
see the whale.

00:56:10.830 --> 00:56:13.080
We might be just as
delighted because we're

00:56:13.080 --> 00:56:15.630
getting hit with the
contrast of how little

00:56:15.630 --> 00:56:18.790
we recognize all the
time, and in that way,

00:56:18.790 --> 00:56:21.480
it reminds me a little
bit about comedy.

00:56:21.480 --> 00:56:25.020
And I've been watching
more comedy lately,

00:56:25.020 --> 00:56:28.200
and sometimes, it's the shock.

00:56:28.200 --> 00:56:32.190
Sometimes, it's the absolute
truth that's revealed,

00:56:32.190 --> 00:56:35.970
and then other times,
what I've noticed--

00:56:35.970 --> 00:56:39.360
and I saw Rogen do comedy
at the Vulcan Club,

00:56:39.360 --> 00:56:42.420
in Austin, which he does
every once in a while.

00:56:42.420 --> 00:56:47.960
And it was small club, and
he was leading out this story

00:56:47.960 --> 00:56:50.340
during his routine
or bit, I think.

00:56:50.340 --> 00:56:50.840
Right?

00:56:50.840 --> 00:56:54.330
This bit, and everyone
knew where it was going.

00:56:54.330 --> 00:56:59.620
We all knew, and then
when he finally told us,

00:56:59.620 --> 00:57:02.100
it was exactly where we
thought it was going,

00:57:02.100 --> 00:57:03.212
and it was hilarious.

00:57:03.212 --> 00:57:04.420
RICK RUBIN: And it felt good.

00:57:04.420 --> 00:57:06.197
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And it felt amazing,

00:57:06.197 --> 00:57:08.530
and I thought in that moment,
I was like, wait a second,

00:57:08.530 --> 00:57:09.920
how did he pull that off?

00:57:09.920 --> 00:57:12.340
That was masterful, because
normally, it's this thing

00:57:12.340 --> 00:57:13.528
like you create one story.

00:57:13.528 --> 00:57:16.070
There's like a scripting out,
almost like a courtroom lawyer.

00:57:16.070 --> 00:57:17.230
And then they kind
of pull the curtain,

00:57:17.230 --> 00:57:18.760
and it's something different.

00:57:18.760 --> 00:57:22.060
And if you look at the science,
the neuroscience, and brain

00:57:22.060 --> 00:57:24.670
imaging of laughter
and humor, which

00:57:24.670 --> 00:57:27.130
I've looked into, to be
honest, and no disrespect

00:57:27.130 --> 00:57:29.500
to the people in that
field, it's pretty lame.

00:57:29.500 --> 00:57:33.520
It's lame, because
it's always the jarring

00:57:33.520 --> 00:57:35.420
nature of a surprise.

00:57:35.420 --> 00:57:38.410
But what he led us to was
something that, oh, no,

00:57:38.410 --> 00:57:39.633
he's actually going there.

00:57:39.633 --> 00:57:41.050
Oh, wait, he's
really going there,

00:57:41.050 --> 00:57:44.920
and it was this anticipation
with a beautiful delivery

00:57:44.920 --> 00:57:45.860
at the end.

00:57:45.860 --> 00:57:49.600
And so I'm convinced
that, based on what

00:57:49.600 --> 00:57:52.360
we're talking about here, that
there's something about when we

00:57:52.360 --> 00:57:54.280
see something, we
think it's about that,

00:57:54.280 --> 00:57:56.590
but that the
delight that we feel

00:57:56.590 --> 00:58:00.340
could be about the other
experiences that now become,

00:58:00.340 --> 00:58:03.940
in a subconscious way, like ha.

00:58:03.940 --> 00:58:07.090
It's almost like laughing
at this perceptual deficit

00:58:07.090 --> 00:58:07.660
that we have.

00:58:07.660 --> 00:58:11.470
It's almost like laughing at
how little we actually know,

00:58:11.470 --> 00:58:13.000
which is what you've said.

00:58:13.000 --> 00:58:14.002
RICK RUBIN: Yeah.

00:58:14.002 --> 00:58:14.710
It could be that.

00:58:14.710 --> 00:58:18.310
It also could be the
sense of community

00:58:18.310 --> 00:58:23.000
of when you think it's going
to go a particular way,

00:58:23.000 --> 00:58:23.990
and it goes that way.

00:58:23.990 --> 00:58:28.451
It's like reinforcement of you.

00:58:28.451 --> 00:58:33.620
It's like, yeah, he's
saying it, but in a way,

00:58:33.620 --> 00:58:34.850
we're saying it together.

00:58:34.850 --> 00:58:38.240
I'm listening, he's saying it,
but we're in this together,

00:58:38.240 --> 00:58:40.295
and that's a good feeling.

00:58:40.295 --> 00:58:42.670
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I have to
think about that for a second.

00:58:42.670 --> 00:58:47.760
I was trying to think about why
certain music still can evoke

00:58:47.760 --> 00:58:50.550
such powerful emotions in me.

00:58:50.550 --> 00:58:52.350
And there does seem to
be something special

00:58:52.350 --> 00:58:54.930
about the music we listen
to when we are teenagers.

00:58:54.930 --> 00:58:59.960
From about 14 until
about 25, it seems

00:58:59.960 --> 00:59:02.240
to get routed into our
nervous system in some way.

00:59:02.240 --> 00:59:04.340
Maybe because that
phase of our life

00:59:04.340 --> 00:59:06.960
is really one of
identity crisis.

00:59:06.960 --> 00:59:10.160
You don't find too many
40-year-olds, some,

00:59:10.160 --> 00:59:11.762
who are wondering
like who they are.

00:59:11.762 --> 00:59:12.470
RICK RUBIN: Yeah.

00:59:12.470 --> 00:59:15.053
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Occasionally,
but almost every young teenager

00:59:15.053 --> 00:59:17.180
or pre-teen and
is like who am I?

00:59:17.180 --> 00:59:18.740
You're defining personality.

00:59:18.740 --> 00:59:20.210
So I always likened it to that.

00:59:20.210 --> 00:59:23.930
But leaving out the
critical period biology

00:59:23.930 --> 00:59:27.227
stuff, what do you think
it is about the music

00:59:27.227 --> 00:59:28.310
that we hear at that time?

00:59:28.310 --> 00:59:30.290
Are that much more
emotionally tuned?

00:59:30.290 --> 00:59:33.170
Have we not shut down our
sensors quite as much?

00:59:33.170 --> 00:59:35.023
Because the songs
and the artists

00:59:35.023 --> 00:59:37.190
don't matter, because they're
very individual to me.

00:59:37.190 --> 00:59:39.770
For other people, it will
be the Beatles or something.

00:59:39.770 --> 00:59:42.020
Now, I just really wish the
Beatles did it for me too.

00:59:42.020 --> 00:59:44.390
But do you think
that's important?

00:59:44.390 --> 00:59:46.700
Because I could see how
it's really terrific.

00:59:46.700 --> 00:59:48.710
I could also see
how it sets up one

00:59:48.710 --> 00:59:50.720
of these what I'll
just use nerdy language

00:59:50.720 --> 00:59:54.410
and call like a
semi-deprived filter.

00:59:54.410 --> 00:59:56.210
Because if I'm only
looking for the way

00:59:56.210 --> 00:59:58.370
that a Stiff Little
Fingers track made

00:59:58.370 --> 01:00:00.800
me feel the first time I
listened to it, when I was 15,

01:00:00.800 --> 01:00:02.420
the feeling is worthwhile.

01:00:02.420 --> 01:00:05.390
But if I'm looking for that,
I'm missing all the other stuff.

01:00:05.390 --> 01:00:06.410
I'm missing the Beatles.

01:00:06.410 --> 01:00:08.990
I'm missing Fleetwood Mac, which
never did it for me either.

01:00:08.990 --> 01:00:12.350
I'm like, I'm missing all
this stuff that people I love

01:00:12.350 --> 01:00:14.780
and respect really love.

01:00:14.780 --> 01:00:17.720
So I've never worried
about it, because there's

01:00:17.720 --> 01:00:22.760
an infinite treasure trove of
other things that I do love.

01:00:22.760 --> 01:00:24.530
But I do sometimes
wonder whether or not

01:00:24.530 --> 01:00:27.110
my life experience
is diminished,

01:00:27.110 --> 01:00:31.340
because I'm not allowing range.

01:00:31.340 --> 01:00:35.750
And you've, obviously,
worked in a huge number

01:00:35.750 --> 01:00:37.520
of different genres of music.

01:00:37.520 --> 01:00:38.270
Punk is one thing.

01:00:38.270 --> 01:00:39.770
Hip hop is--

01:00:39.770 --> 01:00:40.680
Neil Diamond too.

01:00:40.680 --> 01:00:41.180
Right?

01:00:41.180 --> 01:00:44.690
Eminem too, Slayer too, right?

01:00:44.690 --> 01:00:46.860
And in some sense,
as I list these off,

01:00:46.860 --> 01:00:49.520
just think about how much in
high school, maybe nowadays

01:00:49.520 --> 01:00:53.270
less so, but even in
college and as an adult,

01:00:53.270 --> 01:00:56.810
societally, were asked
to constrain ourselves

01:00:56.810 --> 01:00:58.790
to one of these groups.

01:00:58.790 --> 01:01:02.120
I didn't know it was
OK to love Bob Dylan

01:01:02.120 --> 01:01:04.160
and love punk rock
as much as I do,

01:01:04.160 --> 01:01:06.327
until I heard Tim Armstrong
said he loved Bob Dylan.

01:01:06.327 --> 01:01:07.035
RICK RUBIN: Yeah.

01:01:07.035 --> 01:01:08.420
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And I was like--

01:01:08.420 --> 01:01:10.700
and recently, he told me
he loves the Grateful Dead,

01:01:10.700 --> 01:01:12.230
and I was like, whoa.

01:01:12.230 --> 01:01:14.890
But I remember when
you had to pick.

01:01:14.890 --> 01:01:17.390
RICK RUBIN: Both the Ramones
and the Clash love the Beatles,

01:01:17.390 --> 01:01:18.110
so we can--

01:01:18.110 --> 01:01:18.943
ANDREW HUBERMAN: OK.

01:01:18.943 --> 01:01:19.885
I got work to do.

01:01:19.885 --> 01:01:21.260
RICK RUBIN: We'll
do it together.

01:01:21.260 --> 01:01:22.635
ANDREW HUBERMAN: OK.

01:01:22.635 --> 01:01:24.260
RICK RUBIN: I have
a feeling part of it

01:01:24.260 --> 01:01:28.420
is, the reason it gets
in at that age, is it's

01:01:28.420 --> 01:01:32.660
a time when we're
defining who we are,

01:01:32.660 --> 01:01:34.760
and the music is part
of the definition

01:01:34.760 --> 01:01:37.400
of how we see ourselves.

01:01:37.400 --> 01:01:41.390
Like the music that we hear
before that might be the music

01:01:41.390 --> 01:01:43.880
that's on the radio or our
parents' music or our older

01:01:43.880 --> 01:01:45.470
brother or sister's music.

01:01:45.470 --> 01:01:48.230
And then when you're
14 or 15, and you

01:01:48.230 --> 01:01:50.270
start choosing what
you're listening to,

01:01:50.270 --> 01:01:52.400
it's like, now
it's finally mine.

01:01:52.400 --> 01:01:54.110
And my parents
might not like it,

01:01:54.110 --> 01:01:56.700
and my older brothers and
sisters may or may not like it,

01:01:56.700 --> 01:01:58.580
but this one is mine.

01:01:58.580 --> 01:02:07.630
And it always has
that impression in us

01:02:07.630 --> 01:02:10.780
that this is ours.

01:02:10.780 --> 01:02:16.825
My that's my thought of
why it continues to last.

01:02:16.825 --> 01:02:19.163
ANDREW HUBERMAN: How do you
wipe the slate clean then?

01:02:19.163 --> 01:02:21.830
So for instance, if you're going
to go in and work with somebody

01:02:21.830 --> 01:02:23.630
new-- and again, as
people are hearing this,

01:02:23.630 --> 01:02:25.130
I hope that they're
transplanting this

01:02:25.130 --> 01:02:26.443
to whatever it is that they do.

01:02:26.443 --> 01:02:28.360
Because in the realm of
science and podcasting

01:02:28.360 --> 01:02:29.990
and communication,
it's not music,

01:02:29.990 --> 01:02:33.710
but there's a contour and a way.

01:02:33.710 --> 01:02:36.170
Hopefully, this podcast
will look nothing

01:02:36.170 --> 01:02:37.770
like it does in five years.

01:02:37.770 --> 01:02:40.310
That's my hope, is it will
still have the core features

01:02:40.310 --> 01:02:42.830
of the beauty and utility
of biology coming through,

01:02:42.830 --> 01:02:47.065
but I hope it doesn't look
anything like episode two.

01:02:47.065 --> 01:02:49.190
RICK RUBIN: And I think
it'll evolve as you evolve.

01:02:49.190 --> 01:02:54.050
It's just, the truer it
is to what interests you,

01:02:54.050 --> 01:02:56.330
and if you're not
interested in biology

01:02:56.330 --> 01:02:59.240
in the same way in five years,
I would hope it's not the same.

01:02:59.240 --> 01:03:01.250
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'll be doing
psychoanalysis in real time

01:03:01.250 --> 01:03:01.670
here.

01:03:01.670 --> 01:03:03.600
In therapy, we'll all be
lying down on couches.

01:03:03.600 --> 01:03:04.190
RICK RUBIN: Whatever it is.

01:03:04.190 --> 01:03:04.790
Whatever it is.

01:03:04.790 --> 01:03:05.210
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

01:03:05.210 --> 01:03:06.752
We probably won't
be on psychedelics,

01:03:06.752 --> 01:03:07.910
but we might be levitating.

01:03:07.910 --> 01:03:09.980
You never know.

01:03:09.980 --> 01:03:13.160
I'd like to take a brief
break and thank our sponsor

