01:04:09.380 --> 01:04:10.670
of InsideTracker's plans.

01:04:10.670 --> 01:04:13.280
That's
insidetracker.com/huberman

01:04:13.280 --> 01:04:14.670
to get 20% off.

01:04:14.670 --> 01:04:16.920
So how do you--

01:04:16.920 --> 01:04:18.770
let's talk a little
bit, if you would,

01:04:18.770 --> 01:04:23.600
because I know I'm very
interested in your process.

01:04:23.600 --> 01:04:26.420
I'll spare you the
daily routine question.

01:04:26.420 --> 01:04:27.390
It's very cliche.

01:04:27.390 --> 01:04:32.940
But you and I are both lovers of
sunlight, of horizons, and not

01:04:32.940 --> 01:04:38.910
as a trivial source, as
an amazing gift of energy.

01:04:38.910 --> 01:04:39.840
Right?

01:04:39.840 --> 01:04:44.100
And there aren't
words for it really.

01:04:44.100 --> 01:04:48.630
Aside from your
daily routines, when

01:04:48.630 --> 01:04:52.770
it comes to somebody you're
going from project to project,

01:04:52.770 --> 01:04:55.440
and you know you're going to
be doing work with somebody,

01:04:55.440 --> 01:04:56.460
could be your own work.

01:04:56.460 --> 01:04:58.620
And we'll talk about
the writing of this book

01:04:58.620 --> 01:05:00.535
and its structure
which is very unique.

01:05:00.535 --> 01:05:02.910
I've never encountered a book
with this kind of structure

01:05:02.910 --> 01:05:06.270
before, and it's the
most facile read ever,

01:05:06.270 --> 01:05:10.510
and yet every single page,
I underlined, took notes,

01:05:10.510 --> 01:05:11.010
starred.

01:05:11.010 --> 01:05:14.520
And like, as you notice, it's
very worn, very, very worn

01:05:14.520 --> 01:05:17.920
already and only
more so over time.

01:05:17.920 --> 01:05:23.160
Do you have a
process for removing

01:05:23.160 --> 01:05:26.400
the functions of the day and
what you were doing last week

01:05:26.400 --> 01:05:33.450
and what's going on in order
to get more access to this--

01:05:33.450 --> 01:05:35.970
I'm going to think of it
now more as a receiver

01:05:35.970 --> 01:05:36.610
inside of you.

01:05:36.610 --> 01:05:37.110
Right?

01:05:37.110 --> 01:05:40.020
Almost like tuning a radio,
[IMITATING STATIC] and then

01:05:40.020 --> 01:05:40.650
it comes in.

01:05:40.650 --> 01:05:42.810
Like the beginning of like
a Strummer Clash thing.

01:05:42.810 --> 01:05:43.310
Right?

01:05:43.310 --> 01:05:44.085
He love the radio.

01:05:44.085 --> 01:05:45.210
Joe loved the radio, right?

01:05:45.210 --> 01:05:47.430
[IMITATING STATIC] and
then it comes in clear,

01:05:47.430 --> 01:05:48.330
and there it is.

01:05:48.330 --> 01:05:51.770
How do you clear the static?

01:05:51.770 --> 01:05:54.060
What are some of the
operational steps

01:05:54.060 --> 01:05:59.550
that you think might be more
generalizable, regardless

01:05:59.550 --> 01:06:03.930
of where somebody in Africa
is listening to this.

01:06:03.930 --> 01:06:07.180
RICK RUBIN: I would say, when I
engage in a particular project,

01:06:07.180 --> 01:06:13.860
whatever it is, I
dedicate all of myself

01:06:13.860 --> 01:06:15.730
for that period of
time, whatever it is.

01:06:15.730 --> 01:06:18.660
Whether it be 20
minutes or whether it

01:06:18.660 --> 01:06:23.720
be five hours,
whatever it is, total

01:06:23.720 --> 01:06:30.580
focus, and no outside
distraction whatsoever.

01:06:30.580 --> 01:06:34.830
And when I leave that
process, I do my best

01:06:34.830 --> 01:06:36.720
not to think about it,
when I'm away from it.

01:06:36.720 --> 01:06:38.970
I don't bring any
materials with me.

01:06:38.970 --> 01:06:41.970
I don't leave the studio
with works in progress

01:06:41.970 --> 01:06:44.250
and spend time listening
to them during the day

01:06:44.250 --> 01:06:45.930
or looking for ideas.

01:06:45.930 --> 01:06:50.910
I stay as far away from
it, when I'm not directly

01:06:50.910 --> 01:06:53.070
engaging in it, as possible.

01:06:53.070 --> 01:06:58.230
And in the best of situations,
I have something else to totally

01:06:58.230 --> 01:07:00.580
engage myself in in between.

01:07:00.580 --> 01:07:04.530
So instead of working on
project A for five hours,

01:07:04.530 --> 01:07:06.690
and then leaving
and doing nothing,

01:07:06.690 --> 01:07:09.720
I'm hoping to engage
in a Project B

01:07:09.720 --> 01:07:12.870
or B, C, and D
with all of myself

01:07:12.870 --> 01:07:15.630
before going back to
project A again, which might

01:07:15.630 --> 01:07:17.430
be the next day, let's say.

01:07:17.430 --> 01:07:21.150
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So this
relates to an amazing chapter

01:07:21.150 --> 01:07:22.650
and series of
writings in your book,

01:07:22.650 --> 01:07:25.230
that I'm not going to describe,
because I want people to find

01:07:25.230 --> 01:07:27.000
it for themselves,
about disengaging,

01:07:27.000 --> 01:07:29.040
about disengaging
from the process.

01:07:29.040 --> 01:07:31.330
One question I had as
I read that chapter,

01:07:31.330 --> 01:07:33.750
and as you're saying this
now, is even though you're

01:07:33.750 --> 01:07:37.500
disengaged, do you believe that
your subconscious is working it

01:07:37.500 --> 01:07:38.568
through?

01:07:38.568 --> 01:07:39.610
RICK RUBIN: I believe so.

01:07:39.610 --> 01:07:42.540
I believe so, and
I think in general,

01:07:42.540 --> 01:07:46.870
to stew over a problem is not
the way to solve a problem.

01:07:46.870 --> 01:07:53.150
I think to hold the
problems lightly--

01:07:53.150 --> 01:07:56.780
and when I say a problem,
when we're starting a project,

01:07:56.780 --> 01:07:58.475
there's usually
this feeling of--

01:08:00.962 --> 01:08:03.420
there's a question mark, at
the beginning of every project.

01:08:03.420 --> 01:08:06.960
I'm always anxious when
I start a new project,

01:08:06.960 --> 01:08:09.010
because I have no idea
what's going to happen.

01:08:09.010 --> 01:08:09.870
I never know.

01:08:09.870 --> 01:08:18.210
I never-- I may have, in some
cases, a potential backup plan,

01:08:18.210 --> 01:08:25.170
if nothing works, but I really
try not even to have that.

01:08:25.170 --> 01:08:26.910
I prefer not to have that.

01:08:26.910 --> 01:08:30.420
I prefer to go in maybe
to calm myself down enough

01:08:30.420 --> 01:08:32.774
to be able to show up,
there'll be an idea of,

01:08:32.774 --> 01:08:38.260
if nothing works, maybe we
could try something like this.

01:08:38.260 --> 01:08:41.040
But that would only
be for my own anxiety.

01:08:41.040 --> 01:08:44.790
It wouldn't be for
actual practical use.

01:08:44.790 --> 01:08:48.070
But there's always a sense
of anxiety, because I know,

01:08:48.070 --> 01:08:50.765
whatever's going to happen is
completely out of my control.

01:08:54.189 --> 01:08:57.859
Something either interesting
or not will appear,

01:08:57.859 --> 01:09:01.439
and then we're going to
follow that wherever it goes.

01:09:01.439 --> 01:09:09.850
And until something
appears for us to follow,

01:09:09.850 --> 01:09:14.040
I have a lot of anxiety, even
though it has never not come.

01:09:14.040 --> 01:09:17.069
It has come every time, but
there's something about it--

01:09:17.069 --> 01:09:20.370
because I also feel like there
might be expectation on me

01:09:20.370 --> 01:09:22.470
that I'm going to
make it happen,

01:09:22.470 --> 01:09:23.889
and I know that's not happening.

01:09:23.889 --> 01:09:24.889
That's not how it works.

01:09:29.760 --> 01:09:35.910
I show up ready for
it to happen and am

01:09:35.910 --> 01:09:40.649
open to whatever we have to
do to find that first thread.

01:09:40.649 --> 01:09:43.290
And once we find the
thread, then it's like, OK,

01:09:43.290 --> 01:09:46.139
we have a-- and that thread
may lead us to anything.

01:09:46.139 --> 01:09:49.410
It could lead us in a
million different directions.

01:09:49.410 --> 01:09:54.360
But something about having
that glimmer, that we're not

01:09:54.360 --> 01:09:55.980
looking at a blank page.

01:09:55.980 --> 01:10:05.500
We're looking at, OK, we have
the beginnings of, I would say,

01:10:05.500 --> 01:10:06.740
a map.

01:10:06.740 --> 01:10:09.110
But it's a map that we don't
know where it takes us,

01:10:09.110 --> 01:10:10.430
and it's just the beginning.

01:10:10.430 --> 01:10:16.177
It's just like, you are here.

01:10:16.177 --> 01:10:18.010
If you have a map, and
it says you are here,

01:10:18.010 --> 01:10:20.830
even if you can't
see the directions,

01:10:20.830 --> 01:10:22.750
knowing where we are feels OK.

01:10:25.970 --> 01:10:28.700
And once we get--
and usually, again,

01:10:28.700 --> 01:10:32.990
usually in the first day, first
couple of days, it happens.

01:10:36.350 --> 01:10:42.260
But up until then, it's really
an anxiety-producing situation,

01:10:42.260 --> 01:10:44.490
and then I can't remember
the original question.

01:10:44.490 --> 01:10:47.780
So that was the beginning of
something completely different.

01:10:47.780 --> 01:10:49.322
But do you remember
what you asked?

01:10:49.322 --> 01:10:50.030
I don't remember.

01:10:50.030 --> 01:10:50.270
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

01:10:50.270 --> 01:10:52.610
Well, we were talking
about disengaging,

01:10:52.610 --> 01:10:54.520
and is your
subconscious into it?

01:10:54.520 --> 01:10:58.490
And then we're talking
about, I love this.

01:10:58.490 --> 01:11:02.630
So like what is your process
of wading into this thing?

01:11:02.630 --> 01:11:04.250
And you're revealing that now.

01:11:04.250 --> 01:11:07.730
I think of anxiety as readiness.

01:11:07.730 --> 01:11:10.970
I think about the characteristic
features of anxiety,

01:11:10.970 --> 01:11:13.340
it tends to be a bit
of a constriction

01:11:13.340 --> 01:11:16.052
of the visual field into
more of a narrow vision.

01:11:16.052 --> 01:11:17.510
But that's appropriate,
because you

01:11:17.510 --> 01:11:20.280
want to shed what's
going on elsewhere.

01:11:20.280 --> 01:11:23.990
And then even when people
talk about the shakes or this

01:11:23.990 --> 01:11:26.360
like not feeling
OK sitting still,

01:11:26.360 --> 01:11:31.100
anxiety was designed to mobilize
us and not always to run away.

01:11:31.100 --> 01:11:33.950
Rarely do I talk about the
work in my own laboratory,

01:11:33.950 --> 01:11:35.212
but one of the things that--

01:11:35.212 --> 01:11:37.670
frankly, I didn't discover but
it was done in my laboratory

01:11:37.670 --> 01:11:39.440
but is brilliant.

01:11:39.440 --> 01:11:41.810
Graduate student Lindsey
Salay, who's now at Caltech,

01:11:41.810 --> 01:11:46.250
was that we can often
observe animals or humans

01:11:46.250 --> 01:11:49.730
in very high states of
anxiety, as they move forward

01:11:49.730 --> 01:11:50.600
toward a goal.

01:11:50.600 --> 01:11:53.090
And we always think of moving
forward as like this calm

01:11:53.090 --> 01:11:57.920
thing, these heroes, Rosa Parks
telling people like F you.

01:11:57.920 --> 01:11:59.810
Like I'm not getting
off the back.

01:11:59.810 --> 01:12:01.070
I'm not leaving the--

01:12:01.070 --> 01:12:03.665
giving up my seat on
the bus or Muhammad Ali.

01:12:03.665 --> 01:12:06.950
I bet you they were
experiencing tremendous anxiety,

01:12:06.950 --> 01:12:09.080
but it was in the forward tilt.

01:12:09.080 --> 01:12:14.150
And so I think anxiety
is at least comfortable

01:12:14.150 --> 01:12:17.060
when we are forcing
ourselves to stand still.

01:12:17.060 --> 01:12:20.450
So it's a activating
energy, and that

01:12:20.450 --> 01:12:22.730
brings up a word
that I have written

01:12:22.730 --> 01:12:25.130
in my notebook as an
extraction of a lot of themes

01:12:25.130 --> 01:12:27.672
from within the book that you
and I have talked about before,

01:12:27.672 --> 01:12:28.910
which is--

01:12:28.910 --> 01:12:31.940
and here I'm going to
sound very West Coast woo.

01:12:31.940 --> 01:12:36.230
But I mean it as
seriously as it can

01:12:36.230 --> 01:12:42.940
be stated, that I feel like
everything is energetic.

01:12:42.940 --> 01:12:44.770
We can do things from
a place of anger.

01:12:44.770 --> 01:12:47.500
We can do things
from a place of joy.

01:12:47.500 --> 01:12:51.370
We can do things from
a place of delight.

01:12:51.370 --> 01:12:53.140
I like to think
maturing into the idea

01:12:53.140 --> 01:12:57.130
that joy and delight and love
is the ultimate reservoir

01:12:57.130 --> 01:12:58.060
of energy.

01:12:58.060 --> 01:13:01.545
But a lot of the music that I
like from when I was younger

01:13:01.545 --> 01:13:02.920
was because of
the anger that was

01:13:02.920 --> 01:13:05.940
thread into it or the sadness.

01:13:05.940 --> 01:13:08.440
RICK RUBIN: If you think of
your relationship to that music,

01:13:08.440 --> 01:13:10.720
it's a relationship of love.

01:13:10.720 --> 01:13:12.490
You didn't listen to
that to get angry.

01:13:12.490 --> 01:13:12.790
ANDREW HUBERMAN: No.

01:13:12.790 --> 01:13:14.957
RICK RUBIN: You listened
to it because you loved it.

01:13:14.957 --> 01:13:17.140
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
I felt loved by it,

01:13:17.140 --> 01:13:19.390
because it matched
where I was at the time.

01:13:19.390 --> 01:13:22.120
RICK RUBIN: It was true to who
you were and where you were.

01:13:22.120 --> 01:13:23.912
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I know
that collaboration,

01:13:23.912 --> 01:13:25.870
there's a wonderful
chapter on collaboration,

01:13:25.870 --> 01:13:28.000
but it's collaboration,
as you mentioned before,

01:13:28.000 --> 01:13:30.500
with the universe,
not with others.

01:13:30.500 --> 01:13:33.640
But in terms of the, especially
the kind of work that you've

01:13:33.640 --> 01:13:38.620
done and do, when it comes
to working with artists,

01:13:38.620 --> 01:13:39.640
I do wonder--

01:13:39.640 --> 01:13:43.048
and here I'm not looking
for any gossip or stories.

01:13:43.048 --> 01:13:44.590
I've never been
interested in gossip.

01:13:44.590 --> 01:13:46.960
I love stories, but I'm
not interested in gossip.

01:13:46.960 --> 01:13:51.780
But once you see that
thread dangling there,

01:13:51.780 --> 01:13:53.720
and you're going
to go after this.

01:13:53.720 --> 01:13:55.470
Or you grab on to it,
and you're like, OK,

01:13:55.470 --> 01:13:56.887
now you have a
little bit of a map

01:13:56.887 --> 01:13:59.430
and an orientation
within that map.

01:13:59.430 --> 01:14:02.805
I often wonder, scientists
are complicated people.

01:14:02.805 --> 01:14:04.230
People think
they're very boring,

01:14:04.230 --> 01:14:05.813
but they're actually
very complicated.

01:14:05.813 --> 01:14:07.860
Because they're
often living in one

01:14:07.860 --> 01:14:09.810
limited rule set of
the prefrontal cortex.

01:14:09.810 --> 01:14:11.370
That's how you get
good at getting

01:14:11.370 --> 01:14:14.610
degrees is by understanding
the rules of academia

01:14:14.610 --> 01:14:15.930
and playing by those rules.

01:14:15.930 --> 01:14:17.070
RICK RUBIN: Yes.

01:14:17.070 --> 01:14:18.390
ANDREW HUBERMAN: People
tinker with the rules.

01:14:18.390 --> 01:14:19.807
You get your Richard
Axels who are

01:14:19.807 --> 01:14:21.780
very playful in how
they go about it,

01:14:21.780 --> 01:14:23.280
but they are systematic.

01:14:23.280 --> 01:14:25.470
He's known for
rigor, rigor, rigor.

01:14:25.470 --> 01:14:27.690
Right?

01:14:27.690 --> 01:14:30.090
When I think of creative
artists and musical artists,

01:14:30.090 --> 01:14:33.172
I think of a bit
more zany or loose.

01:14:33.172 --> 01:14:35.130
Or you watch the documentary
about the Ramones,

01:14:35.130 --> 01:14:37.170
and you're like, wow,
there's all this chaos.

01:14:37.170 --> 01:14:40.620
How-- because so many of
the brilliant artists,

01:14:40.620 --> 01:14:42.750
musical artists
that are out there,

01:14:42.750 --> 01:14:45.000
seem to have some
chaos inside them,

01:14:45.000 --> 01:14:48.150
or their lives aren't always
structured, oftentimes.

01:14:48.150 --> 01:14:49.650
And science too,
by the way, there

01:14:49.650 --> 01:14:52.740
are substance abuse issues
and personal life issues.

01:14:52.740 --> 01:14:56.490
How-- since you don't
have 100% control,

01:14:56.490 --> 01:14:59.730
they need to play the
instruments, sing, et cetera.

01:14:59.730 --> 01:15:05.530
How do you work with
people who have it in them

01:15:05.530 --> 01:15:07.470
but are getting
in their own way?

01:15:07.470 --> 01:15:10.180
Right?

01:15:10.180 --> 01:15:13.750
And do you think that
the internal chaos

01:15:13.750 --> 01:15:15.280
that a lot of
artists seem to have,

01:15:15.280 --> 01:15:19.210
do you think that
sometimes is actually

01:15:19.210 --> 01:15:22.150
an essential piece of
the creativity picture,

01:15:22.150 --> 01:15:23.400
that you can't disentangle it?

01:15:23.400 --> 01:15:24.108
RICK RUBIN: Yeah.

01:15:24.108 --> 01:15:26.740
I don't think it's an essential
piece in general, but certain

01:15:26.740 --> 01:15:29.590
artists, that's how they do it.

01:15:32.310 --> 01:15:36.740
I would say, I rarely get to
see the chaotic part of artists.

01:15:36.740 --> 01:15:39.500
For whatever reason, they
rarely show it to me,

01:15:39.500 --> 01:15:43.370
and most of them, like
most comedians I know,

01:15:43.370 --> 01:15:45.260
are much more serious
about what they're

01:15:45.260 --> 01:15:49.610
doing than what it looks like
from if you see them on stage.

01:15:49.610 --> 01:15:51.530
There's much more
to it, and there's

01:15:51.530 --> 01:15:58.250
much more focus on craft
going on and digging deep

01:15:58.250 --> 01:16:02.780
than would necessarily be
obvious seeing them jump around

01:16:02.780 --> 01:16:05.102
on stage.

01:16:05.102 --> 01:16:07.310
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'm a fan
of boxing, track and field

01:16:07.310 --> 01:16:09.352
and boxing, the sports
nobody really cares about,

01:16:09.352 --> 01:16:10.760
now that UFC is so popular.

01:16:10.760 --> 01:16:13.405
And track and field is--

01:16:13.405 --> 01:16:14.780
it's a little bit
like wrestling.

01:16:14.780 --> 01:16:15.996
When you go, the
people that are there

01:16:15.996 --> 01:16:17.579
are there because
they really love it.

01:16:17.579 --> 01:16:19.430
We'll talk about
wrestling in a little bit,

01:16:19.430 --> 01:16:21.350
professional wrestling.

01:16:21.350 --> 01:16:23.750
But Floyd Mayweather
is obviously

01:16:23.750 --> 01:16:27.200
a colorful character
and one of the best

01:16:27.200 --> 01:16:28.760
records in boxing of all time.

01:16:28.760 --> 01:16:31.160
And a few years back, I got
into watching his stuff,

01:16:31.160 --> 01:16:35.013
and what one sees is
the cars and the money.

01:16:35.013 --> 01:16:36.680
They literally call
themselves the money

01:16:36.680 --> 01:16:39.620
team, and the
spending, and there's

01:16:39.620 --> 01:16:41.240
all the outrageous stuff.

01:16:41.240 --> 01:16:46.400
But I know someone who was in
camp with him who actually was

01:16:46.400 --> 01:16:49.640
a sparring partner for
him, and the lore has it--

01:16:49.640 --> 01:16:52.760
they have very closed door
sparring or clean ups.

01:16:52.760 --> 01:16:55.190
But the lore is
that he would do--

01:16:55.190 --> 01:16:57.320
because nowadays, it's
12, three-minute rounds

01:16:57.320 --> 01:16:59.210
with a minute in
between, used to be 15.

01:16:59.210 --> 01:17:00.890
But now, neuroscientists
stepped in,

01:17:00.890 --> 01:17:02.390
and it turns out a
lot of the deaths

01:17:02.390 --> 01:17:04.348
were occurring when it
was more than 12 rounds.

01:17:04.348 --> 01:17:06.518
For whatever reason,
cut off at 12

01:17:06.518 --> 01:17:08.060
really seemed to
truncate the deaths.

01:17:08.060 --> 01:17:09.290
There are other things too.

01:17:09.290 --> 01:17:11.390
If the dad is
apparently a cornerman--

01:17:11.390 --> 01:17:12.620
we have someone else
here at the podcast who

01:17:12.620 --> 01:17:13.700
knows more about this than me.

01:17:13.700 --> 01:17:14.300
RICK RUBIN: That's fascinating.

01:17:14.300 --> 01:17:14.510
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

01:17:14.510 --> 01:17:16.700
The kid not wanting to
disappoint the parent

01:17:16.700 --> 01:17:18.050
correlated with death.

01:17:18.050 --> 01:17:22.670
I'll get some of this wrong,
and they can come after me.

01:17:22.670 --> 01:17:26.540
But in any case, this guy
who was in Floyd's camp

01:17:26.540 --> 01:17:29.120
said that he would
do 30 to 60 minutes

01:17:29.120 --> 01:17:33.110
of sparring, bringing in fresh
sparring partners with no rest.

01:17:33.110 --> 01:17:36.740
That he would run three or
four times per 24 hour cycle,

01:17:36.740 --> 01:17:38.810
despite all the
critical need for sleep.

01:17:38.810 --> 01:17:42.080
That his training was
unbelievably intense,

01:17:42.080 --> 01:17:45.440
to the point where he
would just chew out,

01:17:45.440 --> 01:17:48.890
chew up, and destroy
all training partners.

01:17:48.890 --> 01:17:53.040
And yet the perception that we
see is it's playful for him.

01:17:53.040 --> 01:17:54.170
So it sounds very similar.

01:17:54.170 --> 01:17:57.260
Like what we see is often
not what goes into it,

01:17:57.260 --> 01:17:59.660
that people are
intensely rigorous.

01:17:59.660 --> 01:18:01.610
RICK RUBIN: Yeah, and
I think, in a way,

01:18:01.610 --> 01:18:03.620
from a psychological
perspective,

01:18:03.620 --> 01:18:08.120
if you knew you were fighting
someone who wasn't taking it

01:18:08.120 --> 01:18:12.280
seriously, that would
give you some confidence,

01:18:12.280 --> 01:18:14.140
and that would not
be a good thing,

01:18:14.140 --> 01:18:20.020
if the person was actually
working really hard,

01:18:20.020 --> 01:18:21.130
outworking you.

01:18:21.130 --> 01:18:22.490
Do you know what I'm saying?

01:18:22.490 --> 01:18:24.970
Like from a psychological
perspective,

01:18:24.970 --> 01:18:26.053
that makes sense to me.

01:18:26.053 --> 01:18:27.970
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So what
I keep coming back to

01:18:27.970 --> 01:18:30.295
is that I'm imagining
in my mind you

01:18:30.295 --> 01:18:31.600
have to ends of the continuum.

01:18:31.600 --> 01:18:34.900
One that is about fairly narrow
focus, training, training,

01:18:34.900 --> 01:18:37.690
strategy implementation,
cultivating craft, building

01:18:37.690 --> 01:18:40.420
craft, and then the
other side is the cloud.

01:18:40.420 --> 01:18:41.240
It's very nebulous.

01:18:41.240 --> 01:18:41.740
Right?

01:18:41.740 --> 01:18:44.560
And there's this
word that I learned

01:18:44.560 --> 01:18:47.260
from a colleague of mine, when I
was down at the Salk Institute,

01:18:47.260 --> 01:18:49.450
when my lab was there,
because he studies this.

01:18:49.450 --> 01:18:52.488
There's this phenomenon that
I don't want to mispronounce,

01:18:52.488 --> 01:18:54.280
because then it sounds
like something else.

01:18:54.280 --> 01:18:58.850
But the correct
pronunciation is pareidolia,

01:18:58.850 --> 01:19:04.010
and pareidolia is our tendency
to look at an amorphous shape,

01:19:04.010 --> 01:19:07.440
like a cloud or
a tree, and think

01:19:07.440 --> 01:19:09.852
that it looks like something
else, an ice cream cone.

01:19:09.852 --> 01:19:10.560
RICK RUBIN: Yeah.

01:19:10.560 --> 01:19:12.102
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
The man in the moon.

01:19:12.102 --> 01:19:12.805
RICK RUBIN: Yep.

01:19:12.805 --> 01:19:14.430
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
that again reveals

01:19:14.430 --> 01:19:19.050
the extent to which the brain
wants to place symbolic filters

01:19:19.050 --> 01:19:20.800
on things, and we need this.

01:19:20.800 --> 01:19:21.300
Right?

01:19:21.300 --> 01:19:23.820
Because I see you walk
in the door, and Rick,

01:19:23.820 --> 01:19:24.940
I recognize you.

01:19:24.940 --> 01:19:27.030
In fact, we have a brain
area, called the fusiform

01:19:27.030 --> 01:19:30.990
face gyrus, that it literally
is a face recognition area.

01:19:30.990 --> 01:19:33.450
And you could be
at any orientation,

01:19:33.450 --> 01:19:36.740
or I could just see your
eyes and know that it's you.

01:19:36.740 --> 01:19:39.240
There's a phenomenon
called propasognosia,

01:19:39.240 --> 01:19:40.830
where people can see faces.

01:19:40.830 --> 01:19:42.600
They can describe
everything in the face,

01:19:42.600 --> 01:19:46.200
but they don't know,
for instance, that it's

01:19:46.200 --> 01:19:51.447
JFK or Madonna or Lex Fridman.

01:19:51.447 --> 01:19:52.780
RICK RUBIN: It's quite the list.

01:19:52.780 --> 01:19:55.600
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Quite the
list, there you go, Lex.

01:19:55.600 --> 01:19:56.860
Run for office, Lex.

01:19:56.860 --> 01:19:57.970
Just kidding.

01:19:57.970 --> 01:20:02.210
It's hard enough to get you to
respond to my texts as it is.

01:20:02.210 --> 01:20:05.950
So we have these filters, and
so we're taking this cloud,

01:20:05.950 --> 01:20:08.133
and we're deciding
what things are.

01:20:08.133 --> 01:20:08.800
RICK RUBIN: Yes.

01:20:08.800 --> 01:20:11.050
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
what I want to go drill

01:20:11.050 --> 01:20:14.560
into your process a little
bit more deeply, when

01:20:14.560 --> 01:20:16.668
you approach a project--

01:20:16.668 --> 01:20:18.460
so everyone meets each
other, shakes hands.

01:20:18.460 --> 01:20:19.015
Here are the engineers.

01:20:19.015 --> 01:20:20.015
We're going to sit down.

01:20:20.015 --> 01:20:22.360
Everyone knows what
they're doing, because you

01:20:22.360 --> 01:20:23.770
work with professionals.

01:20:23.770 --> 01:20:27.820
And you start going, are you
trying to be with the cloud

01:20:27.820 --> 01:20:29.350
or in the implementation?

01:20:29.350 --> 01:20:31.240
Like where are you
in that continuum?

01:20:31.240 --> 01:20:35.050
And forgive me if I'm like
trying to surgically go

01:20:35.050 --> 01:20:37.777
into your process in a way that
would disrupt it in any way.

01:20:37.777 --> 01:20:39.610
But I trust you been
doing this for a while,

01:20:39.610 --> 01:20:41.740
and there's no threat of that.

01:20:41.740 --> 01:20:46.030
RICK RUBIN: I'm in the cloud,
with the exception of I'm

01:20:46.030 --> 01:20:52.790
aware of what could go wrong on
a technical side, and I might--

01:20:52.790 --> 01:20:54.770
like if something
good is happening,

01:20:54.770 --> 01:20:58.180
I might look over and make
sure that we're rolling.

01:20:58.180 --> 01:21:00.680
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So that's a
leap over to here, momentarily,

01:21:00.680 --> 01:21:01.597
but then you get back.

01:21:01.597 --> 01:21:02.600
RICK RUBIN: Maybe.

01:21:02.600 --> 01:21:04.310
Maybe.

01:21:04.310 --> 01:21:07.580
If I feel like--

01:21:07.580 --> 01:21:14.390
if I was in the moment,
I would be in the cloud.

01:21:14.390 --> 01:21:16.340
And if something good
starts happening,

01:21:16.340 --> 01:21:17.930
it would trigger
something in me.

01:21:17.930 --> 01:21:20.000
Like uh-oh, I hope this is--

01:21:20.000 --> 01:21:22.460
I hope we're really doing
this, because I don't know

01:21:22.460 --> 01:21:23.960
if we could ever do this again.

01:21:23.960 --> 01:21:26.720
That would be a thought of--

01:21:26.720 --> 01:21:29.540
when the first time
the real world would

01:21:29.540 --> 01:21:34.450
come into the picture would be
something good is happening.

01:21:34.450 --> 01:21:36.110
Let's not lose it.

01:21:36.110 --> 01:21:38.710
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And when
that happens, do you--

01:21:38.710 --> 01:21:42.070
never been in a studio
besides a podcast studio.

01:21:42.070 --> 01:21:45.400
Do you say, hey, guys,
that sounded good.

01:21:45.400 --> 01:21:46.420
More of that.

01:21:46.420 --> 01:21:48.070
Or do you wait, you
let them continue?

01:21:48.070 --> 01:21:50.140
Because obviously you don't
want to break their flow.

01:21:50.140 --> 01:21:52.057
RICK RUBIN: We'd never
want to break any flow,

01:21:52.057 --> 01:21:53.390
once it's happening.

01:21:53.390 --> 01:21:53.890
Yeah.

01:21:53.890 --> 01:21:57.363
Once something's happening,
just sit back and watch.

01:21:57.363 --> 01:21:59.530
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And do you
think there's resonance,

01:21:59.530 --> 01:22:02.200
like the team of
engineers and other people

01:22:02.200 --> 01:22:04.990
know when "It," quote,
unquote, is happening?

01:22:04.990 --> 01:22:07.000
RICK RUBIN: If everyone's
paying attention, yes.

01:22:07.000 --> 01:22:08.170
When everyone's
paying attention,

01:22:08.170 --> 01:22:09.520
it's usually pretty obvious.

01:22:09.520 --> 01:22:11.230
Sometimes, the thread
will be something

01:22:11.230 --> 01:22:13.420
different than expected,
and maybe not everybody

01:22:13.420 --> 01:22:15.010
would pick up on it.

01:22:15.010 --> 01:22:17.080
And that might be
a particular-- that

01:22:17.080 --> 01:22:21.700
might be particular based on
my taste or an artist's taste.

01:22:21.700 --> 01:22:27.190
Or someone involvement might
say, let's listen back to that.

01:22:27.190 --> 01:22:29.050
I think that was
better than we thought.

01:22:29.050 --> 01:22:31.855
That can happen.

01:22:31.855 --> 01:22:35.560
You said several
things, and it was

01:22:35.560 --> 01:22:38.640
like you said enough for there
to be several conversations.

01:22:38.640 --> 01:22:39.400
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
I tend to do that.

01:22:39.400 --> 01:22:39.910
Sorry.

01:22:39.910 --> 01:22:40.990
Especially with you.

01:22:40.990 --> 01:22:43.580
I don't get to see you nearly
as often as I would like,

01:22:43.580 --> 01:22:45.677
and so when I do,
I confess that I'm

01:22:45.677 --> 01:22:47.260
a little bit of a
kid in a candy shop.

01:22:47.260 --> 01:22:49.427
RICK RUBIN: I wrote down
the brain tells us stories.

01:22:49.427 --> 01:22:53.470
So you talked about, I walk
in, certain data points,

01:22:53.470 --> 01:22:56.470
you recognize me, but it's a
real like looking at a cloud

01:22:56.470 --> 01:22:57.790
shorthand.

01:22:57.790 --> 01:23:03.850
We go through our lives
doing this all day

01:23:03.850 --> 01:23:10.330
with everything we see, and the
short hand, in the case of me,

01:23:10.330 --> 01:23:13.620
you know me, the shorthand
turns out to be right.

01:23:13.620 --> 01:23:17.000
It checks out.

01:23:17.000 --> 01:23:19.640
If it's something we
don't know and something

01:23:19.640 --> 01:23:23.880
we're not familiar
with, something happens,

01:23:23.880 --> 01:23:26.340
we experience something
on the street.

01:23:26.340 --> 01:23:31.310
Something happens, and
it doesn't make sense.

01:23:31.310 --> 01:23:33.530
Something out of the
ordinary happens.

01:23:33.530 --> 01:23:37.590
The first thing is this
doesn't make sense.

01:23:37.590 --> 01:23:41.863
Then, what we do is, again,
subconscious, unconsciously--

01:23:41.863 --> 01:23:44.030
I don't know if it's
unconscious or subconsciously--

01:23:44.030 --> 01:23:48.920
without thinking, we create a
story that explains what just

01:23:48.920 --> 01:23:55.380
happened, a hypothetical that
makes it OK that what just

01:23:55.380 --> 01:23:56.670
happened happened.

01:23:56.670 --> 01:24:01.490
And oh, maybe he's running
because his dog ran away,

01:24:01.490 --> 01:24:02.570
and he's chasing his dog.

01:24:02.570 --> 01:24:04.370
Maybe that's why he's running.

01:24:04.370 --> 01:24:09.020
And as soon as we have that
thought of what it might be,

01:24:09.020 --> 01:24:11.720
we relax, because now it's
not just a guy running,

01:24:11.720 --> 01:24:12.500
and this is weird.

01:24:12.500 --> 01:24:15.650
But it's a guy running, oh, he's
probably running after his dog.

01:24:15.650 --> 01:24:23.380
And now, we register that
story that we just made up,

01:24:23.380 --> 01:24:27.250
without even knowing we were
making it up, as what happened.

01:24:27.250 --> 01:24:29.570
And then later in the
day, if someone says,

01:24:29.570 --> 01:24:30.910
yeah, did you see that guy
running out of the house?

01:24:30.910 --> 01:24:32.080
It's like, yeah, he
was chasing his dog.

01:24:32.080 --> 01:24:32.860
I saw that.

01:24:32.860 --> 01:24:36.400
And you won't even
realize that it was maybe

01:24:36.400 --> 01:24:42.310
hypothetical story that was the
first possible explanation that

01:24:42.310 --> 01:24:45.470
allowed you to continue walking.

01:24:45.470 --> 01:24:46.820
Do you know what I'm saying?

01:24:46.820 --> 01:24:48.710
That's our whole lives.

01:24:48.710 --> 01:24:51.830
Our whole lives are
reacting to things,

01:24:51.830 --> 01:24:53.660
making up a story
of what we think

01:24:53.660 --> 01:24:55.670
may have happened,
without realizing

01:24:55.670 --> 01:24:56.870
that's what we're doing.

01:24:56.870 --> 01:25:00.080
And then living the rest of
our lives as if that thing

01:25:00.080 --> 01:25:05.000
that we made up really
happened, and we never know.

01:25:05.000 --> 01:25:06.630
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
completely agree.

01:25:06.630 --> 01:25:10.280
We confabulate from
birth until death.

01:25:10.280 --> 01:25:14.270
There's this
well-observed phenomenon

01:25:14.270 --> 01:25:16.370
in people who have
memory deficits.

01:25:16.370 --> 01:25:18.200
So there's the sad
example of this and then

01:25:18.200 --> 01:25:20.540
there's the everyday
typical not--

01:25:20.540 --> 01:25:22.560
who knows-- sad or
not sad example.

01:25:22.560 --> 01:25:25.040
So for instance, if somebody
has a slight memory deficit,

01:25:25.040 --> 01:25:27.110
or someone has
Alzheimer's dementia,

01:25:27.110 --> 01:25:29.830
they'll find themselves in
the hallway at night and say,

01:25:29.830 --> 01:25:30.830
what are you doing here?

01:25:30.830 --> 01:25:33.560
And they'll say, oh, I was
going to get a glass of water,

01:25:33.560 --> 01:25:35.930
but they're walking away
from the direction that

01:25:35.930 --> 01:25:36.770
would make sense.

01:25:36.770 --> 01:25:37.670
RICK RUBIN: Yeah.

01:25:37.670 --> 01:25:38.960
ANDREW HUBERMAN: People who--

01:25:38.960 --> 01:25:40.700
alcoholics who drink
enough develop something

01:25:40.700 --> 01:25:42.740
called Korsakoff syndrome,
where a certain brain

01:25:42.740 --> 01:25:44.403
area gets messed up.

01:25:44.403 --> 01:25:46.070
And you'll ask them
a question, like oh,

01:25:46.070 --> 01:25:47.070
what are you doing here?

01:25:47.070 --> 01:25:49.280
And they will come up
with incredible stories,

01:25:49.280 --> 01:25:51.590
sometimes interesting
stories, that

01:25:51.590 --> 01:25:52.982
have no bearing on reality.

01:25:52.982 --> 01:25:53.690
RICK RUBIN: Yeah.

01:25:53.690 --> 01:25:54.950
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You ask
them who their name is--

01:25:54.950 --> 01:25:55.790
RICK RUBIN: But do they believe?

01:25:55.790 --> 01:25:56.960
They believe that's
what happened.

01:25:56.960 --> 01:25:58.877
ANDREW HUBERMAN: With
100% certainty, and this

01:25:58.877 --> 01:26:01.340
actually relates to a
lot of the now better

01:26:01.340 --> 01:26:04.410
understood controversy
around repressed memories.

01:26:04.410 --> 01:26:07.200
You can, especially
from young people,

01:26:07.200 --> 01:26:10.432
you can pull memories from them
of things that never happened.

01:26:10.432 --> 01:26:12.390
This has been demonstrated
over and over again.

01:26:12.390 --> 01:26:14.180
So courtrooms know to
be very cautious now

01:26:14.180 --> 01:26:16.190
about this whole notion
of repressed memories.

01:26:16.190 --> 01:26:17.523
RICK RUBIN: That's good to know.

01:26:17.523 --> 01:26:20.120
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah,
very, very complicated area

01:26:20.120 --> 01:26:21.950
of the law, as you can imagine.

01:26:21.950 --> 01:26:25.070
Because we tend to
want to trust victims,

01:26:25.070 --> 01:26:27.020
for understandable reasons.

01:26:27.020 --> 01:26:29.270
But in terms of
accuracy of details,

01:26:29.270 --> 01:26:31.550
two people have very
different accounts

01:26:31.550 --> 01:26:33.710
of the same experiences,
and this has

01:26:33.710 --> 01:26:35.750
been shown over and over again.

01:26:35.750 --> 01:26:37.400
That you can do well
in the laboratory.

01:26:37.400 --> 01:26:38.700
It's pretty interesting.

01:26:38.700 --> 01:26:40.280
So again, because
of this selective

01:26:40.280 --> 01:26:43.183
filtering and
storytelling, and we are--

01:26:43.183 --> 01:26:44.600
I think it was
Salman Rushdie, who

01:26:44.600 --> 01:26:46.308
said we are the
storytelling species.

01:26:46.308 --> 01:26:46.850
He probably--

01:26:46.850 --> 01:26:47.030
RICK RUBIN: Wow.

01:26:47.030 --> 01:26:48.800
I was going to say we're
storytelling machines,

01:26:48.800 --> 01:26:49.460
but that's great.

01:26:49.460 --> 01:26:50.390
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

01:26:50.390 --> 01:26:52.430
We are story--

01:26:52.430 --> 01:26:54.170
I would say that
the big five, if I

01:26:54.170 --> 01:26:59.840
had to pick up a brain function,
is we are very limited filters.

01:26:59.840 --> 01:27:03.290
The mantis shrimp sees 67
shades of red for every one

01:27:03.290 --> 01:27:04.380
that we see.

01:27:04.380 --> 01:27:06.920
So they have access to things
we don't have access to.

01:27:06.920 --> 01:27:07.670
RICK RUBIN: Yes.

01:27:07.670 --> 01:27:09.170
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
They're not, as far

01:27:09.170 --> 01:27:13.550
as I releasing, albums of the
Red Hot Chili Peppers caliber,

01:27:13.550 --> 01:27:14.360
but who knows.

01:27:14.360 --> 01:27:15.500
Maybe down there they are.

01:27:15.500 --> 01:27:18.500
I did see something, by the way
as a relevant tangent recently,

01:27:18.500 --> 01:27:20.590
and I don't know if it's--

01:27:20.590 --> 01:27:23.560
look, even if it's
crazy, it's super cool.

01:27:23.560 --> 01:27:27.740
If you take a device
that amplifies

01:27:27.740 --> 01:27:30.080
the electrical signals
coming from cactus,

01:27:30.080 --> 01:27:34.490
and you just translate that
into a simple rule of conversion

01:27:34.490 --> 01:27:36.530
to two or three
pitches of sound,

01:27:36.530 --> 01:27:39.620
the music that comes out of
it is beautiful, nothing short

01:27:39.620 --> 01:27:40.250
of beautiful.

01:27:40.250 --> 01:27:43.040
And when I saw that, the
teenager in me thought,

01:27:43.040 --> 01:27:44.450
you know when we
hear whale song,

01:27:44.450 --> 01:27:45.440
we think it's so beautiful.

01:27:45.440 --> 01:27:47.270
Like what if they're just
like cursing at each other

01:27:47.270 --> 01:27:48.050
the whole time?

01:27:48.050 --> 01:27:48.560
Right?

01:27:48.560 --> 01:27:50.600
Maybe they're in there
like a Rogen episode,

01:27:50.600 --> 01:27:52.683
when he invites all his
comedian friends in there.

01:27:52.683 --> 01:27:53.340
Who knows?

01:27:53.340 --> 01:27:55.400
Maybe it's a
psychoanalytic conversation

01:27:55.400 --> 01:27:56.660
about their childhood traumas.

01:27:56.660 --> 01:27:57.533
I don't know.

01:27:57.533 --> 01:27:58.200
RICK RUBIN: Yes.

01:27:58.200 --> 01:28:01.063
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But we decide,
whale song is beautiful.

01:28:01.063 --> 01:28:01.730
RICK RUBIN: Yes.

01:28:01.730 --> 01:28:04.010
ANDREW HUBERMAN: We decide
cactus are just plants.

01:28:04.010 --> 01:28:06.530
RICK RUBIN: And it's
beautiful to us,

01:28:06.530 --> 01:28:08.960
and we're right, that
it is beautiful to us.

01:28:08.960 --> 01:28:11.340
But it doesn't mean we
know anything about it.

01:28:11.340 --> 01:28:12.590
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That's right.

01:28:12.590 --> 01:28:12.860
Yeah.

01:28:12.860 --> 01:28:14.810
So we have these filters,
perceptual filters.

01:28:14.810 --> 01:28:18.500
We only can see and hear,
smell and taste what we can,

01:28:18.500 --> 01:28:20.900
and then the brain likes
to work in symbols.

01:28:20.900 --> 01:28:24.830
We tend to like to match
that person whose shoes are

01:28:24.830 --> 01:28:28.557
messed up must be homeless.

01:28:28.557 --> 01:28:30.140
I've had a couple
of instances in life

01:28:30.140 --> 01:28:32.840
where I saw what I thought
was a homeless vagrant

01:28:32.840 --> 01:28:35.180
inside a building at an
academic institution.

01:28:35.180 --> 01:28:39.170
It turned out, it was the
most accomplished person

01:28:39.170 --> 01:28:41.065
in the field.

01:28:41.065 --> 01:28:42.860
That's always cool.

01:28:42.860 --> 01:28:45.560
Yes, that happened at Berkeley.

01:28:45.560 --> 01:28:52.290
Then, the other thing that we
do is we tend to put symbol--

01:28:52.290 --> 01:28:55.130
so we said perception, symbol
representations, and then

01:28:55.130 --> 01:28:58.610
our memories are
entirely confabulated,

01:28:58.610 --> 01:29:02.630
based on already deficient
symbol and perceptual

01:29:02.630 --> 01:29:03.313
representation.

01:29:03.313 --> 01:29:03.980
RICK RUBIN: Yes.

01:29:03.980 --> 01:29:05.390
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And so I
never liked the statement

01:29:05.390 --> 01:29:06.920
that we don't know
how the brain works.

01:29:06.920 --> 01:29:08.550
I think we do know
how the brain works,

01:29:08.550 --> 01:29:11.480
but that it works through
very limited filters.

01:29:11.480 --> 01:29:12.350
RICK RUBIN: Yes.

01:29:12.350 --> 01:29:14.670
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So knowing
that and accepting it,

01:29:14.670 --> 01:29:22.430
and it seems to me that this
idea of looking to nature,

01:29:22.430 --> 01:29:26.180
looking outside
us, is so critical.

01:29:26.180 --> 01:29:28.580
And in fact, I hope you
won't mind me sharing this,

01:29:28.580 --> 01:29:31.640
but a few years back, I had
sent you something by text.

01:29:31.640 --> 01:29:34.220
And I was kind of in
disbelief about something

01:29:34.220 --> 01:29:35.610
I had seen in the media.

01:29:35.610 --> 01:29:37.130
I was like, they
got it all wrong.

01:29:37.130 --> 01:29:39.800
And I knew the person
involved, and it was not

01:29:39.800 --> 01:29:41.120
a good situation for them.

01:29:41.120 --> 01:29:44.150
And I was like, they got it
all wrong, and you wrote back.

01:29:44.150 --> 01:29:47.830
You said, it's all lies.

01:29:47.830 --> 01:29:50.690
Back to nature, the only truth.

01:29:50.690 --> 01:29:51.670
RICK RUBIN: Wow.

01:29:51.670 --> 01:29:52.170
That's wild.

01:29:52.170 --> 01:29:53.795
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
I wrote that down.

01:29:53.795 --> 01:29:54.730
I put it over my desk.

01:29:54.730 --> 01:29:55.397
RICK RUBIN: Wow.

01:29:55.397 --> 01:29:57.380
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And I still--

01:29:57.380 --> 01:30:00.520
I'd tattoo it on my forehead,
if I didn't already have

01:30:00.520 --> 01:30:02.500
it well-committed to memory.

01:30:02.500 --> 01:30:05.300
But I know that's true.

01:30:05.300 --> 01:30:05.800
Right?

01:30:05.800 --> 01:30:07.570
Nature we can look at and it's--

01:30:07.570 --> 01:30:09.160
RICK RUBIN: When I
say it's all lies,

01:30:09.160 --> 01:30:11.770
you just talked
about our ability

01:30:11.770 --> 01:30:19.120
to how limited our facility to
see and understand what we see.

01:30:19.120 --> 01:30:19.810
Yes?

01:30:19.810 --> 01:30:20.685
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yes.

01:30:20.685 --> 01:30:25.600
RICK RUBIN: So based on
that, that leads us to we

01:30:25.600 --> 01:30:26.863
can't know much.

01:30:26.863 --> 01:30:28.030
Do you know what I'm saying?

01:30:28.030 --> 01:30:34.500
Our resolution is
so low on everything

01:30:34.500 --> 01:30:40.240
that we're really just like
we're grasping at straws.

01:30:40.240 --> 01:30:42.040
We have no idea.

01:30:42.040 --> 01:30:45.880
We have no idea, and there's
great power and knowing that.

01:30:45.880 --> 01:30:51.470
Because if you think you know
what's going on, chances are,

01:30:51.470 --> 01:30:53.480
you're being deceived.

01:30:53.480 --> 01:30:55.610
Not because somebody
is deceiving you,

01:30:55.610 --> 01:30:57.110
but because they're
telling you what

01:30:57.110 --> 01:30:59.125
they see, and they don't know.

01:30:59.125 --> 01:31:00.750
It's all-- do you
know what I'm saying?

01:31:00.750 --> 01:31:03.290
It's all made up.

01:31:03.290 --> 01:31:05.450
Everything we know is made up.

01:31:05.450 --> 01:31:07.370
Maybe, maybe it's true.

01:31:07.370 --> 01:31:09.860
This brings us to pro wrestling.

01:31:09.860 --> 01:31:12.380
It's the reason
that pro wrestling

01:31:12.380 --> 01:31:16.370
is closer to reality than
anything else we can watch

01:31:16.370 --> 01:31:18.470
or any other content.

01:31:18.470 --> 01:31:21.540
It's we know it's made up.

01:31:21.540 --> 01:31:25.020
We know that it's a performance.

01:31:25.020 --> 01:31:31.090
It's storytelling, and
that's how everything

01:31:31.090 --> 01:31:34.900
is, except we think wrestling
is fake, and the world is real.

01:31:34.900 --> 01:31:37.735
Wrestling's real,
and the world's fake.

01:31:37.735 --> 01:31:39.610
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You
talk about in the book--

01:31:39.610 --> 01:31:42.580
we're definitely going
in this direction.

01:31:42.580 --> 01:31:46.540
In the book, you talk about
this notion of entertaining

01:31:46.540 --> 01:31:49.000
the idea of the
opposite being true.

01:31:49.000 --> 01:31:52.600
And there are not only
emerging but established fields

01:31:52.600 --> 01:31:54.820
of psychology that are
making great ground, I think,

01:31:54.820 --> 01:31:58.120
into the human psyche, Byron
Katie's work and others.

01:31:58.120 --> 01:32:00.250
Where you take a
statement, and you start

01:32:00.250 --> 01:32:02.050
playing with that statement.

01:32:02.050 --> 01:32:04.220
You poke at its authenticity.

01:32:04.220 --> 01:32:06.770
And when I first heard that,
I thought, this is hokey.

01:32:06.770 --> 01:32:07.270
Right?

01:32:07.270 --> 01:32:07.990
It's just words.

01:32:07.990 --> 01:32:09.910
And then I realized
how foolish I

01:32:09.910 --> 01:32:13.450
was being, because she's
really on to something.

01:32:13.450 --> 01:32:16.760
And there are others too,
of course, but in science,

01:32:16.760 --> 01:32:18.170
that's exactly what you do.

01:32:18.170 --> 01:32:21.680
You don't really ask
questions in science.

01:32:21.680 --> 01:32:24.770
You are forced to raise
hypotheses and try

01:32:24.770 --> 01:32:26.520
and say true or false.

01:32:26.520 --> 01:32:29.300
Now, there are limitations
to that approach, certainly.

01:32:29.300 --> 01:32:31.700
Pure observational
studies have been

01:32:31.700 --> 01:32:33.980
incredible in terms of
what they've revealed

01:32:33.980 --> 01:32:35.438
to us, especially in medicine.

01:32:35.438 --> 01:32:37.730
A patient that has a bullet
hole through a certain area

01:32:37.730 --> 01:32:39.770
of the brain, you don't
go in and say, oh, I

01:32:39.770 --> 01:32:43.010
hypothesize that person will
have a deficit in seeing faces.

01:32:43.010 --> 01:32:43.518
No.

01:32:43.518 --> 01:32:45.560
The person wandered into
the clinic, and they go,

01:32:45.560 --> 01:32:48.770
this person sees faces but
can't make sense of them.

01:32:48.770 --> 01:32:51.560
And then you reverse--

01:32:51.560 --> 01:32:54.050
you forensically arrive
at an understanding.

01:32:54.050 --> 01:32:58.910
So but in general, we go
about things in this way,

01:32:58.910 --> 01:33:02.510
and considering that the
opposite might be true, well,

01:33:02.510 --> 01:33:06.050
that's a little bit, I suppose,
of like seeing the whale

01:33:06.050 --> 01:33:07.550
at the surface of the water.

01:33:07.550 --> 01:33:10.230
It's like, well, the
opposite of my experience,

01:33:10.230 --> 01:33:14.010
which is all above
water, for the most part,

01:33:14.010 --> 01:33:17.460
is maybe not the complete
experience of life.

01:33:17.460 --> 01:33:19.560
You start seeing the
inverse all the time.

01:33:19.560 --> 01:33:20.493
So I want to--

01:33:20.493 --> 01:33:22.410
RICK RUBIN: Consider the
inverse all the time,

01:33:22.410 --> 01:33:27.330
and it really relates to the way
that you described how we see

01:33:27.330 --> 01:33:29.760
colors is based on contrast.

01:33:29.760 --> 01:33:33.920
So maybe blue is only blue
in relation to yellow.

01:33:33.920 --> 01:33:42.330
So if blue is our choice, if
we're not considering yellow,

01:33:42.330 --> 01:33:43.653
blue doesn't exist.

01:33:43.653 --> 01:33:44.820
Do you know what I'm saying?

01:33:44.820 --> 01:33:45.000
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Absolutely.

01:33:45.000 --> 01:33:46.860
RICK RUBIN: It's like,
we talk about night.

01:33:46.860 --> 01:33:48.820
It's only night,
because there's day.

01:33:48.820 --> 01:33:50.490
If there was no day,
there is no night.

01:33:50.490 --> 01:33:56.370
In all of our cases,
it's like the yin yang.

01:33:56.370 --> 01:34:00.540
There's the light and
the shadow always.

01:34:00.540 --> 01:34:03.870
There's always another
side for everything,

01:34:03.870 --> 01:34:08.590
and we focus on one aspect.

01:34:08.590 --> 01:34:11.410
But if we look at the
other aspect, chances are,

01:34:11.410 --> 01:34:13.660
we'll learn something too.

01:34:13.660 --> 01:34:15.670
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
The nervous system

01:34:15.670 --> 01:34:18.040
is not just able to do this.

01:34:18.040 --> 01:34:20.560
It's the way it does everything.

01:34:20.560 --> 01:34:22.540
Two experiments I'll
just briefly describe.

01:34:22.540 --> 01:34:23.962
My scientific
great grandparents,

01:34:23.962 --> 01:34:25.420
David Hubel, Torsten
Wiesel, showed

01:34:25.420 --> 01:34:28.150
that, if you force a
person to look at something

01:34:28.150 --> 01:34:30.880
for a long period of time
without moving their eyes--

01:34:30.880 --> 01:34:33.340
there's a way that you can do
this-- the image disappears.

01:34:33.340 --> 01:34:35.680
Because normally, your eyes
are making little microsecods,

01:34:35.680 --> 01:34:37.120
and you're comparing
what you're seeing

01:34:37.120 --> 01:34:38.290
to what's right next to it.

01:34:38.290 --> 01:34:40.563
Pixel by pixel, pixel by
pixel, pixel by pixel.

01:34:40.563 --> 01:34:41.230
RICK RUBIN: Wow.

01:34:41.230 --> 01:34:42.160
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
We don't even have

01:34:42.160 --> 01:34:43.930
to use the example of
pressing on the arm.

01:34:43.930 --> 01:34:45.800
We're sitting in
chairs right now,

01:34:45.800 --> 01:34:47.660
and until I said you
know what's going on

01:34:47.660 --> 01:34:49.910
at the level of sensation
on the backs of your thighs,

01:34:49.910 --> 01:34:50.590
you were unaware of it.

01:34:50.590 --> 01:34:53.170
Because if you experience a
pressure or a smell in a room,

01:34:53.170 --> 01:34:55.420
you ever walk in the smell
is either good or not good,

01:34:55.420 --> 01:34:56.860
pretty soon, the
smell disappears.

01:34:56.860 --> 01:35:01.930
The neurons are still firing
like sledgehammers on a bell,

01:35:01.930 --> 01:35:04.900
but we become blind
and deaf to it.

01:35:04.900 --> 01:35:07.660
Because the nervous
system likes to habituate

01:35:07.660 --> 01:35:10.600
the value of that signal
when it's there often,

01:35:10.600 --> 01:35:12.700
and it's only the stuff
that comes through

01:35:12.700 --> 01:35:14.890
signal the noise
that kind of jolts us

01:35:14.890 --> 01:35:18.070
into attention and awareness.

01:35:18.070 --> 01:35:20.890
And I want to return to
attention awareness, which are

01:35:20.890 --> 01:35:22.390
prominent themes in the book.

01:35:22.390 --> 01:35:25.360
And I think, in an
important way, not just,

01:35:25.360 --> 01:35:27.190
oh, attention
awareness is important,

01:35:27.190 --> 01:35:31.810
but you also give insight into
how to pay better attention,

01:35:31.810 --> 01:35:34.688
how to pay awareness
with the understanding

01:35:34.688 --> 01:35:36.730
that people are going to
go about it differently,

01:35:36.730 --> 01:35:40.550
but I do want to ask
you about wrestling.

01:35:40.550 --> 01:35:45.387
Because when I was growing up,
I lived South of the Cow Palace,

01:35:45.387 --> 01:35:47.220
and there was some
wrestling going on there.

01:35:47.220 --> 01:35:49.810
I think back then it was WWF.

01:35:49.810 --> 01:35:51.470
There was a short
stint in my childhood

01:35:51.470 --> 01:35:55.040
where I paid attention
to, in particular,

01:35:55.040 --> 01:35:57.110
was it Koko B. Ware, the
guy that had a Macaw?

01:35:57.110 --> 01:35:59.630
I was obsessed with tropical
birds, and he would come in,

01:35:59.630 --> 01:36:01.380
and he put his tropical
bird on the thing.

01:36:01.380 --> 01:36:03.050
And then George
"The Animal" Steele,

01:36:03.050 --> 01:36:04.427
the guy that would eat the ring.

01:36:04.427 --> 01:36:05.135
RICK RUBIN: Yeah.

01:36:05.135 --> 01:36:05.968
ANDREW HUBERMAN: OK.

01:36:05.968 --> 01:36:08.060
So and--

01:36:08.060 --> 01:36:09.780
RICK RUBIN: I believe
he was a professor.

01:36:09.780 --> 01:36:10.280
Seriously.

01:36:10.280 --> 01:36:11.340
Seriously.

01:36:11.340 --> 01:36:12.270
Seriously.

01:36:12.270 --> 01:36:13.070
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Was he really?

01:36:13.070 --> 01:36:13.700
RICK RUBIN: In real life.

01:36:13.700 --> 01:36:14.520
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Amazing.

01:36:14.520 --> 01:36:16.040
RICK RUBIN: He was a professor,
but he played Georgia

01:36:16.040 --> 01:36:17.750
"The Animal" Steele
as a wrestler.

01:36:17.750 --> 01:36:19.333
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
I loved the movie

01:36:19.333 --> 01:36:21.542
The Wrestler, The Wrestler.

01:36:21.542 --> 01:36:23.000
RICK RUBIN: Darren
Aronofsky movie.

01:36:23.000 --> 01:36:23.670
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It
was Mickey Rourke.

01:36:23.670 --> 01:36:24.710
RICK RUBIN: With Mickey Rourke.

01:36:24.710 --> 01:36:25.627
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

01:36:25.627 --> 01:36:29.875
One of the reasons I liked it
is I once visited Asbury Park.

01:36:29.875 --> 01:36:31.250
Isn't that where
that was filmed?

01:36:31.250 --> 01:36:33.980
There's a vacant-- he goes
to visit his daughter.

01:36:33.980 --> 01:36:37.123
There's a vacant amusement park
or abandoned amusement park

01:36:37.123 --> 01:36:38.540
scene there that
was really eerie,

01:36:38.540 --> 01:36:40.082
still kind of haunts
me a little bit.

01:36:40.082 --> 01:36:44.072
There's something about
the East Coast in fall.

01:36:44.072 --> 01:36:45.530
All the places that
people normally

01:36:45.530 --> 01:36:48.050
go just for the summer,
that we don't have out here

01:36:48.050 --> 01:36:50.630
in the West coast,
people in the East Coast

01:36:50.630 --> 01:36:52.100
are just tougher than we are.

01:36:52.100 --> 01:36:53.840
It still haunts me.

01:36:53.840 --> 01:36:55.370
Great movie.

01:36:55.370 --> 01:36:57.290
But I remember
watching wrestling,

01:36:57.290 --> 01:37:01.160
and it was at that age, I think
I was probably about 12, 13,

01:37:01.160 --> 01:37:09.350
maybe 11, 12, 13, where
you're entering puberty,

01:37:09.350 --> 01:37:14.090
and puberty is a fundamental
landmark of development.

01:37:14.090 --> 01:37:15.650
It's the most rapid
period of aging.

01:37:15.650 --> 01:37:18.590
It's also when we start
to change our rule set.

01:37:18.590 --> 01:37:21.290
Like certain people and
certain kinds of interactions

01:37:21.290 --> 01:37:24.260
take on profoundly
different meaning.

01:37:24.260 --> 01:37:25.130
Right?

01:37:25.130 --> 01:37:29.720
It's not just a reproductive
competence time, and when kids,

01:37:29.720 --> 01:37:30.740
their bodies change.

01:37:30.740 --> 01:37:32.917
The rulebook changes
fundamentally.

01:37:32.917 --> 01:37:35.000
RICK RUBIN: Our understanding
of the world changes

01:37:35.000 --> 01:37:35.600
in that moment.

01:37:35.600 --> 01:37:36.517
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

01:37:36.517 --> 01:37:39.410
The moment that a child
understands really what sex is

01:37:39.410 --> 01:37:42.500
and how they got there and
that a lot of the stuff that we

01:37:42.500 --> 01:37:45.860
see in the world is kind of
passively, or not so passively,

01:37:45.860 --> 01:37:49.520
being sent through that filter,
it's like it's something.

01:37:49.520 --> 01:37:54.140
It changes the rule
book of perception.

01:37:54.140 --> 01:37:58.400
I view this age,
from about 11 to 13,

01:37:58.400 --> 01:38:01.250
at least for me was a
unique transition point.

01:38:01.250 --> 01:38:06.290
Where the gap between what
I perceived as reality

01:38:06.290 --> 01:38:07.935
and fiction was blurry.

01:38:07.935 --> 01:38:10.310
This is captured pretty well
in that movie "Stand By Me,"

01:38:10.310 --> 01:38:11.870
where they're hanging around
the campfire at night.

01:38:11.870 --> 01:38:13.460
And the kid says,
who do you think

01:38:13.460 --> 01:38:16.280
would win in a fight between
Superman and Mighty Mouse?

01:38:16.280 --> 01:38:19.250
And the other kid
says, like you idiot,

01:38:19.250 --> 01:38:21.770
Mighty Mouse is a cartoon.

01:38:21.770 --> 01:38:25.460
Of course Superman would win.

01:38:25.460 --> 01:38:29.420
And like to me, that's being
11 and 1/2 or 12 years old.

01:38:29.420 --> 01:38:33.140
Where your understanding
of reality as you know

01:38:33.140 --> 01:38:35.030
it is changing, but
it's not completely

01:38:35.030 --> 01:38:36.925
crystallized into an
adult form reality.

01:38:36.925 --> 01:38:39.050
RICK RUBIN: That sounds
like a really healthy place

01:38:39.050 --> 01:38:41.510
to be, to me, like that.

01:38:41.510 --> 01:38:45.260
Not letting it crystallize,
I think there's

01:38:45.260 --> 01:38:47.977
where the downfall happens.

01:38:47.977 --> 01:38:50.060
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So I have
a question specifically

01:38:50.060 --> 01:38:52.310
about wrestling, but it's
really about process.

01:38:52.310 --> 01:38:53.243
RICK RUBIN: Yeah.

01:38:53.243 --> 01:38:55.160
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I want
to know whether or not

01:38:55.160 --> 01:38:59.300
you watch wrestling because it
allows you to access the energy

01:38:59.300 --> 01:39:04.460
state in your body and mind and
that mode of thinking in which

01:39:04.460 --> 01:39:10.330
reality, as one conceives
it, is somewhat blurry.

01:39:10.330 --> 01:39:14.640
Or is it for a number of
other reasons, which is fine.

01:39:14.640 --> 01:39:17.700
Is that the energy you're
trying to export and bring

01:39:17.700 --> 01:39:21.580
to the creative process
elsewhere, to life?

01:39:21.580 --> 01:39:24.270
Is it that anything is
possible, or that we're

01:39:24.270 --> 01:39:25.270
dealing with archetypes?

01:39:25.270 --> 01:39:27.670
Because it doesn't matter if
it's Koko B. Ware or Randy

01:39:27.670 --> 01:39:29.712
"Macho Man" Savage, or
George "The Animal" Steele

01:39:29.712 --> 01:39:30.910
and the lovely Elizabeth.

01:39:30.910 --> 01:39:34.360
I guess I did watch a
little bit of wrestling.

01:39:34.360 --> 01:39:36.910
They are archetypes,
much like the Greek myths

01:39:36.910 --> 01:39:38.290
or the Bible or--

01:39:38.290 --> 01:39:41.980
no disrespect to the Bible or
to Greek myths or to wrestling,

01:39:41.980 --> 01:39:42.910
for that matter.

01:39:42.910 --> 01:39:46.480
Archetypes are a powerful
filter for humans,

01:39:46.480 --> 01:39:49.960
but we know that they're
a very limited filter too,

01:39:49.960 --> 01:39:53.290
because people aren't built
like square wave functions.

01:39:53.290 --> 01:39:55.760
We have curves and
contours and complexity.

01:39:55.760 --> 01:40:00.340
So what is the deal with your
relationship to wrestling?

01:40:00.340 --> 01:40:03.250
RICK RUBIN: I think it
maintains that playfulness.

01:40:03.250 --> 01:40:05.020
Anything is possible.

01:40:05.020 --> 01:40:08.610
We expect the unexpected
all the time in wrestling.

01:40:11.680 --> 01:40:17.890
And it's a way to have
a feeling of the energy

01:40:17.890 --> 01:40:22.300
of a sport with no competition.

01:40:22.300 --> 01:40:25.810
Everyone is working together to
put on the best show they can.

01:40:25.810 --> 01:40:30.040
So it's more like a ballet than
it is like a sporting event,

01:40:30.040 --> 01:40:34.740
and there's great
skill involved.

01:40:34.740 --> 01:40:38.190
It's one of the few things
that I can watch and really

01:40:38.190 --> 01:40:39.660
feel relaxed.

01:40:39.660 --> 01:40:41.078
It relaxes me.

01:40:41.078 --> 01:40:42.870
I don't feel like I
have to think about it.

01:40:42.870 --> 01:40:45.595
I can just relax and enjoy it.

01:40:45.595 --> 01:40:47.220
ANDREW HUBERMAN: This
brings up a topic

01:40:47.220 --> 01:40:49.095
that is very near and
dear to my heart, which

01:40:49.095 --> 01:40:51.090
is this notion of
dopamine schedules.

01:40:51.090 --> 01:40:53.130
I never want to reduce
everything to dopamine,

01:40:53.130 --> 01:40:56.310
but dopamine is the universal
currency of delight, pleasure,

01:40:56.310 --> 01:40:57.600
motivation seeking.

01:40:57.600 --> 01:40:59.310
There are other
chemicals involved too,

01:40:59.310 --> 01:41:02.880
but there's a beautiful
experiment and a couple

01:41:02.880 --> 01:41:05.490
of examples that I'll
use as a foundation

01:41:05.490 --> 01:41:07.950
to more questions
about wrestling

01:41:07.950 --> 01:41:09.480
and why it's powerful.

01:41:09.480 --> 01:41:12.540
And why other people may
want to use wrestling

01:41:12.540 --> 01:41:17.400
or some other endeavor as a
way to access creative energy

01:41:17.400 --> 01:41:19.760
and source.

01:41:19.760 --> 01:41:21.760
Earlier, we talked about
you can train an animal

01:41:21.760 --> 01:41:24.400
to press a lever three
times and then get reward,

01:41:24.400 --> 01:41:27.112
and it will learn three is
the magic number for reward,

01:41:27.112 --> 01:41:28.070
and then it can switch.

01:41:28.070 --> 01:41:28.990
It takes a little
bit of training,

01:41:28.990 --> 01:41:29.830
and then they can switch.

01:41:29.830 --> 01:41:31.210
But they can't do prime numbers.

01:41:31.210 --> 01:41:33.640
They can't do high
abstraction schedules.

01:41:33.640 --> 01:41:34.990
Humans either.

01:41:34.990 --> 01:41:38.440
We're not very good at
figuring out the rule set

01:41:38.440 --> 01:41:40.750
for optimal foraging.

01:41:40.750 --> 01:41:44.420
We do it well enough to persist
as a species, at least for now,

01:41:44.420 --> 01:41:50.420
but it's very likely that we
are not tapping into that system

01:41:50.420 --> 01:41:52.460
as well as we could,
and how would we

01:41:52.460 --> 01:41:53.570
know, if we don't know?

01:41:53.570 --> 01:41:56.150
It's one of those you who don't
know what you don't knows.

01:41:56.150 --> 01:41:57.710
There's a beautiful
experiment that

01:41:57.710 --> 01:41:59.960
explored when
dopamine is released

01:41:59.960 --> 01:42:04.080
in the context of watching
sport or watching comedy,

01:42:04.080 --> 01:42:05.070
believe it or not.

01:42:05.070 --> 01:42:07.025
And with the comedy
stuff, it was every time

01:42:07.025 --> 01:42:08.400
there was a
surprise, it was kind

01:42:08.400 --> 01:42:10.740
of that jarring like
ha-ha, and they'd measure

01:42:10.740 --> 01:42:11.820
people's dopamine output.

01:42:11.820 --> 01:42:14.460
They were also brain imaging.

01:42:14.460 --> 01:42:18.090
In a game of basketball,
it's a beautiful opportunity

01:42:18.090 --> 01:42:18.810
experimentally.

01:42:18.810 --> 01:42:20.743
Because every time
one team gets the ball

01:42:20.743 --> 01:42:22.410
or is shooting free
throws or something,

01:42:22.410 --> 01:42:24.118
they're going down
court, and it's either

01:42:24.118 --> 01:42:25.970
going to end up in the
basket, or it's not.

01:42:25.970 --> 01:42:28.470
Might end up on the free throw
line, but it's end up or not.

01:42:28.470 --> 01:42:31.800
So what they found is that
the schedule of anticipation

01:42:31.800 --> 01:42:34.140
was every time there was a
switch of which team got it.

01:42:34.140 --> 01:42:36.540
So you're waiting,
waiting, and then it's, ah.

01:42:36.540 --> 01:42:37.540
You're waiting, waiting.

01:42:37.540 --> 01:42:38.190
Yes.

01:42:38.190 --> 01:42:39.870
Waiting, waiting, three-pointer.

01:42:39.870 --> 01:42:41.700
Ah, awesome, and if
something happened

01:42:41.700 --> 01:42:43.260
where it looked
like they were going

01:42:43.260 --> 01:42:46.890
to make the three-pointer, but
then somebody basically swatted

01:42:46.890 --> 01:42:49.140
the ball away, and then
went for a half court shot,

01:42:49.140 --> 01:42:52.402
like you don't expect that very
often, bigger dopamine release.

01:42:52.402 --> 01:42:54.360
So that's kind of how
the dopamine thing works.

01:42:54.360 --> 01:42:58.440
When you describe
wrestling, I wonder,

01:42:58.440 --> 01:43:00.930
because you don't
know the script,

01:43:00.930 --> 01:43:03.370
it's not one team gets it,
then the other team gets it.

01:43:03.370 --> 01:43:04.787
You don't know
who's going to win.

01:43:04.787 --> 01:43:06.443
Anything could happen
is what you said.

01:43:06.443 --> 01:43:07.110
RICK RUBIN: Yes.

01:43:07.110 --> 01:43:08.485
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
The availability

01:43:08.485 --> 01:43:14.690
of that dopamine surge or drip,
which is a powerful thing,

01:43:14.690 --> 01:43:17.210
is completely--

01:43:17.210 --> 01:43:21.165
it's completely out of your
reach in terms of anticipation.

01:43:21.165 --> 01:43:22.790
You don't know when
it's going to come,

01:43:22.790 --> 01:43:25.820
but it must arrive often
enough that you return to it

01:43:25.820 --> 01:43:28.800
11 hours a week of watching.

01:43:28.800 --> 01:43:31.050
In many ways, the
way I'm starting

01:43:31.050 --> 01:43:33.780
to conceptualize the creative
process is a little bit

01:43:33.780 --> 01:43:34.470
the same.

01:43:34.470 --> 01:43:36.510
You don't know where
those nuggets of gold

01:43:36.510 --> 01:43:39.840
and those loose threads are,
but you have enough experience--

01:43:39.840 --> 01:43:42.420
and in this case, I am
referring to you specifically--

01:43:42.420 --> 01:43:44.340
to know that they are in there.

01:43:44.340 --> 01:43:46.920
The people walking in this
room have a certain level

01:43:46.920 --> 01:43:54.190
of ability and talent to create
that the map will form itself,

01:43:54.190 --> 01:43:56.830
as we are going
through the voyage.

01:43:56.830 --> 01:43:58.990
And those nuggets of--

01:43:58.990 --> 01:44:01.210
here I'm calling them
dopamine-- but they

01:44:01.210 --> 01:44:03.575
are out there,
and that knowledge

01:44:03.575 --> 01:44:05.200
is enough to get you
to come back again

01:44:05.200 --> 01:44:07.430
and again to trust the process.

01:44:07.430 --> 01:44:12.100
So I actually think the way
you described wrestling as it's

01:44:12.100 --> 01:44:14.480
the energy of the sport.

01:44:14.480 --> 01:44:17.560
It's not whether or not
it's this move or that move

01:44:17.560 --> 01:44:18.760
or who wins or who loses.

01:44:18.760 --> 01:44:21.430
It's the energy,
and I'm guessing

01:44:21.430 --> 01:44:25.180
it's the energy that it
creates in you as an observer.

01:44:25.180 --> 01:44:25.960
RICK RUBIN: Yes.

01:44:25.960 --> 01:44:29.800
It's the energy creates
in me, and the reality

01:44:29.800 --> 01:44:33.880
that it's honest in
what it is, in a world

01:44:33.880 --> 01:44:38.480
where seemingly nothing
is honest at what it is.

01:44:38.480 --> 01:44:43.240
And again, not because people
are lying all the time.

01:44:43.240 --> 01:44:46.393
We have little data, we make
up a story to explain it,

01:44:46.393 --> 01:44:47.935
and then we say
that's what happened.

01:44:50.910 --> 01:44:55.560
And we have trusted sources
who do exactly what I just

01:44:55.560 --> 01:45:05.040
described and who pass this
down as gospel of what we teach.

01:45:05.040 --> 01:45:07.750
And maybe it's true,
and maybe it's not.

01:45:07.750 --> 01:45:12.310
With wrestling, we know, maybe
it's true, maybe it's not.

01:45:12.310 --> 01:45:14.503
We lean towards
it not being true,

01:45:14.503 --> 01:45:16.420
but what's really
interesting about wrestling,

01:45:16.420 --> 01:45:18.337
and maybe one of the
most fun things about it,

01:45:18.337 --> 01:45:24.020
is that, sometimes, real life
works its way into the story.

01:45:24.020 --> 01:45:28.480
Like two wrestlers get married.

01:45:28.480 --> 01:45:29.200
Now--

01:45:29.200 --> 01:45:30.040
ANDREW HUBERMAN: In real life.

01:45:30.040 --> 01:45:31.373
RICK RUBIN: Well, we don't know.

01:45:31.373 --> 01:45:32.500
It's like you never know.

01:45:32.500 --> 01:45:34.870
It's like in the
storyline, they're

01:45:34.870 --> 01:45:36.940
getting married or
getting divorced,

01:45:36.940 --> 01:45:42.200
or best friends
turn on each other.

01:45:42.200 --> 01:45:45.550
And it could be
part of the story,

01:45:45.550 --> 01:45:48.580
and it could really be
happening, because they do.

01:45:48.580 --> 01:45:49.180
Right?

01:45:49.180 --> 01:45:51.890
Someone gets someone
breaks their leg.

01:45:51.890 --> 01:45:54.608
So they're out, because
their leg is broken.

01:45:54.608 --> 01:45:55.650
Did they break their leg?

01:45:55.650 --> 01:45:56.260
We don't know.

01:45:56.260 --> 01:45:57.550
They're out.

01:45:57.550 --> 01:45:58.870
Do you know what I'm saying?

01:45:58.870 --> 01:46:00.610
We're told they broke their leg.

01:46:00.610 --> 01:46:06.990
So there's always this
like I wonder what's true?

01:46:06.990 --> 01:46:08.700
I wonder where the line is.

01:46:08.700 --> 01:46:13.780
We know that it's scripted
and/or predetermined.

01:46:13.780 --> 01:46:16.230
That's how they say,
it's predetermined.

01:46:16.230 --> 01:46:23.770
But we don't know where reality
is and isn't, and in some ways,

01:46:23.770 --> 01:46:25.960
that's our real
experience of the world,

01:46:25.960 --> 01:46:30.430
is this we don't really know
where reality is and isn't.

01:46:30.430 --> 01:46:32.860
We have an idea, maybe.

01:46:32.860 --> 01:46:34.420
I think in some
ways, wrestling's

01:46:34.420 --> 01:46:37.870
more honest or legitimate,
because we start with the idea

01:46:37.870 --> 01:46:42.002
that it's fixed.

01:46:42.002 --> 01:46:44.460
When we go to a boxing match,
we don't go to a boxing match

01:46:44.460 --> 01:46:45.720
thinking it's fixed.

01:46:45.720 --> 01:46:50.550
Yet, it might be, and
historically, it's happened.

01:46:50.550 --> 01:46:53.460
Or there was just something
in baseball where--

01:46:53.460 --> 01:46:54.362
was it baseball?

01:46:54.362 --> 01:46:56.070
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
don't follow baseball.

01:46:56.070 --> 01:46:57.060
I should know.

01:46:57.060 --> 01:47:01.680
RICK RUBIN: It was
just a big sports--

01:47:01.680 --> 01:47:02.790
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Oh.

01:47:02.790 --> 01:47:03.810
RICK RUBIN: One of
the teams that--

01:47:03.810 --> 01:47:04.920
ANDREW HUBERMAN: The
plays, basically.

01:47:04.920 --> 01:47:05.790
RICK RUBIN: Yes.

01:47:05.790 --> 01:47:07.090
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Was it
the call signals of the--

01:47:07.090 --> 01:47:07.350
RICK RUBIN: Yes.

01:47:07.350 --> 01:47:08.683
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Of the catcher?

01:47:08.683 --> 01:47:09.540
RICK RUBIN: Yes.

01:47:09.540 --> 01:47:10.680
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

01:47:10.680 --> 01:47:13.680
You're not supposed
to deprogram the--

01:47:13.680 --> 01:47:17.080
or deconstruct the call
signals of the other team,

01:47:17.080 --> 01:47:19.147
and I guess maybe a team
got caught doing that.

01:47:19.147 --> 01:47:20.730
RICK RUBIN: Yes, and
the team that won

01:47:20.730 --> 01:47:24.210
whatever the World Series was.

01:47:24.210 --> 01:47:29.340
So it's like, with wrestling,
that wouldn't be a scandal.

01:47:29.340 --> 01:47:30.997
Do you know what I'm saying?

01:47:30.997 --> 01:47:32.580
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Almost anything goes.

01:47:32.580 --> 01:47:34.710
RICK RUBIN: Anything
goes, and that's

01:47:34.710 --> 01:47:36.300
what the world is really like.

01:47:36.300 --> 01:47:39.393
So in some ways,
it's comforting,

01:47:39.393 --> 01:47:41.310
and there's still this
mystery of like, wow, I

01:47:41.310 --> 01:47:43.990
wonder if that's true or not,
because we never really know.

01:47:43.990 --> 01:47:47.160
If someone gets hurt, did
they really break their back,

01:47:47.160 --> 01:47:48.990
or are they just
going on vacation?

01:47:48.990 --> 01:47:49.590
We don't know.

01:47:49.590 --> 01:47:50.730
We'll never know.

01:47:50.730 --> 01:47:51.780
It's fascinating.

01:47:51.780 --> 01:47:52.620
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
It is fascinating,

01:47:52.620 --> 01:47:54.787
and I feel like there are
certain people who show up

01:47:54.787 --> 01:47:58.890
in a way that is surprising
in not just one direction

01:47:58.890 --> 01:47:59.922
but in all directions.

01:47:59.922 --> 01:48:01.380
Like it's one thing
for a celebrity

01:48:01.380 --> 01:48:03.188
to come out and
make a statement.

01:48:03.188 --> 01:48:04.980
That can be interesting
or not interesting,

01:48:04.980 --> 01:48:08.702
depending on the celebrity and
the statement and the delivery.

01:48:08.702 --> 01:48:10.410
And I'm probably going
to get this wrong,

01:48:10.410 --> 01:48:14.430
because I'm terrible at pop
culture things, most of them

01:48:14.430 --> 01:48:14.940
anyway.

01:48:14.940 --> 01:48:17.400
But as I recall,
Lady Gaga showed up

01:48:17.400 --> 01:48:20.970
to some event wearing
an outfit made of meat,

01:48:20.970 --> 01:48:23.142
and I can't tell you
for the life of me

01:48:23.142 --> 01:48:24.600
whether or not that
was a statement

01:48:24.600 --> 01:48:26.518
against meat or for meat.

01:48:26.518 --> 01:48:28.560
Maybe, it was a statement
for the carnivore diet.

01:48:28.560 --> 01:48:30.185
Maybe, it was a
statement for veganism.

01:48:30.185 --> 01:48:31.432
I don't know.

01:48:31.432 --> 01:48:32.640
RICK RUBIN: Or maybe neither.

01:48:32.640 --> 01:48:33.540
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Or maybe neither,

01:48:33.540 --> 01:48:34.950
but it was definitely
a statement

01:48:34.950 --> 01:48:37.060
in that it broke with the norm.

01:48:37.060 --> 01:48:42.720
And it said to me, OK, she
creates different rules

01:48:42.720 --> 01:48:47.730
for herself or breaks boundaries
that other people had.

01:48:47.730 --> 01:48:50.287
I never heard of anyone
doing that before.

01:48:50.287 --> 01:48:52.620
It doesn't mean they hadn't,
but I never heard of anyone

01:48:52.620 --> 01:48:53.640
doing it before.

01:48:53.640 --> 01:49:00.970
But we do tend to associate
outside the current playbook

01:49:00.970 --> 01:49:03.430
with, quote,
unquote, creativity,

01:49:03.430 --> 01:49:07.790
unless it crosses a
line, in which case, it

01:49:07.790 --> 01:49:08.840
becomes something else.

01:49:08.840 --> 01:49:11.030
It becomes almost theater
for sake of theater.

01:49:11.030 --> 01:49:13.197
But what you're telling me
is that, within the realm

01:49:13.197 --> 01:49:17.280
of wrestling, theater is
the goal, at some level,

01:49:17.280 --> 01:49:20.010
and everybody knows it who
goes into those arenas, who

01:49:20.010 --> 01:49:20.520
watches it.

01:49:20.520 --> 01:49:21.210
RICK RUBIN: Yes.

01:49:21.210 --> 01:49:22.335
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Everybody.

01:49:22.335 --> 01:49:23.340
RICK RUBIN: Yes.

01:49:23.340 --> 01:49:24.840
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And everyone agrees

01:49:24.840 --> 01:49:27.690
to kind of suspend
outside reality

01:49:27.690 --> 01:49:29.340
and say this is reality.

01:49:29.340 --> 01:49:32.730
RICK RUBIN: Yes, and they boo
for the bad guys and cheer

01:49:32.730 --> 01:49:34.800
for the good guys,
knowing that backstage

01:49:34.800 --> 01:49:35.842
they're probably friends.

01:49:35.842 --> 01:49:37.342
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Except for the kids

01:49:37.342 --> 01:49:39.460
that are 11, who think
it's really real.

01:49:39.460 --> 01:49:39.960
RICK RUBIN: I don't know.

01:49:39.960 --> 01:49:41.252
I don't even know if they know.

01:49:41.252 --> 01:49:42.207
I'm not sure.

01:49:42.207 --> 01:49:43.790
ANDREW HUBERMAN: The
only other person

01:49:43.790 --> 01:49:49.460
I know who has vocalized their
love of professional wrestling

01:49:49.460 --> 01:49:52.190
to the extent that you have is
Lars Frederiksen, the rhythm

01:49:52.190 --> 01:49:54.840
guitar player for Rancid
who loves wrestling.

01:49:54.840 --> 01:49:57.260
But his statement--
and forgive me, Lars,

01:49:57.260 --> 01:49:59.240
if I'm getting this wrong--

01:49:59.240 --> 01:50:01.880
is that, because he grew up
in an area of the South Bay,

01:50:01.880 --> 01:50:03.170
where there were no teams.

01:50:03.170 --> 01:50:04.700
Like now, there's the
San Jose Earthquakes,

01:50:04.700 --> 01:50:06.380
but there was no football
team in San Jose.

01:50:06.380 --> 01:50:07.280
He's from Campbell.

01:50:07.280 --> 01:50:10.310
But there were no like good
teams, no sports teams,

01:50:10.310 --> 01:50:13.370
but they had wrestling,
and he had it where?

01:50:13.370 --> 01:50:15.180
On the television set.

01:50:15.180 --> 01:50:17.390
And so if you
didn't have a-- like

01:50:17.390 --> 01:50:20.570
I didn't grow up with any
organized sports thing.

01:50:20.570 --> 01:50:23.300
The 49ers were up the road, but
for me, it was skateboarding,

01:50:23.300 --> 01:50:25.700
and I love it for
the same reason.

01:50:25.700 --> 01:50:27.960
You actually never really
know what's going to happen.

01:50:27.960 --> 01:50:28.880
There is no rulebook.

01:50:28.880 --> 01:50:33.095
The rulebook is made
up, but they are very--

01:50:33.095 --> 01:50:35.000
it's a unique sport, in that--

01:50:35.000 --> 01:50:36.620
surfing's a bit like this too--

01:50:36.620 --> 01:50:41.250
in that, they are absolutely
maniacal about making

01:50:41.250 --> 01:50:42.760
things look a certain way.

01:50:42.760 --> 01:50:44.070
It's not about just doing it.

01:50:44.070 --> 01:50:46.572
It's about doing it and
making it look good.

01:50:46.572 --> 01:50:47.280
RICK RUBIN: Yeah.

01:50:47.280 --> 01:50:48.780
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Smooth, catching it

01:50:48.780 --> 01:50:51.348
with the front foot,
and the trends change.

01:50:51.348 --> 01:50:52.140
RICK RUBIN: Styles.

01:50:52.140 --> 01:50:52.710
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Style.

01:50:52.710 --> 01:50:53.580
RICK RUBIN: It's a style.

01:50:53.580 --> 01:50:55.260
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Style, and that style

01:50:55.260 --> 01:50:58.720
is this like nebulous thing of
like in fashion or in sports.

01:50:58.720 --> 01:50:59.220
Right.

01:50:59.220 --> 01:51:01.420
Whereas with a football,
there's some amazing catches.

01:51:01.420 --> 01:51:03.545
There's even like the catch,
which I happen to know

01:51:03.545 --> 01:51:06.630
is a 49er-- the catch
during the Super Bowl.

01:51:06.630 --> 01:51:09.900
But in general, it's like the
goal is get in the end zone,

01:51:09.900 --> 01:51:10.925
win the game.

01:51:10.925 --> 01:51:12.300
And I'm sure
football players are

01:51:12.300 --> 01:51:14.070
like cringing as
I say this, but it

01:51:14.070 --> 01:51:17.130
doesn't matter if you run
ugly, if you run fastest.

01:51:17.130 --> 01:51:19.020
In skateboarding,
that would never fly.

01:51:19.020 --> 01:51:23.310
In fact, you'd basically be
ridiculed out of the sport.

01:51:23.310 --> 01:51:25.500
In wrestling, is it the same?

01:51:25.500 --> 01:51:27.690
Are there style to wrestling?

01:51:27.690 --> 01:51:29.550
RICK RUBIN: It's
all performance.

01:51:29.550 --> 01:51:32.820
It's all the charisma
of the people involved.

01:51:32.820 --> 01:51:36.390
There's the physical
ability, the ability

01:51:36.390 --> 01:51:40.920
to talk and tell a story,
and how charismatic

01:51:40.920 --> 01:51:41.720
the performers are.

01:51:41.720 --> 01:51:44.220
Whether you want to watch them,
whether you want to see them

01:51:44.220 --> 01:51:46.500
win, whether you want
to see them lose,

01:51:46.500 --> 01:51:50.252
and whether you're interested
in cheering or booing for them.

01:51:50.252 --> 01:51:52.710
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I was going
to say it reminds me of opera,

01:51:52.710 --> 01:51:56.790
but operas get released
over and over again.

01:51:56.790 --> 01:51:58.440
You know the story
and how it ends

01:51:58.440 --> 01:52:01.050
when you walk in, if you've
listened to it before.

01:52:01.050 --> 01:52:04.260
So wrestling does seem
to be unique in that way.

01:52:04.260 --> 01:52:06.630
It's real time
iteration, at least

01:52:06.630 --> 01:52:08.473
from the perspective of the--

01:52:08.473 --> 01:52:10.140
RICK RUBIN: And it's
real time iteration

01:52:10.140 --> 01:52:12.510
based on because people
get hurt all the time.

01:52:12.510 --> 01:52:14.880
They're doing really
crazy physical stuff.

01:52:14.880 --> 01:52:19.430
So if someone gets hurt,
the story has to change.

01:52:19.430 --> 01:52:21.770
Because in real life, they
can't show up next week

01:52:21.770 --> 01:52:24.020
and do what was
planned in the script.

01:52:24.020 --> 01:52:29.390
So it's very alive,
and there's a lot of--

01:52:33.230 --> 01:52:37.250
something interesting and
unexpected is always happening.

01:52:37.250 --> 01:52:42.585
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well,
in a much more calm form,

01:52:42.585 --> 01:52:44.210
I'll share with you
something, I'd just

01:52:44.210 --> 01:52:45.377
like your perspective on it.

01:52:45.377 --> 01:52:52.520
For years, I used a tool in
order to try and access ideas,

01:52:52.520 --> 01:52:55.130
since I was a
little kid actually,

01:52:55.130 --> 01:52:57.282
because I have a
little bit of OCD,

01:52:57.282 --> 01:52:58.490
a little bit of a Tourette's.

01:52:58.490 --> 01:52:59.782
When I get tired, I'll do that.

01:52:59.782 --> 01:53:03.602
I'm very strategy
implementation oriented.

01:53:03.602 --> 01:53:05.060
I had that when I
was a little kid,

01:53:05.060 --> 01:53:07.477
I needed all my stuffed animals
arranged in a certain way.

01:53:07.477 --> 01:53:08.270
LEGOs had to be--

01:53:08.270 --> 01:53:10.790
a little neurotic or a lot.

01:53:10.790 --> 01:53:13.190
And then science is very much
about you have to do things

01:53:13.190 --> 01:53:14.600
with a lot of precision.

01:53:14.600 --> 01:53:17.600
And I discovered that
the ultimate reset for me

01:53:17.600 --> 01:53:20.090
when I was in graduate
school or a postdoc

01:53:20.090 --> 01:53:23.420
if I couldn't make it to a
really good agnostic front

01:53:23.420 --> 01:53:24.590
show, or like chaos.

01:53:24.590 --> 01:53:27.210
The chaos of a punk rock
show for me was this reset.

01:53:27.210 --> 01:53:29.330
It was to release
all this thing.

01:53:29.330 --> 01:53:31.100
And I got energy from it.

01:53:31.100 --> 01:53:33.890
First time I saw
Transplants play, it's was,

01:53:33.890 --> 01:53:36.140
whoa, because you don't
know what's going to happen.

01:53:36.140 --> 01:53:37.010
And it was scary.

01:53:37.010 --> 01:53:38.970
And I loved it.

01:53:38.970 --> 01:53:40.880
The other thing that
I used over time

01:53:40.880 --> 01:53:45.005
to reset this ability to
think in a structured way

01:53:45.005 --> 01:53:46.880
without it feeling like
it was overcoming me,

01:53:46.880 --> 01:53:49.040
maybe even access the
same thing in some ways

01:53:49.040 --> 01:53:50.540
that you're accessing
with wrestling

01:53:50.540 --> 01:53:53.210
was I like to stare at aquaria.

01:53:53.210 --> 01:53:55.370
I like to go to aquariums
or I'd build aquariums.

01:53:55.370 --> 01:53:56.720
And I would just sit
there because you never

01:53:56.720 --> 01:53:58.160
know which way the
fish are going to go.

01:53:58.160 --> 01:53:59.780
You think it's going that
way, but then all of a sudden

01:53:59.780 --> 01:54:01.197
they'll turn and
go the other way.

01:54:01.197 --> 01:54:02.750
It's completely unpredictable.

01:54:02.750 --> 01:54:04.970
And I love aquaria
because of the tranquility

01:54:04.970 --> 01:54:07.940
and had them in my
lab for a long time.

01:54:07.940 --> 01:54:12.440
I just adore aquariums because
of the non-linearity of it.

01:54:12.440 --> 01:54:17.425
It's not A, B, C.
It's A, Z, Z, Q.

01:54:17.425 --> 01:54:19.550
And I think this is what
some people try and access

01:54:19.550 --> 01:54:22.250
through psychedelics, but that
didn't seem to me like a very

01:54:22.250 --> 01:54:25.370
good way to do it
on a regular basis,

01:54:25.370 --> 01:54:28.530
whereas with aquaria,
the tanks are there.

01:54:28.530 --> 01:54:31.250
So in your book, you
talk about something

01:54:31.250 --> 01:54:33.350
that I also share
a love for, which

01:54:33.350 --> 01:54:40.130
is how the ocean and aspects of
nature like clouds and ocean,

01:54:40.130 --> 01:54:42.540
they have a
predictability to them.

01:54:42.540 --> 01:54:45.140
We know where they are
and where to find them.

01:54:45.140 --> 01:54:47.990
Fortunately, the sun rises
and sets every day, at least

01:54:47.990 --> 01:54:48.900
for now.

01:54:48.900 --> 01:54:53.840
And we can count on them
with 100% reliability.

01:54:53.840 --> 01:54:56.330
And yet, they are
from the perspective

01:54:56.330 --> 01:54:59.060
of what physicists would
say, they're very chaotic.

01:54:59.060 --> 01:55:01.360
You can't look at a wave
and know exactly how

01:55:01.360 --> 01:55:02.610
the foam is going to roll out.

01:55:02.610 --> 01:55:04.443
You know it's going to
roll in and roll out.

01:55:04.443 --> 01:55:07.580
We have the tides, but when
I hear about wrestling,

01:55:07.580 --> 01:55:09.230
I think about my
love of aquaria.

01:55:09.230 --> 01:55:13.460
And I think about my love of
punk rock music, for instance,

01:55:13.460 --> 01:55:15.820
or I think about the ocean.

01:55:15.820 --> 01:55:19.840
I think in that way that we
actually have a need to source

01:55:19.840 --> 01:55:23.530
from things that have both
a combination of structure

01:55:23.530 --> 01:55:24.910
and no structure.

01:55:24.910 --> 01:55:26.410
RICK RUBIN: I think
it's interesting

01:55:26.410 --> 01:55:29.020
that there are some
places that don't change

01:55:29.020 --> 01:55:31.240
and some places
that change a lot.

01:55:31.240 --> 01:55:33.670
And I can remember
thinking about this.

01:55:33.670 --> 01:55:35.620
I was walking-- there's
a beach that I walk on

01:55:35.620 --> 01:55:40.750
in Hawaii, that I walk on
every morning when I'm there.

01:55:40.750 --> 01:55:43.080
And if you walk on the
same beach every day,

01:55:43.080 --> 01:55:45.360
you get a sense
of what it's like.

01:55:45.360 --> 01:55:47.230
And I remember I was in Hawaii.

01:55:47.230 --> 01:55:48.980
I walked on the beach
every day for a year

01:55:48.980 --> 01:55:50.430
or however long it was.

01:55:50.430 --> 01:55:52.980
And then I left for six
months, then I came back.

01:55:52.980 --> 01:55:55.320
And the next time I
walked on the beach,

01:55:55.320 --> 01:56:02.320
it was an entirely different
beach, entirely different.

01:56:02.320 --> 01:56:04.060
And I remember
thinking in that moment

01:56:04.060 --> 01:56:08.860
like this is an unusual
place because I pictured

01:56:08.860 --> 01:56:12.970
the house that I
didn't even grow up in,

01:56:12.970 --> 01:56:18.250
the house I lived in
maybe for the first seven

01:56:18.250 --> 01:56:19.450
years of my life.

01:56:19.450 --> 01:56:22.000
And I think about what
the backyard looked like.

01:56:22.000 --> 01:56:24.880
And I think about a particular
old tree that was there.

01:56:24.880 --> 01:56:27.430
And I don't know this
for sure, but my sense

01:56:27.430 --> 01:56:30.250
is, if I were to go
back to where I grew up,

01:56:30.250 --> 01:56:34.330
and go to that place,
and look in that yard,

01:56:34.330 --> 01:56:36.520
it would probably
look pretty similar.

01:56:36.520 --> 01:56:38.470
Yet, here was this
beach that I was

01:56:38.470 --> 01:56:42.850
walking on in Hawaii that
in the course of six months

01:56:42.850 --> 01:56:45.220
completely changed its face.

01:56:45.220 --> 01:56:47.865
And just how interesting
both of those things are.

01:56:47.865 --> 01:56:49.240
And that depending
on the project

01:56:49.240 --> 01:56:52.570
we're working on, to be
able to go to a place

01:56:52.570 --> 01:56:56.050
that we know has the
potential to change a lot

01:56:56.050 --> 01:57:02.660
and what that would do to
our connection with the Earth

01:57:02.660 --> 01:57:07.600
when we're experiencing that
versus going to a place that

01:57:07.600 --> 01:57:09.340
has very little change.

01:57:09.340 --> 01:57:14.260
And you can count on it being
the way it's always been.

01:57:14.260 --> 01:57:16.030
That both of those
are interesting things

01:57:16.030 --> 01:57:19.270
to be able to draw
upon depending on what

01:57:19.270 --> 01:57:22.480
we want to open in our psyche.

01:57:22.480 --> 01:57:25.720
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I have an
almost unhealthy fascination

01:57:25.720 --> 01:57:29.160
with New York in the
mid '80s and '90s.

01:57:29.160 --> 01:57:30.910
RICK RUBIN: You didn't
live there, though.

01:57:30.910 --> 01:57:32.743
ANDREW HUBERMAN: No,
but since I was a kid--

01:57:32.743 --> 01:57:34.285
I went there when
I was a little kid.

01:57:34.285 --> 01:57:35.592
And I was fascinated by it.

01:57:35.592 --> 01:57:37.300
There's also a very
interesting migration

01:57:37.300 --> 01:57:40.240
of East Coast to
West Coast creatives,

01:57:40.240 --> 01:57:44.200
including yourself, that played
an important part in my life

01:57:44.200 --> 01:57:46.270
just seeing things
and hearing things

01:57:46.270 --> 01:57:47.440
that were meaningful to me.

01:57:47.440 --> 01:57:49.780
But I, for instance,
I love the movie--

01:57:49.780 --> 01:57:52.515
I haven't seen the documentary
about Jean-Michel Basquiat,

01:57:52.515 --> 01:57:54.640
Basquiat, because of the
characters that are in it,

01:57:54.640 --> 01:57:57.310
and the huge number of people in
that like Parker Posey, Dennis

01:57:57.310 --> 01:57:59.930
Hopper, and Christopher
Wall, and on, and on.

01:57:59.930 --> 01:58:02.560
Those images of New York at
that time are so exciting

01:58:02.560 --> 01:58:04.360
and what was happening.

01:58:04.360 --> 01:58:06.160
I wish I could transplant
myself to that.

01:58:06.160 --> 01:58:08.368
If I had a time machine,
that's where I'd land first.

01:58:10.740 --> 01:58:14.310
I hear a lot of people say, New
York isn't what it used to be.

01:58:14.310 --> 01:58:16.560
San Francisco isn't what
it used to be, whatever.

01:58:16.560 --> 01:58:18.960
LA isn't what-- there
does seem to be something

01:58:18.960 --> 01:58:20.970
that feels a little bit
disruptive to people

01:58:20.970 --> 01:58:22.830
about cities changing.

01:58:22.830 --> 01:58:27.840
But the idea that natural
landscapes change is actually--

01:58:27.840 --> 01:58:30.570
we even accept like, hey,
fires sweep through places.

01:58:30.570 --> 01:58:32.940
And assuming they weren't
started by humans,

01:58:32.940 --> 01:58:34.185
we accept that.

01:58:34.185 --> 01:58:36.930
That change and the
reordering of landscapes

01:58:36.930 --> 01:58:38.440
is normal and healthy.

01:58:38.440 --> 01:58:40.230
And I always tell
myself, you have

01:58:40.230 --> 01:58:42.780
the kids growing up in New York,
or San Francisco, or Chicago

01:58:42.780 --> 01:58:43.350
now.

01:58:43.350 --> 01:58:44.700
They only know it that way.

01:58:44.700 --> 01:58:46.500
So to them, it's as
cool or as uncool

01:58:46.500 --> 01:58:47.587
as it's ever going to be.

01:58:47.587 --> 01:58:49.170
They either want to
get out or they're

01:58:49.170 --> 01:58:50.730
loving every piece of it.

01:58:50.730 --> 01:58:53.380
And this happened for all the
people that came before us.

01:58:53.380 --> 01:58:56.820
So my question is
a very basic one.

01:58:56.820 --> 01:58:59.040
Do you miss the New York
that you came up in?

01:58:59.040 --> 01:59:01.140
Are you somebody who is
attached to the past?

01:59:01.140 --> 01:59:02.640
RICK RUBIN: I'm not
attached at all.

01:59:02.640 --> 01:59:04.860
I'm not attached to
anything in the past.

01:59:04.860 --> 01:59:06.270
I don't look back at all.

01:59:06.270 --> 01:59:08.728
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You don't
think about, oh, in my dorm room

01:59:08.728 --> 01:59:10.170
at NYU, Beastie Boys, this.

01:59:10.170 --> 01:59:10.890
I miss-- no.

01:59:10.890 --> 01:59:14.550
Your optics are forward,
present and forward.

01:59:14.550 --> 01:59:18.198
RICK RUBIN: Only
present and forward.

01:59:18.198 --> 01:59:19.990
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Is
there a process to that

01:59:19.990 --> 01:59:22.180
or it just happens to
be where you default to?

01:59:22.180 --> 01:59:23.222
RICK RUBIN: I don't know.

01:59:23.222 --> 01:59:25.570
I'm not sure, but
that's how I do it.

01:59:25.570 --> 01:59:29.890
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Nostalgia
is not in Rick Rubin's brain.

01:59:29.890 --> 01:59:30.567
RICK RUBIN: No.

01:59:30.567 --> 01:59:31.900
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Lucky you, man.

01:59:31.900 --> 01:59:32.740
RICK RUBIN: No.

01:59:32.740 --> 01:59:36.520
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I say that
with genuine admiration.

01:59:39.720 --> 01:59:42.660
So you can hear a
song that maybe you

01:59:42.660 --> 01:59:45.210
had a role in producing or
not, something from the past.

01:59:45.210 --> 01:59:49.380
And you're accessing
a state, presumably,

01:59:49.380 --> 01:59:51.630
but you're not pining
for wishing how it was.

01:59:51.630 --> 01:59:52.380
RICK RUBIN: Never.

01:59:54.588 --> 01:59:56.130
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
I'm no psychologist,

01:59:56.130 --> 01:59:57.963
but I'm going to venture
to say that I think

01:59:57.963 --> 01:59:59.880
that's a very unique quality.

01:59:59.880 --> 02:00:06.678
I think a lot of people wish
for or wish that things did not

02:00:06.678 --> 02:00:08.220
happen the way they
did, that there's

02:00:08.220 --> 02:00:09.510
a lot of living in the past.

02:00:09.510 --> 02:00:12.840
There's a lot of this notion
of people future trip.

02:00:12.840 --> 02:00:15.540
I don't actually think that's
the default state of the brain.

02:00:15.540 --> 02:00:18.330
I think a lot of people live in
emotional anchors to the past,

02:00:18.330 --> 02:00:19.290
good and bad.

02:00:19.290 --> 02:00:21.750
RICK RUBIN: Yeah.

02:00:21.750 --> 02:00:22.585
I have none.

02:00:22.585 --> 02:00:24.210
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
watching wrestling

02:00:24.210 --> 02:00:26.370
is one way that you
cleanse the palate.

02:00:26.370 --> 02:00:27.720
RICK RUBIN: Yeah.

02:00:27.720 --> 02:00:29.820
ANDREW HUBERMAN: When you
go to a meal and you--

02:00:29.820 --> 02:00:30.840
they pass around this or that--

02:00:30.840 --> 02:00:32.190
I don't know if they do
this anymore, but pass

02:00:32.190 --> 02:00:34.357
around a little bit of
sorbet to cleanse the palate.

02:00:34.357 --> 02:00:36.795
It turns out there's a
biological reason for that.

02:00:36.795 --> 02:00:38.670
There's a kind of
neutralization of the taste

02:00:38.670 --> 02:00:41.250
receptors between savory,
and sweet, et cetera.

02:00:41.250 --> 02:00:44.123
So wrestling is your
palate neutralizer.

02:00:44.123 --> 02:00:45.540
RICK RUBIN: I know
that if I watch

02:00:45.540 --> 02:00:47.040
wrestling before I
go to sleep, it's

02:00:47.040 --> 02:00:48.543
going to be a good
night's sleep.

02:00:48.543 --> 02:00:50.460
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Do you
dream about wrestling?

02:00:50.460 --> 02:00:53.490
RICK RUBIN: No, never.

02:00:53.490 --> 02:00:55.560
But it's just relaxing.

02:00:55.560 --> 02:00:59.633
It's just relaxing.

02:00:59.633 --> 02:01:01.050
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Do you anticipate

02:01:01.050 --> 02:01:03.360
when you watch it, like
here comes the dopamine hit?

02:01:03.360 --> 02:01:06.150
RICK RUBIN: Sometimes, sometimes
when it happens it's exciting.

02:01:06.150 --> 02:01:06.960
ANDREW HUBERMAN: He's going
up for the three-pointer.

02:01:06.960 --> 02:01:09.180
RICK RUBIN: Yeah,
sometimes it's exciting.

02:01:09.180 --> 02:01:09.990
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
But do you enjoy--

02:01:09.990 --> 02:01:12.240
RICK RUBIN: But even then,
it's like the stakes are low.

02:01:12.240 --> 02:01:14.782
It's like I don't really care
what happens, which feels good.

02:01:17.280 --> 02:01:20.082
I'm just being entertained.

02:01:20.082 --> 02:01:22.290
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Do they
actually get hurt sometimes?

02:01:22.290 --> 02:01:23.070
You said they do.

02:01:23.070 --> 02:01:24.400
RICK RUBIN: A lot, often.

02:01:24.400 --> 02:01:24.900
They do.

02:01:24.900 --> 02:01:26.430
I mean, they're
basically stunt men.

02:01:26.430 --> 02:01:29.580
So imagine stuntmen getting
hurt doing a crazy stunt.

02:01:29.580 --> 02:01:30.660
It happens all the time.

02:01:30.660 --> 02:01:31.650
ANDREW HUBERMAN: When
the movie The Wrestler,

02:01:31.650 --> 02:01:33.660
I remember he got
staples stapled into him.

02:01:33.660 --> 02:01:35.160
And I thought that's
pretty intense.

02:01:35.160 --> 02:01:36.872
I once went and saw--

02:01:36.872 --> 02:01:38.580
I guess they called
it Mexican wrestling.

02:01:38.580 --> 02:01:39.780
I don't know if they
call it that anymore,

02:01:39.780 --> 02:01:41.030
where the guys dip their hands--

02:01:41.030 --> 02:01:41.280
RICK RUBIN: Lucha libre.

02:01:41.280 --> 02:01:42.780
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, they
dip their hands in glass.

02:01:42.780 --> 02:01:43.870
This was in Sacramento.

02:01:43.870 --> 02:01:44.620
And I once saw it.

02:01:44.620 --> 02:01:46.770
I honestly didn't
have a stomach for it.

02:01:46.770 --> 02:01:47.850
I really didn't.

02:01:47.850 --> 02:01:49.200
I couldn't believe it was legal.

02:01:49.200 --> 02:01:50.460
It might not have been legal.

02:01:50.460 --> 02:01:51.060
RICK RUBIN: Yeah.

02:01:51.060 --> 02:01:51.960
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But I thought--

02:01:51.960 --> 02:01:54.540
RICK RUBIN: There's crazy
stuff in wrestling sometimes.

02:01:54.540 --> 02:01:56.290
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Wow.

02:01:56.290 --> 02:01:58.600
So before sleep,
is that typically

02:01:58.600 --> 02:01:59.710
when you watch wrestling?

02:01:59.710 --> 02:02:00.443
RICK RUBIN: Yes.

02:02:00.443 --> 02:02:02.610
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Do you
think it's useful for people

02:02:02.610 --> 02:02:07.650
to have some activity that
allows them to clear their mind

02:02:07.650 --> 02:02:09.872
and create peace before
heading off to sleep?

02:02:09.872 --> 02:02:10.830
RICK RUBIN: I think so.

02:02:10.830 --> 02:02:12.525
And I think yoga
nidras would be good.

02:02:12.525 --> 02:02:16.440
It's like yoga
nidra, pro wrestling,

02:02:16.440 --> 02:02:17.550
any of those type things.

02:02:17.550 --> 02:02:19.758
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, not
watching the Dahmer thing.

02:02:19.758 --> 02:02:21.482
I won't watch that.

02:02:21.482 --> 02:02:23.910
RICK RUBIN: I don't watch
any horror, anything,

02:02:23.910 --> 02:02:26.070
or I don't like violent things.

02:02:26.070 --> 02:02:27.820
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah,
I know it exists.

02:02:27.820 --> 02:02:29.340
I know horrible things
happen in the world,

02:02:29.340 --> 02:02:31.465
but I certainly don't want
to do that before sleep.

02:02:31.465 --> 02:02:35.130
I think these liminal states
before and emerging from sleep

02:02:35.130 --> 02:02:37.230
are very powerful.

02:02:37.230 --> 02:02:41.220
When you wake up in the
morning, are your thoughts

02:02:41.220 --> 02:02:43.140
immediately structured
or do you enjoy

02:02:43.140 --> 02:02:44.790
the clearing of the clouds?

02:02:44.790 --> 02:02:47.430
RICK RUBIN: It's a slow
process for me to wake up.

02:02:47.430 --> 02:02:48.270
And I like that.

02:02:48.270 --> 02:02:53.115
I like not engaging
too much too soon.

02:02:56.520 --> 02:03:00.510
I usually fall asleep listening
to a lecture or something

02:03:00.510 --> 02:03:05.100
speaking because
if I don't, I can

02:03:05.100 --> 02:03:07.200
get caught in my own thoughts.

02:03:07.200 --> 02:03:12.240
And listening to something
is enough of a focus point

02:03:12.240 --> 02:03:15.658
that it stops me from
talking to myself.

02:03:15.658 --> 02:03:16.950
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I do the same.

02:03:16.950 --> 02:03:21.550
My grandfather listened to the
radio, to sports on the radio.

02:03:21.550 --> 02:03:22.950
And he would fall asleep.

02:03:22.950 --> 02:03:25.800
Oftentimes, he was a smoker
with a cigarette in his mouth.

02:03:25.800 --> 02:03:27.720
His wife's responsibility
was to stay

02:03:27.720 --> 02:03:30.720
up later than he did to make
sure he didn't burn everything

02:03:30.720 --> 02:03:32.520
down.

02:03:32.520 --> 02:03:36.210
And then when you wake up,
you said it's a slow process.

02:03:36.210 --> 02:03:39.452
Is it an hour or two before
you feel like you're--

02:03:39.452 --> 02:03:41.160
RICK RUBIN: I would
say probably an hour.

02:03:41.160 --> 02:03:43.380
I usually wake up and
try to get in the sun

02:03:43.380 --> 02:03:48.590
as soon as I possibly
can and hope to spend--

02:03:48.590 --> 02:03:49.922
hope to spend about an hour.

02:03:49.922 --> 02:03:51.380
And then I'll
usually go for a walk

02:03:51.380 --> 02:03:57.460
on the beach for another hour
or 90 minutes, depending.

02:03:57.460 --> 02:03:59.660
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Are
you with family members

02:03:59.660 --> 02:04:00.910
and other people at that time?

02:04:00.910 --> 02:04:03.670
RICK RUBIN: No, I'm
usually focused by myself.

02:04:03.670 --> 02:04:04.788
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Phone?

02:04:04.788 --> 02:04:06.580
RICK RUBIN: I'll be
listening to something.

02:04:06.580 --> 02:04:10.660
I don't look at the phone, but
I listen to, again, a lecture,

02:04:10.660 --> 02:04:13.360
or podcast, or audiobook.

02:04:13.360 --> 02:04:14.560
I like audiobooks a lot.

02:04:14.560 --> 02:04:15.893
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, I do too.

02:04:15.893 --> 02:04:18.340
If an idea comes to mind
do you write it down?

02:04:18.340 --> 02:04:20.350
RICK RUBIN: I may, it depends.

02:04:20.350 --> 02:04:21.640
I like to.

02:04:21.640 --> 02:04:26.530
I usually have-- would
do a note in my phone.

02:04:26.530 --> 02:04:29.072
I don't usually carry pen and
paper with me when I'm walking.

02:04:29.072 --> 02:04:30.613
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Yeah, I do the same.

02:04:30.613 --> 02:04:32.440
I do a long Sunday hike or jog.

02:04:32.440 --> 02:04:35.980
And I will audio
script into my phone.

02:04:35.980 --> 02:04:37.540
People sometimes
give me funny looks

02:04:37.540 --> 02:04:38.830
because I'm talking to myself.

02:04:38.830 --> 02:04:40.788
RICK RUBIN: That's a nice
way to do it, though.

02:04:40.788 --> 02:04:44.260
I'd like to learn more of
the audio methods of doing it

02:04:44.260 --> 02:04:45.880
instead of the typing methods.

02:04:45.880 --> 02:04:48.310
Right now I type and I don't
think it's the best way.

02:04:48.310 --> 02:04:52.750
ANDREW HUBERMAN: The voice
memos function in the iPhone

02:04:52.750 --> 02:04:54.430
and other phones is really good.

02:04:54.430 --> 02:04:58.300
And there are now
companies like rev.com

02:04:58.300 --> 02:05:01.270
that will turn those
into Word docs scripts

02:05:01.270 --> 02:05:06.432
that are fairly well
corrected, fairly inexpensive.

02:05:06.432 --> 02:05:08.140
No, they're not a
sponsor of the podcast.

02:05:08.140 --> 02:05:08.980
I just happen to use it.

02:05:08.980 --> 02:05:09.480
It's great.

02:05:09.480 --> 02:05:12.400
I actually learned that
trick from Richard Axel,

02:05:12.400 --> 02:05:18.250
the Nicorette chewing, wild
man, Nobel Prize winner.

02:05:18.250 --> 02:05:22.510
He writes manuscripts by walking
around his office, pacing,

02:05:22.510 --> 02:05:23.865
and talking into his phone.

02:05:23.865 --> 02:05:25.990
RICK RUBIN: I always think
of the Woody Allen movie

02:05:25.990 --> 02:05:33.070
where the Alan Alda character
is talking about-- yeah,

02:05:33.070 --> 02:05:35.470
he's speaking comedy
ideas into the phone.

02:05:35.470 --> 02:05:36.760
It's really pretentious.

02:05:36.760 --> 02:05:38.470
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah,
I liked that movie

02:05:38.470 --> 02:05:41.650
about Harvey Milk, that Sean
Penn played Harvey Milk,

02:05:41.650 --> 02:05:43.810
because that all took
place before I was alive,

02:05:43.810 --> 02:05:44.920
mostly, in the Bay Area.

02:05:44.920 --> 02:05:47.480
But there's these
beautiful scenes of him,

02:05:47.480 --> 02:05:49.960
as I recall, sitting there
at his kitchen table talking

02:05:49.960 --> 02:05:51.970
into a tape recorder
at night, talking

02:05:51.970 --> 02:05:55.060
about how he predicted that he
would be possibly assassinated,

02:05:55.060 --> 02:05:55.780
et cetera.

02:05:55.780 --> 02:05:58.000
And this goes back
to the Strummer thing

02:05:58.000 --> 02:06:00.430
about writing things down.

02:06:00.430 --> 02:06:02.830
I think that a lot of
people, including myself

02:06:02.830 --> 02:06:06.850
feel a little bit of
egotistical guilt around,

02:06:06.850 --> 02:06:08.920
who am I to think
that my ideas could

02:06:08.920 --> 02:06:10.360
be worthwhile or something?

02:06:10.360 --> 02:06:14.470
But I think over time,
I've come to realize

02:06:14.470 --> 02:06:17.080
that the ideas about
experiments or questions

02:06:17.080 --> 02:06:21.350
I have about health,
they don't always,

02:06:21.350 --> 02:06:25.060
but oftentimes can lead to real
seeds that grow into big trees.

02:06:25.060 --> 02:06:28.420
RICK RUBIN: But it's something
that's interesting to you,

02:06:28.420 --> 02:06:31.630
it doesn't matter what
anyone else thinks.

02:06:31.630 --> 02:06:34.330
Most of my notes are
not for anyone else's--

02:06:34.330 --> 02:06:35.710
for anyone else's used.

02:06:35.710 --> 02:06:38.890
Like I hear about something
that's interesting to me

02:06:38.890 --> 02:06:40.750
and I think about, OK,
I want to learn more

02:06:40.750 --> 02:06:42.100
about this, whatever it is.

02:06:42.100 --> 02:06:44.470
And then, sometimes those
things work their way

02:06:44.470 --> 02:06:47.020
into things I'm doing
because the universe seems

02:06:47.020 --> 02:06:50.860
to work in that way, but I
rarely am learning something

02:06:50.860 --> 02:06:54.010
with the idea of using it.

02:06:54.010 --> 02:06:57.940
I learn things with the idea
of, this is what I want to know.

02:06:57.940 --> 02:06:59.960
This is what's
interesting to me.

02:06:59.960 --> 02:07:03.130
And then often those things
that are interesting to me

02:07:03.130 --> 02:07:08.052
can find their way into other
projects just because they do.

02:07:08.052 --> 02:07:10.510
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, that's
almost like coal or kindling,

02:07:10.510 --> 02:07:14.590
but the moment that you
think of it that way,

02:07:14.590 --> 02:07:17.680
it sounds so extractive.

02:07:17.680 --> 02:07:21.850
So you take this walk
and you're writing down

02:07:21.850 --> 02:07:24.340
the occasional idea, perhaps.

02:07:24.340 --> 02:07:27.895
And then what is the next--

02:07:27.895 --> 02:07:30.190
the way that--
here are less than,

02:07:30.190 --> 02:07:31.690
do this, then this, then that.

02:07:31.690 --> 02:07:34.107
I'm interested in, where
does your mind shift to?

02:07:34.107 --> 02:07:36.190
Does it become more
structured as the day goes on?

02:07:36.190 --> 02:07:38.410
Does your thinking
become more structured

02:07:38.410 --> 02:07:39.820
around projects and plans?

02:07:39.820 --> 02:07:43.280
RICK RUBIN: I try
to deal with things

02:07:43.280 --> 02:07:45.770
that need dealing
with after that

02:07:45.770 --> 02:07:49.500
and in preparation
for going to work.

02:07:49.500 --> 02:07:54.110
And then when I go to
work, it's more like free--

02:07:54.110 --> 02:07:57.260
this free thing where I'm,
again, hoping something good

02:07:57.260 --> 02:08:03.800
comes, welcoming something good,
paying attention, and maybe

02:08:03.800 --> 02:08:08.910
trying to will it to
happen, but knowing I

02:08:08.910 --> 02:08:10.920
don't have the ability
to make it happen.

02:08:10.920 --> 02:08:16.450
I can just be present for it
and be ready if it does arrive.

02:08:16.450 --> 02:08:20.140
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Some of the
more surprising and I found

02:08:20.140 --> 02:08:23.560
really interesting and
useful features of the book

02:08:23.560 --> 02:08:28.690
were about dancing
with structure and lack

02:08:28.690 --> 02:08:29.600
of structure.

02:08:29.600 --> 02:08:32.290
So when I think of structure,
I think of deadlines.

02:08:32.290 --> 02:08:36.910
So when you are in the
process of creating something,

02:08:36.910 --> 02:08:40.738
obviously, deadlines are
relevant, time of day.

02:08:40.738 --> 02:08:42.280
There's only so many
hours in the day

02:08:42.280 --> 02:08:46.105
where one can stay in the groove
or the readiness to receive.

02:08:49.100 --> 02:08:51.140
Have you ever found
yourself in that mode

02:08:51.140 --> 02:08:54.000
where you're grinding
like, oh, here we are.

02:08:54.000 --> 02:08:55.700
OK, I'm not coming
home for dinner.

02:08:55.700 --> 02:09:00.200
It's the next-- we're going
to push, put on the coffee pot

02:09:00.200 --> 02:09:00.920
kind of thing.

02:09:00.920 --> 02:09:01.670
RICK RUBIN: A lot.

02:09:01.670 --> 02:09:02.360
A lot.

02:09:02.360 --> 02:09:07.730
Over the course of my life,
a lot, not as much now.

02:09:07.730 --> 02:09:10.190
And one of the things
that I discovered

02:09:10.190 --> 02:09:16.697
through working on the book
was the phases of work--

02:09:16.697 --> 02:09:19.030
we're not required to treat
the different phases of work

02:09:19.030 --> 02:09:22.270
in the same way,
whereas before, I did.

02:09:22.270 --> 02:09:26.410
Before, everything was
in this state of play.

02:09:26.410 --> 02:09:31.300
Everything had a wide
open time schedule.

02:09:31.300 --> 02:09:34.150
It happens when it happens.

02:09:34.150 --> 02:09:38.560
And if it takes two years or
three years, it doesn't matter.

02:09:38.560 --> 02:09:39.430
It's not about that.

02:09:39.430 --> 02:09:41.770
It's only about this
thing has to be great.

02:09:41.770 --> 02:09:43.890
And what I came to realize
in working on the book

02:09:43.890 --> 02:09:46.170
is that there are
different phases.

02:09:46.170 --> 02:09:49.240
And the first phase is
this seed collecting phase,

02:09:49.240 --> 02:09:52.830
which is an ongoing
part of life in general.

02:09:52.830 --> 02:09:53.700
I do that.

02:09:53.700 --> 02:09:59.130
I do that always whether I'm
working on something or not.

02:09:59.130 --> 02:10:01.020
I'm always in the
seed collecting phase.

02:10:01.020 --> 02:10:05.100
And there's no deadline or
just anything that interests

02:10:05.100 --> 02:10:08.590
me that I think I want
to learn more about

02:10:08.590 --> 02:10:12.070
or has potential to be
something, anything.

02:10:12.070 --> 02:10:14.560
I hear something,
I think, uhm, I'd

02:10:14.560 --> 02:10:16.560
like to read more
about that, or I wonder

02:10:16.560 --> 02:10:17.810
if there's a movie about that.

02:10:17.810 --> 02:10:18.977
Is there a movie about that?

02:10:18.977 --> 02:10:21.630
If not, maybe there's
a movie to be made.

02:10:21.630 --> 02:10:25.120
Again, I want-- this is
something I want in my life.

02:10:25.120 --> 02:10:26.270
Let's see if it exists.

02:10:26.270 --> 02:10:27.910
If it doesn't exist,
then maybe that's

02:10:27.910 --> 02:10:30.070
something interesting to pursue.

02:10:30.070 --> 02:10:34.070
But I know that the desire
is there because I have it.

02:10:34.070 --> 02:10:36.970
So in the seed phase,
there's no deadlines.

02:10:36.970 --> 02:10:39.340
It's just a wide open part.

02:10:39.340 --> 02:10:42.070
And then the next phase is
called the experimentation

02:10:42.070 --> 02:10:45.790
phase, where we start
experimenting to see

02:10:45.790 --> 02:10:48.900
what the seeds want to do.

02:10:48.900 --> 02:10:51.810
You're involved but
you're more of a--

02:10:55.370 --> 02:10:58.400
you're not really
dictating the action.

02:10:58.400 --> 02:11:02.720
You're setting the stage
for something to happen,

02:11:02.720 --> 02:11:06.380
but it's not about you yet.

02:11:06.380 --> 02:11:10.160
So it'd be like the equivalent
of you'd plant the seed.

02:11:10.160 --> 02:11:11.690
You'd would water it.

02:11:11.690 --> 02:11:13.790
You'd make sure
it was in the sun.

02:11:13.790 --> 02:11:16.060
And you'd wait.

02:11:16.060 --> 02:11:20.320
So you're involved, but
you can't make it grow.

02:11:20.320 --> 02:11:22.720
And then when it
sprouts, and it grows,

02:11:22.720 --> 02:11:26.780
or if it turns into a plant,
then you can look at the plants

02:11:26.780 --> 02:11:29.460
like, OK, how does this plant--

02:11:29.460 --> 02:11:32.583
what's the potential
of this plant?

02:11:32.583 --> 02:11:35.000
And then that the third phase
is the crafting phase, where

02:11:35.000 --> 02:11:37.190
it's like, OK, I
have this plant,

02:11:37.190 --> 02:11:39.200
maybe I'm going to
trim it or maybe I'm

02:11:39.200 --> 02:11:42.350
going to combine it
with these other plants

02:11:42.350 --> 02:11:43.880
to make something else with it.

02:11:43.880 --> 02:11:46.650
Now, it's like
material that you have.

02:11:46.650 --> 02:11:51.210
And then finally is the
completion or finishing phase,

02:11:51.210 --> 02:11:55.972
which is the final edit,
getting to the version of it--

02:11:55.972 --> 02:11:57.680
the version of it
that's the one that you

02:11:57.680 --> 02:11:59.270
can share with the
world if that's

02:11:59.270 --> 02:12:01.490
something you're going to do.

02:12:01.490 --> 02:12:05.060
And I've come to
realize that by the time

02:12:05.060 --> 02:12:10.130
you're going into the completion
phase, you can have a deadline.

02:12:10.130 --> 02:12:12.330
And it won't hurt the project.

02:12:12.330 --> 02:12:14.180
In fact, it might
help the project.

02:12:14.180 --> 02:12:15.620
And I didn't know that before.

02:12:15.620 --> 02:12:18.650
So I've worked on projects
that have gone longer than they

02:12:18.650 --> 02:12:23.000
necessarily needed
to and maybe not

02:12:23.000 --> 02:12:24.500
in the best interests
of the project

02:12:24.500 --> 02:12:25.625
because I didn't know that.

02:12:25.625 --> 02:12:28.460
I didn't understand
the timing of that

02:12:28.460 --> 02:12:34.940
because I am so aware
of the necessity

02:12:34.940 --> 02:12:41.620
in the experimental phase to not
have a deadline that I assumed

02:12:41.620 --> 02:12:44.060
that held through
the whole project.

02:12:47.360 --> 02:12:50.532
And they're not-- it's not
a clear phase one finishes,

02:12:50.532 --> 02:12:51.740
and then you start phase two.

02:12:51.740 --> 02:12:54.020
Phase two finishes and
you start phase three.

02:12:54.020 --> 02:12:55.940
You move back and
forth between them.

02:12:55.940 --> 02:12:57.450
I'm collecting
seeds all the time.

02:12:57.450 --> 02:12:58.970
I'm always in phase one.

02:12:58.970 --> 02:13:02.460
And then, probably
to some degree,

02:13:02.460 --> 02:13:05.790
there's always some version of
experimentation going on, maybe

02:13:05.790 --> 02:13:08.370
not now, but if something's
on a list of things

02:13:08.370 --> 02:13:11.520
I want to look at, hopefully,
I'll get to the list

02:13:11.520 --> 02:13:13.440
and give them some
experimentation

02:13:13.440 --> 02:13:15.240
and see what they can turn into.

02:13:15.240 --> 02:13:17.910
And then if they do
turn into something,

02:13:17.910 --> 02:13:21.060
then they get to the crafting
phase where it's more, OK,

02:13:21.060 --> 02:13:22.930
now I have this thing.

02:13:22.930 --> 02:13:25.530
What do I know about this thing?

02:13:25.530 --> 02:13:27.270
What can I match this with?

02:13:27.270 --> 02:13:28.620
What can I use this for?

02:13:28.620 --> 02:13:31.500
How can I be involved
as a craftsman?

02:13:31.500 --> 02:13:34.530
And by the end of
the crafting phase

02:13:34.530 --> 02:13:40.110
or deep into the crafting phase,
you can start seeing the end.

02:13:40.110 --> 02:13:41.700
You can start seeing an end.

02:13:41.700 --> 02:13:43.950
And then you can
even dictate an end.

02:13:43.950 --> 02:13:46.140
But I recommend, if
you do, just dictate it

02:13:46.140 --> 02:13:49.500
for you not for anyone else
because if something comes up

02:13:49.500 --> 02:13:57.090
where you learn-- if you set
a deadline, public deadline,

02:13:57.090 --> 02:13:59.143
and then a new discovery
happens along the way.

02:13:59.143 --> 02:14:00.810
And you realize, oh,
this could actually

02:14:00.810 --> 02:14:03.870
be much better than I
thought, but I need more time,

02:14:03.870 --> 02:14:06.390
it's harder to do that
if you set the deadline.

02:14:06.390 --> 02:14:11.040
So I would say, have an internal
deadline to get to finish it.

02:14:11.040 --> 02:14:14.820
That said, if an unusual
situation comes up

02:14:14.820 --> 02:14:19.190
and it's better for everything
not to meet that deadline,

02:14:19.190 --> 02:14:21.380
it's one of those rules that--

02:14:21.380 --> 02:14:23.360
you set the rule
to break it if it's

02:14:23.360 --> 02:14:26.000
what's best for the project.

02:14:26.000 --> 02:14:27.770
But that was a new thing for me.

02:14:27.770 --> 02:14:30.650
And it helped me a lot.

02:14:30.650 --> 02:14:32.985
ANDREW HUBERMAN: When
did you realize that?

02:14:32.985 --> 02:14:35.110
RICK RUBIN: In collecting
the material for the book

02:14:35.110 --> 02:14:36.027
and thinking about it.

02:14:36.027 --> 02:14:39.130
When I realized
that it was phases--

02:14:39.130 --> 02:14:40.650
I didn't know any of this.

02:14:40.650 --> 02:14:42.760
When I started
writing the book, I

02:14:42.760 --> 02:14:45.745
didn't know hardly any of
the things in the book.

02:14:48.412 --> 02:14:52.990
Most of it would be reverse
engineering something

02:14:52.990 --> 02:14:57.820
that I had experienced,
a successful experience

02:14:57.820 --> 02:15:00.520
using these methods without
knowing they were methods, just

02:15:00.520 --> 02:15:04.790
following my instincts
got me to something good.

02:15:04.790 --> 02:15:10.690
And then I would look back
at, why did I want to do that?

02:15:10.690 --> 02:15:14.740
And is there a principle
at play that could

02:15:14.740 --> 02:15:18.640
be of use outside of this case?

02:15:18.640 --> 02:15:21.040
And how do I explain that?

02:15:21.040 --> 02:15:22.720
And that's what
the book is, these

02:15:22.720 --> 02:15:25.630
reverse engineered
principles that

02:15:25.630 --> 02:15:32.590
have led to a good decision
making and try and make things.

02:15:32.590 --> 02:15:34.910
ANDREW HUBERMAN: The
chapter on self-doubt

02:15:34.910 --> 02:15:37.143
was really interesting to me.

02:15:37.143 --> 02:15:39.560
RICK RUBIN: Tell me what it
says because I can't remember.

02:15:39.560 --> 02:15:43.350
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well, I'll
read the first sentence of it,

02:15:43.350 --> 02:15:45.720
which is that self-doubt
lives in all of us.

02:15:45.720 --> 02:15:49.220
And while we may wish it was
gone, it is there to serve us.

02:15:49.220 --> 02:15:53.900
And it goes on to describe
how to dance with self-doubt

02:15:53.900 --> 02:15:56.430
in not so many words.

02:15:56.430 --> 02:16:00.170
I think there's a
saying that is actually

02:16:00.170 --> 02:16:05.180
from the landscape of
psychology, which is generally

02:16:05.180 --> 02:16:11.240
discussed in a pathological
context, which is, if nothing

02:16:11.240 --> 02:16:12.740
matters, anything goes.

02:16:12.740 --> 02:16:15.110
This is usually the phrase
used to describe people

02:16:15.110 --> 02:16:18.360
who feel as if there's
no use in living,

02:16:18.360 --> 02:16:22.550
so just go crazy,
often to self destruct.

02:16:22.550 --> 02:16:26.060
But there's a light
version of this, I realize,

02:16:26.060 --> 02:16:29.600
where in some sense the
creative process seems

02:16:29.600 --> 02:16:31.160
to have something to do with--

02:16:31.160 --> 02:16:32.900
if you're not paying
attention to what

02:16:32.900 --> 02:16:35.750
outcomes are like who likes
it, who doesn't like it,

02:16:35.750 --> 02:16:39.290
and you're just doing it for
you, you make the rule play,

02:16:39.290 --> 02:16:41.570
I want to delight myself.

02:16:41.570 --> 02:16:44.030
Well then, anything
goes and you have

02:16:44.030 --> 02:16:46.309
an infinite rule set
there to extract from,

02:16:46.309 --> 02:16:47.370
at least initially.

02:16:47.370 --> 02:16:52.600
So as one gets better
at their craft,

02:16:52.600 --> 02:16:54.400
you can imagine
self-doubt goes down.

02:16:54.400 --> 02:16:56.860
I think that's the perception
of a lot of people.

02:16:56.860 --> 02:16:58.360
You get better at
what you're doing.

02:16:58.360 --> 02:17:02.209
You can land free throws
as a basketball player.

02:17:02.209 --> 02:17:04.209
You can hit more home
runs as a baseball player.

02:17:04.209 --> 02:17:08.350
You can produce more
platinum albums as an artist.

02:17:08.350 --> 02:17:11.260
Self confidence goes up.

02:17:11.260 --> 02:17:12.190
Self-doubt goes down.

02:17:12.190 --> 02:17:14.032
But I think you and
I both know a number

02:17:14.032 --> 02:17:15.490
of people who are
successful enough

02:17:15.490 --> 02:17:19.030
to know that oftentimes there's
a mirror image to that, where

02:17:19.030 --> 02:17:21.430
people feel pressure
because they did it once now

02:17:21.430 --> 02:17:22.570
they got to do it again.

02:17:22.570 --> 02:17:31.250
RICK RUBIN: Yes or that you
think you're so good at it

02:17:31.250 --> 02:17:34.415
that it comes easily and you
don't have to apply yourself.

02:17:34.415 --> 02:17:35.540
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Arrogance.

02:17:35.540 --> 02:17:36.350
RICK RUBIN: Yeah.

02:17:36.350 --> 02:17:41.498
So self-doubt it's like a--

02:17:41.498 --> 02:17:43.549
it's a check on yourself.

02:17:43.549 --> 02:17:47.420
It can either be really helpful
or it can undermine you.

02:17:47.420 --> 02:17:49.850
So it's something we all have.

02:17:49.850 --> 02:17:52.980
And if we let it undermine us,
then we don't make anything

02:17:52.980 --> 02:17:53.990
and that's not good.

02:17:56.730 --> 02:18:02.280
But when used as a
balancing tool in our lives,

02:18:02.280 --> 02:18:05.370
it serves a great function
where we really do--

02:18:05.370 --> 02:18:07.889
it's OK to have
all the confidence

02:18:07.889 --> 02:18:12.120
in the world and
still second guess,

02:18:12.120 --> 02:18:14.465
is this the best it can be?

02:18:14.465 --> 02:18:15.510
You can doubt--

02:18:15.510 --> 02:18:17.219
I think the phrase
is in the book.

02:18:17.219 --> 02:18:20.639
You can doubt your way to a
great work, to a masterpiece.

02:18:23.559 --> 02:18:27.490
Sometimes that
questioning allows

02:18:27.490 --> 02:18:30.190
you to push further
than just accepting,

02:18:30.190 --> 02:18:32.820
I made it so it's good.

02:18:32.820 --> 02:18:35.219
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, I've
encountered more people that

02:18:35.219 --> 02:18:39.600
seem to be driven by
self-doubt and the need

02:18:39.600 --> 02:18:42.209
to constantly perform
and perform again,

02:18:42.209 --> 02:18:44.160
then I have real arrogance.

02:18:44.160 --> 02:18:46.889
Just hasn't been my
experience, fortunately.

02:18:46.889 --> 02:18:49.770
I've met some arrogant
people in my life.

02:18:49.770 --> 02:18:51.030
And of course, we never--

02:18:51.030 --> 02:18:54.840
as a psychiatrist who I admire
a lot and bio-engineer who

02:18:54.840 --> 02:18:57.480
was a guest on this
podcast Carl Di Saraf said,

02:18:57.480 --> 02:19:00.924
we never really know
how other people feel.

02:19:00.924 --> 02:19:03.299
I mean, most of the time, we
don't even know how we feel.

02:19:03.299 --> 02:19:05.910
Again, language is a
very deprived format

02:19:05.910 --> 02:19:07.080
for explaining feelings.

02:19:07.080 --> 02:19:09.690
So we think somebody feels
one way but we can observe--

02:19:09.690 --> 02:19:12.549
and it could be another, but
we observe their behavior.

02:19:12.549 --> 02:19:15.240
So in the sense of
returning to the work,

02:19:15.240 --> 02:19:17.580
just always
returning to process,

02:19:17.580 --> 02:19:21.420
it sounds like your routine is
fairly scripted, at least now.

02:19:21.420 --> 02:19:23.610
But the things that
you are getting

02:19:23.610 --> 02:19:29.790
in touch with-- wrestling,
sleep and dreaming, the ocean,

02:19:29.790 --> 02:19:32.910
there's a predictability of
them because you can access them

02:19:32.910 --> 02:19:35.280
in a predictable
way, but they seem

02:19:35.280 --> 02:19:37.950
to have a lot of
unpredictability in them.

02:19:37.950 --> 02:19:39.957
The ocean is completely
unpredictable.

02:19:39.957 --> 02:19:42.540
RICK RUBIN: I also listen to a
lot of music that I don't know.

02:19:42.540 --> 02:19:49.730
So I listen to a lot of
classical music, and less so

02:19:49.730 --> 02:19:54.290
but some jazz, and
a lot of old music

02:19:54.290 --> 02:19:56.210
that I never heard before.

02:19:56.210 --> 02:19:59.420
And I like being
surprised by music.

02:20:02.760 --> 02:20:05.250
And sometimes it really
catches me off guard.

02:20:05.250 --> 02:20:09.110
I shazam a lot when I
hear something I like.

02:20:09.110 --> 02:20:10.860
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Have
you ever encountered

02:20:10.860 --> 02:20:13.530
music that really
works well live

02:20:13.530 --> 02:20:16.350
but just does not
work in a recording?

02:20:16.350 --> 02:20:19.320
Or that it's much better
live but the recording is

02:20:19.320 --> 02:20:20.460
sort of, meh.

02:20:20.460 --> 02:20:22.000
You don't have to name names.

02:20:22.000 --> 02:20:23.208
RICK RUBIN: I don't think so.

02:20:23.208 --> 02:20:26.100
I feel like maybe there
are some artists who

02:20:26.100 --> 02:20:30.000
are great live who've never
captured it well on record.

02:20:30.000 --> 02:20:32.435
Example would probably
be the Grateful Dead

02:20:32.435 --> 02:20:33.810
is a good example
of a band where

02:20:33.810 --> 02:20:37.050
I feel like their albums
are not their strong point.

02:20:37.050 --> 02:20:39.660
But if you hear live
recordings, they're

02:20:39.660 --> 02:20:42.150
really interesting and really
different from each other.

02:20:42.150 --> 02:20:45.480
And that's part of what makes
the Grateful Dead interesting

02:20:45.480 --> 02:20:47.745
is their unpredictability.

02:20:47.745 --> 02:20:48.870
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I confess.

02:20:48.870 --> 02:20:50.790
I had a sister who listened
to the Grateful Dead.

02:20:50.790 --> 02:20:52.950
And I got taken to a few
shows when I was younger.

02:20:52.950 --> 02:20:55.200
And they would do that--
was it called space?

02:20:55.200 --> 02:20:57.660
It was these drum
solos that would go on

02:20:57.660 --> 02:20:58.560
for hours and hours.

02:20:58.560 --> 02:21:00.518
This is like the antithesis
of punk rock shows,

02:21:00.518 --> 02:21:03.090
where songs are like
90 to 120 seconds.

02:21:03.090 --> 02:21:06.690
And I remember
thinking, what is this?

02:21:06.690 --> 02:21:07.590
What is this?

02:21:07.590 --> 02:21:12.000
But people I know, who
love the Grateful Dead,

02:21:12.000 --> 02:21:16.440
love that uncertainty about
where that drum thing--

02:21:16.440 --> 02:21:17.790
I think they do call it space.

02:21:17.790 --> 02:21:18.960
Forgive me deadheads.

02:21:18.960 --> 02:21:22.020
I'm not enough of
one to get it right.

02:21:22.020 --> 02:21:24.150
RICK RUBIN: But they're
looking for something.

02:21:24.150 --> 02:21:26.050
And sometimes they find it.

02:21:26.050 --> 02:21:27.930
And if you're there
when they find it,

02:21:27.930 --> 02:21:29.940
it feels exciting
because it's not just--

02:21:32.750 --> 02:21:34.640
it's not just
following a script.

02:21:34.640 --> 02:21:37.290
It's like something
is really happening.

02:21:37.290 --> 02:21:39.920
It's a real moment.

02:21:39.920 --> 02:21:46.120
Something that I aim
for in the studio

02:21:46.120 --> 02:21:50.660
is to create real moments
that when you hear them,

02:21:50.660 --> 02:21:53.500
they don't necessarily
sound perfect.

02:21:53.500 --> 02:21:56.090
They sound like something
that really happened.

02:21:56.090 --> 02:21:58.300
And in that moment,
something happened.

02:21:58.300 --> 02:22:00.130
And it's a special moment.

02:22:00.130 --> 02:22:07.470
And you can feel that if
they were to play it again,

02:22:07.470 --> 02:22:09.000
it wouldn't be like that.

02:22:09.000 --> 02:22:11.100
There's something really
exciting about that.

02:22:11.100 --> 02:22:15.670
It's really what-- it's
how jazz works as well.

02:22:15.670 --> 02:22:21.060
And I think some of-- bringing
some of that jazz mentality

02:22:21.060 --> 02:22:26.040
into other types of music
is really interesting,

02:22:26.040 --> 02:22:28.830
makes for compelling things,
because when you hear them,

02:22:28.830 --> 02:22:33.725
there's a certain amount of--

02:22:33.725 --> 02:22:36.890
you really have to pay
attention to do it.

02:22:36.890 --> 02:22:39.470
When you're doing it, you're
really paying attention.

02:22:39.470 --> 02:22:41.180
It's like, I don't really know.

02:22:41.180 --> 02:22:41.905
There's no music.

02:22:44.540 --> 02:22:46.520
There's no map to follow.

02:22:46.520 --> 02:22:50.270
And now we're working
together to make something.

02:22:50.270 --> 02:22:51.980
Do I play or not play?

02:22:51.980 --> 02:22:53.510
When do I play?

02:22:53.510 --> 02:22:55.490
And you're really
paying attention.

02:22:55.490 --> 02:22:58.505
And can I add or you go
to start adding something.

02:22:58.505 --> 02:22:59.880
And someone else
added something.

02:22:59.880 --> 02:23:01.640
It's like, oh, I can't do that.

02:23:01.640 --> 02:23:06.260
And it's like everyone's
just in this thing,

02:23:06.260 --> 02:23:12.660
in this moment experiencing
this thing at once that you

02:23:12.660 --> 02:23:14.700
can feel as a listener.

02:23:14.700 --> 02:23:21.180
And we get to hear their
excitement of finding it.

02:23:21.180 --> 02:23:23.190
And it's thrilling
when it happens.

02:23:23.190 --> 02:23:25.410
So I like that experience.

02:23:25.410 --> 02:23:28.110
I feel that's what
the Dead do live.

02:23:28.110 --> 02:23:31.930
They'll play songs
in different ways.

02:23:31.930 --> 02:23:35.983
And again, I don't know
very much about the Dead.

02:23:35.983 --> 02:23:37.650
It's newer for me to
listen to the Dead.

02:23:37.650 --> 02:23:39.890
Growing up, I never
listen to the Dead,

02:23:39.890 --> 02:23:42.530
but probably because I
heard songs on their albums

02:23:42.530 --> 02:23:44.460
and thought, this doesn't
really speak to me.

02:23:44.460 --> 02:23:47.390
But I think that the
albums don't really reflect

02:23:47.390 --> 02:23:48.485
what's special about them.

02:23:48.485 --> 02:23:50.360
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I think
a lot of their shows

02:23:50.360 --> 02:23:52.940
were recorded or videotaped--

02:23:52.940 --> 02:23:56.390
RICK RUBIN: By fans
which they supported.

02:23:56.390 --> 02:23:59.280
They supported that everybody
come, everybody tape,

02:23:59.280 --> 02:24:01.580
everybody trade tapes.

02:24:01.580 --> 02:24:04.460
It made sense for
who that band was.

02:24:04.460 --> 02:24:06.650
ANDREW HUBERMAN: They
redefined or they defined--

02:24:06.650 --> 02:24:08.670
excuse me, the
notion of followers.

02:24:08.670 --> 02:24:10.640
I mean, people literally
gave up their lives

02:24:10.640 --> 02:24:14.240
or spent much of their lives
literally driving from city

02:24:14.240 --> 02:24:15.980
to city to follow them.

02:24:15.980 --> 02:24:19.220
RICK RUBIN: Because it's not
like going from city to city

02:24:19.220 --> 02:24:22.730
to watch a movie over and
over because it's not a movie.

02:24:22.730 --> 02:24:24.080
It's different every night.

02:24:24.080 --> 02:24:26.140
It's changing.

02:24:26.140 --> 02:24:29.090
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's pretty
incredible phenomenon.

02:24:29.090 --> 02:24:31.870
I don't know of anything else
quite like it except cults.

02:24:31.870 --> 02:24:33.850
And those often don't end well.

02:24:36.850 --> 02:24:39.880
I think a guy that mixed
the punch for the Jonestown

02:24:39.880 --> 02:24:41.238
Massacre went to my high school.

02:24:41.238 --> 02:24:42.280
RICK RUBIN: Is that true?

02:24:42.280 --> 02:24:43.060
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I think so.

02:24:43.060 --> 02:24:43.480
RICK RUBIN: That's amazing.

02:24:43.480 --> 02:24:44.980
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
My sister is really

02:24:44.980 --> 02:24:50.950
good at all this '70s, '80s
dark psychology trivia.

02:24:50.950 --> 02:24:52.360
She's a very light person.

02:24:52.360 --> 02:24:54.377
RICK RUBIN: Did you read
Season of the Witch.

02:24:54.377 --> 02:24:55.210
ANDREW HUBERMAN: No.

02:24:55.210 --> 02:24:57.100
RICK RUBIN: It's about
San Francisco in the '60s.

02:24:57.100 --> 02:24:57.640
It's great.

02:24:57.640 --> 02:24:59.290
You'll love it.

02:24:59.290 --> 02:25:00.100
Great book.

02:25:00.100 --> 02:25:02.740
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
ought to check it out.

02:25:02.740 --> 02:25:06.820
The way you describe
experiences going by in time,

02:25:06.820 --> 02:25:10.570
or things emerging in time,
and the creative process being

02:25:10.570 --> 02:25:16.540
a way of capturing those
moments, maybe rearranging,

02:25:16.540 --> 02:25:20.080
maybe watering, et
cetera, I thought

02:25:20.080 --> 02:25:22.930
was beautifully captured
in the analogy you

02:25:22.930 --> 02:25:29.710
gave about a conveyor
belt going by of things.

02:25:29.710 --> 02:25:32.710
We think of the creative process
like it's going to land in us,

02:25:32.710 --> 02:25:34.778
or we're going to
enter it, or that we're

02:25:34.778 --> 02:25:36.820
going to sit there in a
chair and grit our teeth.

02:25:36.820 --> 02:25:39.137
There's some Hemingway
quote where you just

02:25:39.137 --> 02:25:41.470
sit there and stare at the
page until the beads of blood

02:25:41.470 --> 02:25:43.690
form on your forehead or
something, maybe it was him,

02:25:43.690 --> 02:25:44.170
maybe it was--

02:25:44.170 --> 02:25:44.326
I don't know.

02:25:44.326 --> 02:25:45.680
It sounds like
Bukowski or something.

02:25:45.680 --> 02:25:47.180
Anyway, I'm going
to get this wrong.

02:25:47.180 --> 02:25:49.240
People tell me in the comments.

02:25:49.240 --> 02:25:50.770
Maybe no one said it.

02:25:50.770 --> 02:25:52.990
It was a dream.

02:25:52.990 --> 02:25:55.660
But I love this
conveyor belt thing.

02:25:55.660 --> 02:25:59.390
That reminds me of
being in laboratory,

02:25:59.390 --> 02:26:01.880
doing experiments thinking I
was trying to solve one thing,

02:26:01.880 --> 02:26:04.030
and then seeing
something else, and then

02:26:04.030 --> 02:26:06.530
having to make the decision,
like is that really cool enough

02:26:06.530 --> 02:26:10.400
to drop everything and go that
direction, or to spend a night,

02:26:10.400 --> 02:26:12.200
or a week, or a
career going that way?

02:26:12.200 --> 02:26:14.030
I mean, these are
big decisions, given

02:26:14.030 --> 02:26:15.920
that at least as far
as we know, we're

02:26:15.920 --> 02:26:19.540
going to live 100 years or less.

02:26:19.540 --> 02:26:22.840
But this idea that we have
thoughts and experiences

02:26:22.840 --> 02:26:23.560
in our past.

02:26:23.560 --> 02:26:25.330
And we can draw on,
and try, and make

02:26:25.330 --> 02:26:28.600
good decisions-- do we grab
these things off the conveyor

02:26:28.600 --> 02:26:29.890
or not?

02:26:29.890 --> 02:26:31.390
I'm hearing you.

02:26:31.390 --> 02:26:35.710
And I'm starting to realize
that being attached to the past

02:26:35.710 --> 02:26:37.930
might be the worst
thing that one could

02:26:37.930 --> 02:26:40.240
do in terms of
being able to make

02:26:40.240 --> 02:26:44.170
good decisions in this
context, because we

02:26:44.170 --> 02:26:47.000
have a playbook of what's
worked and what hasn't worked.

02:26:47.000 --> 02:26:49.060
But you actually
talk about this--

02:26:49.060 --> 02:26:52.060
there's a passage
in the book that--

02:26:52.060 --> 02:26:54.280
I'll just read it.

02:26:54.280 --> 02:26:56.830
To be aware of the assumption
that the way you work

02:26:56.830 --> 02:26:59.320
is the best way simply
because it's the way

02:26:59.320 --> 02:27:01.990
you've done it before.

02:27:01.990 --> 02:27:05.170
I sat with this page for almost
10 full minutes, which is not

02:27:05.170 --> 02:27:08.182
something I do very often.

02:27:08.182 --> 02:27:10.140
Maybe you could elaborate
on this a little bit.

02:27:10.140 --> 02:27:14.000
I mean, we want to have
mechanisms and routines we

02:27:14.000 --> 02:27:20.030
can trust, but this is, I
think, an important warning.

02:27:20.030 --> 02:27:23.010
RICK RUBIN: Yeah
when something works,

02:27:23.010 --> 02:27:28.235
it's easy to be
fooled into believing

02:27:28.235 --> 02:27:32.170
that's the way to do it
or that's the right way.

02:27:32.170 --> 02:27:33.480
It's just a way.

02:27:33.480 --> 02:27:36.900
And it's just a way that
happened to work that time.

02:27:36.900 --> 02:27:44.040
And this plays into
when you get advice

02:27:44.040 --> 02:27:48.140
from people who have
more experience than you.

02:27:48.140 --> 02:27:50.720
You explain your situation.

02:27:50.720 --> 02:27:52.730
They tell you their advice.

02:27:52.730 --> 02:27:54.290
The advice that
they're giving you

02:27:54.290 --> 02:27:57.860
is not based on your
life or your experience.

02:27:57.860 --> 02:28:00.680
It's based on their life
and their experience.

02:28:00.680 --> 02:28:03.290
And the stories
that they're telling

02:28:03.290 --> 02:28:07.540
are based on
experiences they've had,

02:28:07.540 --> 02:28:10.690
that have very different
data points than yours.

02:28:10.690 --> 02:28:14.660
So maybe they're
giving you good advice,

02:28:14.660 --> 02:28:17.360
but maybe they're giving
you good advice for them

02:28:17.360 --> 02:28:20.280
and not giving you
good advice for you.

02:28:20.280 --> 02:28:26.430
And it's easy when we
try something and have

02:28:26.430 --> 02:28:31.870
a result, a positive
result thinking,

02:28:31.870 --> 02:28:34.600
everybody can do this.

02:28:34.600 --> 02:28:38.600
The way I was vegan for
a long time, 22 years.

02:28:38.600 --> 02:28:42.190
And then I started eating--

02:28:42.190 --> 02:28:48.470
I started eating animal
protein and then eventually

02:28:48.470 --> 02:28:50.510
changed my diet a few
times to the point

02:28:50.510 --> 02:28:53.120
where I lost a lot of weight.

02:28:53.120 --> 02:28:54.655
The way that I did
it worked for me.

02:28:57.680 --> 02:29:04.400
Right before that happened, I
did something that I was told

02:29:04.400 --> 02:29:08.480
that everyone else who did what
you did, they all lost weight,

02:29:08.480 --> 02:29:10.380
for whatever reason, I didn't.

02:29:10.380 --> 02:29:15.270
So the idea that we know
what's right for someone else--

02:29:15.270 --> 02:29:17.480
I think, it's hard
enough to even figure out

02:29:17.480 --> 02:29:19.380
what's right for ourselves.

02:29:19.380 --> 02:29:24.770
And if we do somehow crack the
code of what's right for us,

02:29:24.770 --> 02:29:27.830
be happy we have it,
and then still know,

02:29:27.830 --> 02:29:29.510
I wonder if that's the only way.

02:29:29.510 --> 02:29:32.270
Maybe there's an even better
way that we're not considering.

02:29:35.114 --> 02:29:37.640
Not to get comfortable
with thinking

02:29:37.640 --> 02:29:40.040
we know how it works
just because we

02:29:40.040 --> 02:29:42.200
get the outcome we want.

02:29:42.200 --> 02:29:44.630
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I was raised
in science with a principle.

02:29:44.630 --> 02:29:46.640
It was literally dictated
to me as a principle,

02:29:46.640 --> 02:29:48.350
almost like a rule
of religion, which

02:29:48.350 --> 02:29:50.510
was that the brain is plastic.

02:29:50.510 --> 02:29:52.580
It can change and learn
until you're about 25.

02:29:52.580 --> 02:29:55.500
And then the critical
periods end and that's it.

02:29:55.500 --> 02:29:57.330
And this was a rule.

02:29:57.330 --> 02:29:59.670
Essentially, it was
dictated a Nobel Prize,

02:29:59.670 --> 02:30:01.890
which was very deserved,
given to my scientific great

02:30:01.890 --> 02:30:02.730
grandparents.

02:30:02.730 --> 02:30:06.080
They deserve it.

02:30:06.080 --> 02:30:09.950
But I was told there was no
changing of brain structure

02:30:09.950 --> 02:30:14.750
function in any meaningful
way after age 25 or so.

02:30:14.750 --> 02:30:17.900
It turns out that's
completely wrong.

02:30:17.900 --> 02:30:21.530
Sorry David and Torsten,
but they knew it was wrong.

02:30:21.530 --> 02:30:23.300
RICK RUBIN: Wow,
that's interesting.

02:30:23.300 --> 02:30:24.883
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Yeah, it was actively

02:30:24.883 --> 02:30:28.002
suppressed because of the
competitive nature of prizes

02:30:28.002 --> 02:30:29.210
and discoveries at that time.

02:30:29.210 --> 02:30:32.540
And a guy named Mike
Merzenich and his student,

02:30:32.540 --> 02:30:37.460
Gregg Recanzone were showing
that adult plasticity exists

02:30:37.460 --> 02:30:41.630
and only now is this really
starting to emerge as a theme,

02:30:41.630 --> 02:30:42.980
just crazy.

02:30:42.980 --> 02:30:46.250
There were so many reasons
and the textbooks said it.

02:30:46.250 --> 02:30:47.720
We were all told it.

02:30:47.720 --> 02:30:49.940
And it changed our behavior.

02:30:49.940 --> 02:30:52.220
Now we know this to
be completely false.

02:30:52.220 --> 02:30:54.200
There's plasticity
throughout the lifespan.

02:30:54.200 --> 02:30:55.830
There's limits to
it here and there,

02:30:55.830 --> 02:30:58.945
but it's just far and
away a different story.

02:30:58.945 --> 02:31:01.070
RICK RUBIN: So why would
that be the only time that

02:31:01.070 --> 02:31:01.790
ever happened?

02:31:01.790 --> 02:31:03.650
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Exactly.

02:31:03.650 --> 02:31:06.542
But the field was run
by a very small cabal

02:31:06.542 --> 02:31:07.500
of people at that time.

02:31:07.500 --> 02:31:10.520
RICK RUBIN: All fields are run
by a very small cabal of people

02:31:10.520 --> 02:31:14.300
who have an investment in things
being the way they are now

02:31:14.300 --> 02:31:15.470
because they're in charge.

02:31:15.470 --> 02:31:16.400
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
one of the great things

02:31:16.400 --> 02:31:18.380
about getting older is that--

02:31:18.380 --> 02:31:21.080
well, fortunately,
everyone eventually ages.

02:31:21.080 --> 02:31:22.010
And I hope that--

02:31:22.010 --> 02:31:23.977
David unfortunately passed away.

02:31:23.977 --> 02:31:24.560
He was lovely.

02:31:24.560 --> 02:31:25.310
Torsten is lovely.

02:31:25.310 --> 02:31:26.210
He's still alive.

02:31:26.210 --> 02:31:27.230
And they would say--

02:31:27.230 --> 02:31:29.330
I think Torsten
would say, yeah, we

02:31:29.330 --> 02:31:31.730
should have been a
little more open or kind

02:31:31.730 --> 02:31:33.650
in allowing these other ideas.

02:31:33.650 --> 02:31:34.883
But I think that--

02:31:34.883 --> 02:31:36.800
RICK RUBIN: But just
think about all the years

02:31:36.800 --> 02:31:41.570
that were wasted with
this misunderstanding.

02:31:41.570 --> 02:31:43.850
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Absolutely, absolutely.

02:31:43.850 --> 02:31:45.020
And it went beyond that.

02:31:45.020 --> 02:31:47.930
And there were BBC specials
that helped propagate this.

02:31:47.930 --> 02:31:49.670
And one of the
goals of the podcast

02:31:49.670 --> 02:31:53.660
has been to try and shed,
shine light on ideas

02:31:53.660 --> 02:31:55.250
that at first seem crazy.

02:31:55.250 --> 02:31:58.490
I know you and I are both
semi-obsessed with the health

02:31:58.490 --> 02:31:59.780
benefits of light.

02:31:59.780 --> 02:32:03.260
And you hear about this stuff
like negative ion therapy.

02:32:03.260 --> 02:32:04.340
It sounds crazy, right?

02:32:04.340 --> 02:32:06.298
Sounds like something
you would only hear about

02:32:06.298 --> 02:32:07.340
at Esalen or in Big Sur.

02:32:07.340 --> 02:32:10.910
Turns out negative ionization
therapy for sleep and mood

02:32:10.910 --> 02:32:13.970
is based on really amazing work
out of Columbia by a guy named

02:32:13.970 --> 02:32:15.260
Michael Terman.

02:32:15.260 --> 02:32:17.810
The Nobel Prize, I
think it was in 1916,

02:32:17.810 --> 02:32:22.010
was given for phototherapy
for the treatment of lupus.

02:32:22.010 --> 02:32:23.840
This idea that certain
wavelengths of light

02:32:23.840 --> 02:32:27.200
can help treat medical
conditions is not a new idea.

02:32:27.200 --> 02:32:29.430
But somehow, we see a red light.

02:32:29.430 --> 02:32:32.180
We're not used to seeing
red lights except in sunsets

02:32:32.180 --> 02:32:33.410
and on stoplights.

02:32:33.410 --> 02:32:38.630
And somehow, it bothers people
or it makes them feel like--

02:32:38.630 --> 02:32:43.820
RICK RUBIN: It undermines
a business model

02:32:43.820 --> 02:32:47.205
that doesn't take red
light into consideration.

02:32:47.205 --> 02:32:48.830
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Until it does and then

02:32:48.830 --> 02:32:51.230
it was-- and then
it's co-opted there.

02:32:51.230 --> 02:32:53.960
And the place what I
look to is acupuncture.

02:32:53.960 --> 02:32:56.690
For a lot of years, people
said, well, acupuncture, this

02:32:56.690 --> 02:32:58.850
is like no mechanism, no
mechanism, no mechanism.

02:32:58.850 --> 02:33:01.340
There's a lab at Harvard,
a guy named Qiufu Ma,

02:33:01.340 --> 02:33:03.530
who I reasonably
well, whose laboratory

02:33:03.530 --> 02:33:06.140
is dedicated to trying to figure
out the biological mechanisms

02:33:06.140 --> 02:33:06.830
of acupuncture.

02:33:06.830 --> 02:33:09.020
And they are discovering
what everyone

02:33:09.020 --> 02:33:10.610
is known for thousands
of years, which

02:33:10.610 --> 02:33:14.150
is that incredible effects
on anti-inflammation, the gut

02:33:14.150 --> 02:33:14.996
microbiome.

02:33:16.610 --> 02:33:18.740
RICK RUBIN: I have
a friend who was

02:33:18.740 --> 02:33:21.380
having a terrible back problem.

02:33:21.380 --> 02:33:24.200
And I suggested that he
see an acupuncturist.

02:33:24.200 --> 02:33:27.170
And he went to the
acupuncturist that I suggested.

02:33:27.170 --> 02:33:33.490
And his back problem completely
healed almost instantaneously.

02:33:33.490 --> 02:33:36.820
And I asked him, have
you been keeping up,

02:33:36.820 --> 02:33:38.347
because he had another flare up.

02:33:38.347 --> 02:33:38.930
He's like, no.

02:33:38.930 --> 02:33:42.490
I can't go back there because
acupuncture doesn't work.

02:33:42.490 --> 02:33:46.150
I said, well, you
saw it work for you.

02:33:46.150 --> 02:33:48.380
He's like, yeah, but
there's no science.

02:33:48.380 --> 02:33:50.820
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Wow, he's got it--

02:33:50.820 --> 02:33:55.472
now there's good science and
published in premier journals.

02:33:55.472 --> 02:33:57.680
What's interesting is there's
a little bit of science

02:33:57.680 --> 02:34:00.097
editorial, but since we like
to exchange information about

02:34:00.097 --> 02:34:02.810
health and things of that
sort, the editorial staff

02:34:02.810 --> 02:34:06.890
of a journal dictates what gets
published and what doesn't.

02:34:06.890 --> 02:34:09.470
And the premier journals
have an outsized effect

02:34:09.470 --> 02:34:10.880
on what the media covers.

02:34:10.880 --> 02:34:15.710
And so the beautiful thing
is the journal staff now

02:34:15.710 --> 02:34:19.760
is of the age that they grew
up hearing about acupuncture,

02:34:19.760 --> 02:34:22.610
hypnosis has a powerful
clinical effect

02:34:22.610 --> 02:34:27.030
if it's done right, yoga
nidra and similar practices.

02:34:27.030 --> 02:34:30.620
And so the tides are
changing, but I sometimes

02:34:30.620 --> 02:34:33.260
like to take a step back and
think, what are we confronted

02:34:33.260 --> 02:34:37.490
with now that seems crazy that
in 10 years, the kids that

02:34:37.490 --> 02:34:39.200
will be the-- because
to me they're kids,

02:34:39.200 --> 02:34:41.210
will be journal editors.

02:34:41.210 --> 02:34:43.910
I'm like, oh yeah, absolutely.

02:34:43.910 --> 02:34:45.922
I'm making this up but
putting tuning forks

02:34:45.922 --> 02:34:47.630
against your head or
something like that,

02:34:47.630 --> 02:34:51.050
like sound wave therapy.

02:34:51.050 --> 02:34:55.340
I think when one
adopts a stance of--

02:34:55.340 --> 02:34:59.690
we have to filter everything
through the limitations

02:34:59.690 --> 02:35:04.400
of our biology, but also
through the sociology,

02:35:04.400 --> 02:35:08.090
the way culture goes, it
becomes a different story.

02:35:08.090 --> 02:35:11.310
How do you deal with that,
not just in terms of health,

02:35:11.310 --> 02:35:14.602
but in terms of
thinking about anything?

02:35:14.602 --> 02:35:17.060
It sounds like you don't spend
a whole lot of time thinking

02:35:17.060 --> 02:35:19.070
about what people are going
to think is cool or not.

02:35:19.070 --> 02:35:19.820
RICK RUBIN: No, I can't.

02:35:19.820 --> 02:35:21.320
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You're
a punk rocker at heart.

02:35:21.320 --> 02:35:21.980
RICK RUBIN: Yes.

02:35:21.980 --> 02:35:23.980
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You still
are there, I got you.

02:35:23.980 --> 02:35:25.110
RICK RUBIN: I can't.

02:35:25.110 --> 02:35:26.960
I just know what I
like and what I don't.

02:35:26.960 --> 02:35:29.450
I know what works for
me and what doesn't.

02:35:29.450 --> 02:35:30.590
I try things.

02:35:30.590 --> 02:35:34.580
And I'm constantly looking
for new better solutions

02:35:34.580 --> 02:35:35.600
to anything.

02:35:35.600 --> 02:35:37.620
And wherever they come
from doesn't matter.

02:35:37.620 --> 02:35:38.780
It could come from--

02:35:38.780 --> 02:35:40.610
it could come from
Stanford or it

02:35:40.610 --> 02:35:44.660
could come from the guy talking
to himself on the street.

02:35:44.660 --> 02:35:46.760
If it works, I'm good.

02:35:46.760 --> 02:35:48.535
It doesn't really
matter to me at all.

02:35:51.740 --> 02:35:54.058
I don't hold any of it tightly.

02:35:54.058 --> 02:35:55.850
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well,
fortunately, there's

02:35:55.850 --> 02:35:57.558
now a division of the
National Institutes

02:35:57.558 --> 02:36:01.310
of Health called complementary
and alternative health.

02:36:01.310 --> 02:36:03.980
And it's amazing.

02:36:03.980 --> 02:36:08.030
NCCIH is run by a woman
who has published on--

02:36:08.030 --> 02:36:14.270
this is interesting, some
of the anti-cancer effects

02:36:14.270 --> 02:36:18.920
of things like acupuncture,
not that acupuncture

02:36:18.920 --> 02:36:25.780
can cure all cancers, but
real data that I think

02:36:25.780 --> 02:36:29.350
for a lot of people, certainly
of the generation above us,

02:36:29.350 --> 02:36:31.780
they just are not interested.

02:36:31.780 --> 02:36:38.320
It sheds new light on the Andrew
Wiles, the Paul Stametses,

02:36:38.320 --> 02:36:40.870
the wild ones.

02:36:40.870 --> 02:36:43.388
RICK RUBIN: Ozone therapy,
there are so many.

02:36:43.388 --> 02:36:44.680
There's so many we can look at.

02:36:44.680 --> 02:36:46.900
I mean for a long
time, nutrition

02:36:46.900 --> 02:36:49.300
was just thought of
as something that

02:36:49.300 --> 02:36:51.550
doesn't matter what you eat.

02:36:51.550 --> 02:36:53.380
It's what medicine
you take and what--

02:36:56.310 --> 02:36:58.470
the food is everything.

02:36:58.470 --> 02:37:01.590
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Food
is a powerful variable.

02:37:01.590 --> 02:37:04.350
In the landscape of
online nutrition,

02:37:04.350 --> 02:37:07.620
it's one of the third rails
for anyone like myself

02:37:07.620 --> 02:37:09.090
who's out there on social media.

02:37:09.090 --> 02:37:11.010
You do a very good
job of putting out

02:37:11.010 --> 02:37:14.940
posts on Twitter and Instagram,
but each day you take it down,

02:37:14.940 --> 02:37:15.900
you put up a new one.

02:37:15.900 --> 02:37:17.010
RICK RUBIN: And I
don't talk about any--

02:37:17.010 --> 02:37:18.000
I only talk about--

02:37:18.000 --> 02:37:20.040
I talk about creative ideas.

02:37:20.040 --> 02:37:21.900
I don't talk about
anything specific

02:37:21.900 --> 02:37:28.910
related to anything other
than maybe something like,

02:37:28.910 --> 02:37:30.130
don't believe what you hear.

02:37:30.130 --> 02:37:32.180
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
It's right, exactly.

02:37:32.180 --> 02:37:34.130
Well, in the landscape
of nutrition sometimes

02:37:34.130 --> 02:37:36.260
I now place it
through the filter

02:37:36.260 --> 02:37:37.700
of professional wrestling.

02:37:37.700 --> 02:37:40.520
You've got your vegans,
and your omnivores,

02:37:40.520 --> 02:37:44.420
and your carnivore MD.

02:37:44.420 --> 02:37:45.770
And you've got liver king.

02:37:45.770 --> 02:37:48.920
And you've got
everything in between.

02:37:48.920 --> 02:37:52.790
So you could translate that to
any number of different areas.

02:37:52.790 --> 02:37:54.123
Fashion probably has its people.

02:37:54.123 --> 02:37:55.582
I'm just not aware
of who they are.

02:37:55.582 --> 02:37:56.510
Music has theirs.

02:37:56.510 --> 02:37:58.430
And sports has theirs.

02:37:58.430 --> 02:38:01.850
And science has
theirs characters.

02:38:01.850 --> 02:38:05.810
So are we all just
pro-wrestling characters

02:38:05.810 --> 02:38:07.370
in these different
domains and we're

02:38:07.370 --> 02:38:10.580
taking ourselves and each
other way too seriously?

02:38:10.580 --> 02:38:14.178
RICK RUBIN: Yeah, it's all--
we don't know anything.

02:38:14.178 --> 02:38:16.220
If someone has an idea
and it sounds interesting,

02:38:16.220 --> 02:38:17.130
do you try it.

02:38:17.130 --> 02:38:19.497
And if it doesn't work, it's
OK and try something else.

02:38:19.497 --> 02:38:21.080
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
You're an empiricist.

02:38:21.080 --> 02:38:23.540
RICK RUBIN: Yeah, whatever
works, whatever works.

02:38:23.540 --> 02:38:26.990
And if something seems
interesting to you

02:38:26.990 --> 02:38:30.410
and you're excited by
it, why not try it?

02:38:30.410 --> 02:38:32.720
I try very fringy things.

02:38:32.720 --> 02:38:39.230
I like, in some ways,
the more unrealistic

02:38:39.230 --> 02:38:41.510
it seems, the more
interesting it is to me

02:38:41.510 --> 02:38:44.210
because I feel like that's
getting closer to something

02:38:44.210 --> 02:38:49.970
that somebody doesn't
want me to know.

02:38:49.970 --> 02:38:51.720
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But
you're not a big drug

02:38:51.720 --> 02:38:54.660
guy like the big psychedelic
craze that's happening now

02:38:54.660 --> 02:38:56.468
and that happened some years.

02:38:56.468 --> 02:38:57.760
RICK RUBIN: I'm not against it.

02:38:57.760 --> 02:38:59.843
It's just has never been
something that I've done.

02:38:59.843 --> 02:39:01.518
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Yeah, yeah, it's

02:39:01.518 --> 02:39:03.060
an interesting area
that's definitely

02:39:03.060 --> 02:39:07.020
making headway inside of
standard academic science

02:39:07.020 --> 02:39:07.905
and medicine now.

02:39:10.960 --> 02:39:14.620
RICK RUBIN: I'm interested in
non-pharmacological approaches

02:39:14.620 --> 02:39:16.900
to things, whatever they are.

02:39:16.900 --> 02:39:19.450
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well, I'm a
big believer that also that

02:39:19.450 --> 02:39:22.120
behavioral do's
and don'ts, first,

02:39:22.120 --> 02:39:25.730
are that they're the most fun
to explore because, in general,

02:39:25.730 --> 02:39:29.243
unless it's something like
jumping between buildings,

02:39:29.243 --> 02:39:31.160
doing parkour, or
something, most of the time,

02:39:31.160 --> 02:39:33.550
you're not going to
injure or harm yourself.

02:39:33.550 --> 02:39:35.650
There's more room for
iteration than there

02:39:35.650 --> 02:39:38.170
is with a pill or a potion.

02:39:38.170 --> 02:39:42.110
Although, certainly,
pharmacology has its place.

02:39:42.110 --> 02:39:46.870
So you've had creative
works certainly

02:39:46.870 --> 02:39:50.590
within the realm of music, also
comedy, and producing film,

02:39:50.590 --> 02:39:52.180
and other things.

02:39:52.180 --> 02:39:57.040
For somebody out there
who, of whatever age,

02:39:57.040 --> 02:40:00.100
maybe they're
creating, maybe they

02:40:00.100 --> 02:40:06.218
know they have this creative
antennae, the sources outside.

02:40:06.218 --> 02:40:07.510
What was it that Strummer said?

02:40:07.510 --> 02:40:09.820
I actually wrote this on
the wall of my laboratory.

02:40:09.820 --> 02:40:11.343
No input no output.

02:40:11.343 --> 02:40:12.010
RICK RUBIN: Wow.

02:40:12.010 --> 02:40:13.630
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
That Strummer's law.

02:40:13.630 --> 02:40:14.800
It's written in my laboratory.

02:40:14.800 --> 02:40:16.570
The people in my lab were
like, what's going on here?

02:40:16.570 --> 02:40:18.250
I think one guy
knew what that was,

02:40:18.250 --> 02:40:20.740
but it was a picture of him,
and picture of my bull dog,

02:40:20.740 --> 02:40:22.660
and no input no output.

02:40:22.660 --> 02:40:25.600
I don't think I can just
stay in a room with four

02:40:25.600 --> 02:40:27.683
walls, and a ceiling, and
nothing else and create.

02:40:27.683 --> 02:40:29.933
I mean, I know that there
are certain number of things

02:40:29.933 --> 02:40:31.390
in here, but I do
think accessing

02:40:31.390 --> 02:40:32.523
the world is important.

02:40:32.523 --> 02:40:34.940
RICK RUBIN: And the world is
giving us clues all the time.

02:40:34.940 --> 02:40:37.810
If we're paying attention,
that's another part of it.

02:40:37.810 --> 02:40:44.080
If you're paying attention, the
thing that you are looking for

02:40:44.080 --> 02:40:48.280
is being either whispered
or screamed at you

02:40:48.280 --> 02:40:52.570
in the outside world if
you're paying attention.

02:40:52.570 --> 02:40:57.100
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well, I forget
the exact title of the chapter,

02:40:57.100 --> 02:41:01.420
but there's a chapter
about staying open to clues

02:41:01.420 --> 02:41:06.780
or being on the
lookout for clues.

02:41:06.780 --> 02:41:09.600
Now, I feel tempted to
look for the exact title

02:41:09.600 --> 02:41:10.800
of that chapter.

02:41:10.800 --> 02:41:12.420
RICK RUBIN: It's probably look
for clues is what I guess.

02:41:12.420 --> 02:41:14.460
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Look for clues
sounds like it sounds right.

02:41:14.460 --> 02:41:16.410
And since you wrote it,
I'm guessing that's right.

02:41:16.410 --> 02:41:18.160
RICK RUBIN: Well it's
something like that.

02:41:18.160 --> 02:41:21.690
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So do you think
there are clues in everywhere?

02:41:21.690 --> 02:41:24.210
RICK RUBIN: Yes, I think
there are clues everywhere.

02:41:24.210 --> 02:41:27.780
If we pay attention,
we'll hear a phrase.

02:41:27.780 --> 02:41:30.150
It'll trigger a thought.

02:41:30.150 --> 02:41:32.390
We'll see something unexpected.

02:41:35.220 --> 02:41:38.710
If someone recommends
something to you,

02:41:38.710 --> 02:41:40.170
maybe it's a coincidence.

02:41:40.170 --> 02:41:42.390
If three people recommend
the same thing to you

02:41:42.390 --> 02:41:45.750
maybe it's not, who knows?

02:41:45.750 --> 02:41:47.720
Who knows?

02:41:47.720 --> 02:41:55.420
I do believe the universe is
on the side of creativity.

02:41:55.420 --> 02:42:00.740
And the universe is
supporting things to happen.

02:42:00.740 --> 02:42:02.840
And they can happen
through you or they could

02:42:02.840 --> 02:42:04.710
happen through someone else.

02:42:04.710 --> 02:42:09.503
So if you're paying attention,
maybe it'll happen through you.

02:42:09.503 --> 02:42:11.670
ANDREW HUBERMAN: We had a
guest on the podcast named

02:42:11.670 --> 02:42:12.570
Justin Sonnenburg.

02:42:12.570 --> 02:42:14.490
He's an expert in
the gut microbiome.

02:42:14.490 --> 02:42:17.400
And he applied something
that-- without knowing,

02:42:17.400 --> 02:42:21.510
he applied the
opposite principle,

02:42:21.510 --> 02:42:23.340
the opposite is true principle.

02:42:23.340 --> 02:42:25.800
We were talking about these
trillions of gut microbiota

02:42:25.800 --> 02:42:28.393
that clearly are doing
amazing things to create,

02:42:28.393 --> 02:42:30.060
neurotransmitters,
and govern our brain,

02:42:30.060 --> 02:42:33.270
and even decision making, how
much sugar is in our system

02:42:33.270 --> 02:42:35.400
driving appetite, et cetera.

02:42:35.400 --> 02:42:39.360
And he said, we think
of them as cargo,

02:42:39.360 --> 02:42:42.630
but maybe we're just vehicles
and they're in charge.

02:42:42.630 --> 02:42:44.190
That all of our
interactions like

02:42:44.190 --> 02:42:46.023
every time we shake
hands or touch our eyes,

02:42:46.023 --> 02:42:47.670
we're exchanging gut microbiota.

02:42:47.670 --> 02:42:52.230
And we think of intelligence
as thinking and intelligence.

02:42:52.230 --> 02:42:54.270
And he's a microbiologist.

02:42:54.270 --> 02:42:58.830
And in all seriousness
he said, maybe

02:42:58.830 --> 02:43:00.300
we're the ones
being manipulated.

02:43:00.300 --> 02:43:01.470
We're the house cats.

02:43:01.470 --> 02:43:03.570
And we think here we are,
we're falling in love,

02:43:03.570 --> 02:43:07.350
and kissing, and shaking hands,
and washing hands, and doing

02:43:07.350 --> 02:43:09.990
all sorts of things to isolate
or connect with one another.

02:43:09.990 --> 02:43:11.760
And maybe the gut
microbiota are really

02:43:11.760 --> 02:43:13.710
trying to optimize
their survival.

02:43:13.710 --> 02:43:17.580
RICK RUBIN: That's what Laird
Hamilton said that at one point

02:43:17.580 --> 02:43:18.810
in the sauna--

02:43:18.810 --> 02:43:22.260
that when you're in the
sauna, it's really hot.

02:43:22.260 --> 02:43:25.920
The feeling that you have
of wanting to get out

02:43:25.920 --> 02:43:30.087
could be the bad critters in
your body that can't handle it.

02:43:30.087 --> 02:43:31.920
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Like
let's get out of here.

02:43:31.920 --> 02:43:36.660
RICK RUBIN: Are trying to
convince you, from the inside,

02:43:36.660 --> 02:43:37.440
to get out.

02:43:37.440 --> 02:43:40.380
Maybe that's where the feeling
of being compelled to get out

02:43:40.380 --> 02:43:41.250
comes from.

02:43:41.250 --> 02:43:43.350
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So Elon
getting us all to Mars

02:43:43.350 --> 02:43:45.540
might be a bit of--

02:43:45.540 --> 02:43:47.580
maybe they just
want to get to Mars.

02:43:47.580 --> 02:43:48.780
And so they're

02:43:48.780 --> 02:43:49.960
RICK RUBIN: Maybe.

02:43:49.960 --> 02:43:51.502
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Because I'm starting

02:43:51.502 --> 02:43:53.970
to feel like I'm channeling
Lex Freedman here for a moment.

02:43:53.970 --> 02:43:56.795
No, I think this considering
the opposite is really key.

02:43:56.795 --> 02:43:58.170
And while it might
sound mystical

02:43:58.170 --> 02:44:00.960
to people or a little bit like
we're just playing with ideas,

02:44:00.960 --> 02:44:03.330
that's exactly what
you do in science.

02:44:03.330 --> 02:44:06.150
Someone walks in with a
result and says, I found this.

02:44:06.150 --> 02:44:06.810
This is true.

02:44:06.810 --> 02:44:09.570
And you say, but what if
it's all something else?

02:44:09.570 --> 02:44:11.010
A good example
might be-- here I'm

02:44:11.010 --> 02:44:13.440
pulling from podcast
episodes that we've had.

02:44:13.440 --> 02:44:16.740
But Alia Crum is this
amazing psychologist

02:44:16.740 --> 02:44:18.690
who works on belief effects.

02:44:18.690 --> 02:44:21.420
Your knowledge strongly shapes
the physiological outcome.

02:44:21.420 --> 02:44:25.510
And she had this amazing
graduate thesis where she said,

02:44:25.510 --> 02:44:29.660
what if all of exercise
is placebo, all of it?

02:44:29.660 --> 02:44:32.010
Yeah, burn some calories
and does some things.

02:44:32.010 --> 02:44:33.260
Turns out this isn't the case.

02:44:33.260 --> 02:44:35.728
But it turns out a lot of
the effects of exercise,

02:44:35.728 --> 02:44:37.520
positive effects--
lowering blood pressure,

02:44:37.520 --> 02:44:41.480
relieving stress,
positive are placebo

02:44:41.480 --> 02:44:43.220
but nobody thinks
of it like that

02:44:43.220 --> 02:44:47.587
because we're so attached to
calories burned, et cetera.

02:44:47.587 --> 02:44:49.170
RICK RUBIN: I think
that's a big point

02:44:49.170 --> 02:44:54.720
that the belief part of it is
a huge part of the conversation

02:44:54.720 --> 02:44:56.370
about everything.

02:44:56.370 --> 02:44:58.590
What we believe has power.

02:44:58.590 --> 02:45:01.050
If we believe we can
make something great,

02:45:01.050 --> 02:45:03.120
the chances of us
making something great

02:45:03.120 --> 02:45:06.670
are better than if we
don't believe we can.

02:45:06.670 --> 02:45:11.610
So I would say, any
ability to harness

02:45:11.610 --> 02:45:16.360
your belief on your behalf is
a really healthy thing to do.

02:45:16.360 --> 02:45:19.560
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And one
thing that you make very clear

02:45:19.560 --> 02:45:23.490
is that while our own abilities
may come into question

02:45:23.490 --> 02:45:26.870
from time to time,
you absolutely

02:45:26.870 --> 02:45:32.090
believe that the elements from
which to create are out there.

02:45:32.090 --> 02:45:33.360
RICK RUBIN: Absolutely.

02:45:33.360 --> 02:45:36.290
All the elements are here.

02:45:36.290 --> 02:45:37.880
Everything is here.

02:45:37.880 --> 02:45:39.210
We get to pick and choose.

02:45:39.210 --> 02:45:43.250
We get to-- the conveyor belts
going by with the little gifts.

02:45:43.250 --> 02:45:48.350
And we can-- first, we have to
notice there's a conveyor belt.

02:45:48.350 --> 02:45:50.600
Then we notice the gifts.

02:45:50.600 --> 02:45:52.640
And then that's
the starting point.

02:45:52.640 --> 02:45:58.090
And then we may even
feel empowered enough

02:45:58.090 --> 02:45:59.650
to grab one of the
gifts, and open it

02:45:59.650 --> 02:46:01.420
up, and see what's inside.

02:46:01.420 --> 02:46:04.300
And then maybe that started
something really beautiful

02:46:04.300 --> 02:46:07.060
that we wouldn't have done.

02:46:09.570 --> 02:46:13.650
Everything that I
make or have made

02:46:13.650 --> 02:46:17.670
has always been based
on something that I see

02:46:17.670 --> 02:46:22.630
or hear that allows
me to see something

02:46:22.630 --> 02:46:24.803
that I didn't see before.

02:46:24.803 --> 02:46:27.220
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So I was going
to ask you whether or not,

02:46:27.220 --> 02:46:30.150
it's important to be
happy in order to create,

02:46:30.150 --> 02:46:32.160
but certainly a lot of
people that were unhappy

02:46:32.160 --> 02:46:33.540
were still able to create.

02:46:33.540 --> 02:46:36.540
But the more I listen
to you, it seems

02:46:36.540 --> 02:46:39.743
that it's really about an
ability to pay attention.

02:46:39.743 --> 02:46:40.410
RICK RUBIN: Yes.

02:46:40.410 --> 02:46:43.920
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So if I'm
unhappy or if I'm happy

02:46:43.920 --> 02:46:46.380
may not be as relevant
as whether or not

02:46:46.380 --> 02:46:49.020
I'm able to stay undistracted.

02:46:49.020 --> 02:46:49.980
RICK RUBIN: Yes.

02:46:49.980 --> 02:46:50.970
I would say that's--

02:46:50.970 --> 02:46:56.370
I would say being able to stay
present in the work is probably

02:46:56.370 --> 02:46:59.370
the most important part of it.

02:46:59.370 --> 02:47:05.180
And how you feel is less of
an issue unless how you feel

02:47:05.180 --> 02:47:10.063
gets in the way of you feeling
how the work makes you feel.

02:47:10.063 --> 02:47:11.230
Do you know what I'm saying?

02:47:11.230 --> 02:47:15.830
If you're in a lot of pain
and you're looking at a piece

02:47:15.830 --> 02:47:20.210
of art, it may be hard to know
how that art makes you feel

02:47:20.210 --> 02:47:21.410
because you're--

02:47:21.410 --> 02:47:25.205
the big signal in your
body is the physical pain.

02:47:25.205 --> 02:47:27.330
I'm sure there are some
people who can do that too,

02:47:27.330 --> 02:47:30.805
who can, even through the
physical pain, can feel it.

02:47:30.805 --> 02:47:33.180
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Now, there's
this idea of transmutation,

02:47:33.180 --> 02:47:37.290
of taking one emotion and
contorting it and co-opting it

02:47:37.290 --> 02:47:39.780
into another action
in an adaptive way.

02:47:39.780 --> 02:47:43.260
But this idea of
distraction being a problem,

02:47:43.260 --> 02:47:44.970
this really resonates, I think.

02:47:44.970 --> 02:47:46.830
When I think of times
of great productivity

02:47:46.830 --> 02:47:49.410
is when I was able
to be undistracted.

02:47:49.410 --> 02:47:52.180
I could also see how success
can be its own distraction.

02:47:52.180 --> 02:47:54.800
This is often discussed in the
context of fighting sports,

02:47:54.800 --> 02:47:56.550
where someone starts
making a lot of money

02:47:56.550 --> 02:47:58.633
and pretty soon their focus
becomes all the things

02:47:58.633 --> 02:48:00.630
they can access with
their success as opposed

02:48:00.630 --> 02:48:02.797
to the thing that got them
there in the first place.

02:48:05.000 --> 02:48:07.190
Keeping an underdog mentality.

02:48:07.190 --> 02:48:08.100
RICK RUBIN: Yeah.

02:48:08.100 --> 02:48:09.600
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Before we conclude,

02:48:09.600 --> 02:48:13.070
I do want to ask you about
one other aspect of process,

02:48:13.070 --> 02:48:15.470
which is meditation.

02:48:15.470 --> 02:48:18.080
Meditation is interesting
to me because when

02:48:18.080 --> 02:48:20.780
we close our eyes as
most meditations are done

02:48:20.780 --> 02:48:25.400
and we focus on our brain,
our brain has no sensation.

02:48:25.400 --> 02:48:26.030
Like if we--

02:48:26.030 --> 02:48:28.100
RICK RUBIN: I wouldn't
say we focus on our brain.

02:48:28.100 --> 02:48:29.808
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Or
we focus on something

02:48:29.808 --> 02:48:31.820
other than our normal
experience, is that--

02:48:31.820 --> 02:48:33.522
how would you define meditation?

02:48:33.522 --> 02:48:35.480
RICK RUBIN: Well, there's
different-- there are

02:48:35.480 --> 02:48:38.270
different types of meditation.

02:48:41.700 --> 02:48:45.180
Either way, I would say there
is no form of meditation where

02:48:45.180 --> 02:48:46.720
we're focused on our brain.

02:48:46.720 --> 02:48:47.820
ANDREW HUBERMAN: OK, good.

02:48:47.820 --> 02:48:49.295
I'm glad we disagree.

02:48:49.295 --> 02:48:51.670
RICK RUBIN: I would say, here
are the things that happen.

02:48:51.670 --> 02:48:56.230
We either are engaging
in a mantra, which

02:48:56.230 --> 02:49:00.550
would be a version
of almost creating

02:49:00.550 --> 02:49:04.840
a trance for ourselves, not
unlike listening to something

02:49:04.840 --> 02:49:08.500
when we go to sleep that would
distract our conscious mind

02:49:08.500 --> 02:49:11.850
from participating.

02:49:11.850 --> 02:49:18.300
We would be overriding
the talking mind with just

02:49:18.300 --> 02:49:21.270
a sound that we're
generating, or a word,

02:49:21.270 --> 02:49:24.570
or phrase, a series of phrases.

02:49:24.570 --> 02:49:28.500
Meta meditation is a
loving kindness meditation

02:49:28.500 --> 02:49:31.440
with phrases,
could be that or it

02:49:31.440 --> 02:49:33.150
could be focused on the breath.

02:49:33.150 --> 02:49:35.790
But the purpose of being
focused on the breath

02:49:35.790 --> 02:49:43.710
is to not hear the self-talk
that we normally have.

02:49:43.710 --> 02:49:48.390
It's a single pointed
focus exercise in those

02:49:48.390 --> 02:49:49.140
that I described.

02:49:49.140 --> 02:49:52.290
The other version is an
awareness meditation,

02:49:52.290 --> 02:49:54.600
where you're closing your eyes.

02:49:54.600 --> 02:50:01.130
And you're being with
whatever is and noticing--

02:50:01.130 --> 02:50:04.280
so if we were to do it
now, and you could do it

02:50:04.280 --> 02:50:08.090
eyes open or eyes closed
with an awareness practice.

02:50:08.090 --> 02:50:12.790
But the first thing that I
would do is I would feel--

02:50:12.790 --> 02:50:14.680
I feel a little
ringing in my ears.

02:50:14.680 --> 02:50:18.470
It might be from the
electronic equipment around us.

02:50:18.470 --> 02:50:20.140
And I don't mean that
I hear the sound.

02:50:20.140 --> 02:50:23.110
It's like a vibration.

02:50:23.110 --> 02:50:27.756
I hear cars passing
in the distance.

02:50:27.756 --> 02:50:35.205
Let's see what else comes out, I
can feel a feeling in my chest.

02:50:39.060 --> 02:50:45.320
I can feel this part of my face.

02:50:45.320 --> 02:50:47.770
I'm not sure why.

02:50:47.770 --> 02:50:51.560
It feels like it's
related to my jaw.

02:50:51.560 --> 02:50:54.790
More car sounds.

02:50:54.790 --> 02:50:57.025
I'm aware of a little
feeling of warmth.

02:51:00.140 --> 02:51:02.810
So now, I would say the
room feels a bit warm.

02:51:02.810 --> 02:51:06.080
I wasn't aware of that before,
when I wasn't just being

02:51:06.080 --> 02:51:08.660
with what's happening.

02:51:08.660 --> 02:51:10.415
I feel a little itch
on my left shoulder.

02:51:14.090 --> 02:51:16.530
So that would be an
awareness practice,

02:51:16.530 --> 02:51:19.040
which is another kind of
meditation where you're just

02:51:19.040 --> 02:51:20.840
paying attention
to what's going on.

02:51:20.840 --> 02:51:21.860
There's no story.

02:51:21.860 --> 02:51:24.200
There's no this means this.

02:51:24.200 --> 02:51:27.440
None of those things.

02:51:27.440 --> 02:51:30.230
It's like an inventory,
almost, of everything

02:51:30.230 --> 02:51:32.030
that comes up when it comes up.

02:51:32.030 --> 02:51:34.350
And you do that for
a period of time.

02:51:34.350 --> 02:51:38.090
But in all of those
cases, in the example

02:51:38.090 --> 02:51:39.710
of doing the
awareness meditation,

02:51:39.710 --> 02:51:43.580
or doing a mantra meditation,
or focusing on the breath,

02:51:43.580 --> 02:51:46.800
in none of them am I thinking.

02:51:46.800 --> 02:51:51.334
And none of them am
I concentrating on--

02:51:51.334 --> 02:51:57.090
I'm being aware of
sense perceptions

02:51:57.090 --> 02:52:02.160
in the awareness one or
in the other meditations.

02:52:02.160 --> 02:52:05.280
I'm doing a practice
so that I'm not

02:52:05.280 --> 02:52:07.653
aware of thinking
about anything else.

02:52:07.653 --> 02:52:09.570
ANDREW HUBERMAN: When
did you start meditating

02:52:09.570 --> 02:52:12.210
and how often do
you meditate now?

02:52:12.210 --> 02:52:13.830
RICK RUBIN: I learned
when I was 14.

02:52:13.830 --> 02:52:15.600
And I started with TM.

02:52:15.600 --> 02:52:17.580
And that's probably
the meditation

02:52:17.580 --> 02:52:20.610
that I've done the most in
my life and I come back to.

02:52:20.610 --> 02:52:24.510
Although I tried many
different kinds and also

02:52:24.510 --> 02:52:26.640
different physical
forms of meditation--

02:52:26.640 --> 02:52:28.470
Tai Chi, things like that.

02:52:28.470 --> 02:52:31.630
I meditated for
five or six years.

02:52:31.630 --> 02:52:36.120
And then I stopped when I
went to school, to university.

02:52:36.120 --> 02:52:38.700
And then I started again
several years later.

02:52:38.700 --> 02:52:42.000
And when I started again,
I realized how profound

02:52:42.000 --> 02:52:45.660
it wasn't in me that I
had done it when I did it

02:52:45.660 --> 02:52:52.420
So I usually have some
sort of a practice.

02:52:52.420 --> 02:52:56.100
In some ways, the beach walks
could be a form of meditation.

02:52:59.080 --> 02:53:01.720
But for me, typically,
I would wake up.

02:53:01.720 --> 02:53:06.400
It'd be the first thing I would
do during that in-between time,

02:53:06.400 --> 02:53:08.770
maybe go out in the
sun, close my eyes,

02:53:08.770 --> 02:53:13.390
and meditate before
starting my day.

02:53:13.390 --> 02:53:15.400
If I'm doing it twice
a day, the second time

02:53:15.400 --> 02:53:17.860
would probably be
right before dinner

02:53:17.860 --> 02:53:20.650
if I'm doing it on
a regular schedule.

02:53:20.650 --> 02:53:23.950
Then if I find myself
on an airplane,

02:53:23.950 --> 02:53:27.350
I might meditate for an hour.

02:53:27.350 --> 02:53:30.740
I can remember, one time,
meditating the entire flight

02:53:30.740 --> 02:53:34.160
from New York to LA, just
was a great opportunity

02:53:34.160 --> 02:53:36.740
to do a deep dive.

02:53:36.740 --> 02:53:37.760
And time passes.

02:53:37.760 --> 02:53:39.210
You lose track of time.

02:53:39.210 --> 02:53:41.890
You don't even know--

02:53:41.890 --> 02:53:43.600
it's like going to
sleep and waking up.

02:53:43.600 --> 02:53:45.760
You don't feel like
that was eight hours.

02:53:45.760 --> 02:53:48.840
It's just time stops.

02:53:51.940 --> 02:53:54.290
Not always, but when it
does, it's a great feeling.

02:53:54.290 --> 02:53:55.210
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Yeah, you've sent me

02:53:55.210 --> 02:53:57.160
some meditations
including the one that you

02:53:57.160 --> 02:53:59.080
did on that transatlantic--

02:53:59.080 --> 02:54:00.160
transcontinental flight.

02:54:00.160 --> 02:54:04.060
And I've been trying to get-- do
longer and longer meditations,

02:54:04.060 --> 02:54:06.610
but I've always
meditated a little bit.

02:54:06.610 --> 02:54:10.060
But your meditation practice is
one that I'm starting to adopt,

02:54:10.060 --> 02:54:11.710
maybe we could
convince you to give us

02:54:11.710 --> 02:54:14.710
a suggestions of one or two
and we can link out to them

02:54:14.710 --> 02:54:15.315
for listeners.

02:54:15.315 --> 02:54:16.750
I'm sure they'd appreciate.

02:54:16.750 --> 02:54:20.710
RICK RUBIN: And there's also
meditation like practices

02:54:20.710 --> 02:54:23.710
to do that involve--

02:54:23.710 --> 02:54:27.730
there's something called
the surgical series

02:54:27.730 --> 02:54:34.250
from the Monroe Institute which
I used when I had a surgery.

02:54:34.250 --> 02:54:36.510
You listen to this recording.

02:54:36.510 --> 02:54:41.390
And it both allows your
body to heal much faster

02:54:41.390 --> 02:54:49.300
and remove some of the trauma
that goes on when getting cut

02:54:49.300 --> 02:54:53.240
open-- it's traumatic.

02:54:53.240 --> 02:54:56.270
But just through listening
to certain things,

02:54:56.270 --> 02:55:00.860
you can have a really powerful
effect, heal much faster.

02:55:00.860 --> 02:55:09.040
I remember I was about to
be put under for a surgery.

02:55:09.040 --> 02:55:11.640
And my eyes were closed.

02:55:11.640 --> 02:55:16.610
And I wasn't
communicating with anyone

02:55:16.610 --> 02:55:18.095
there because I
was going inside.

02:55:20.660 --> 02:55:22.010
And my wife was with me.

02:55:22.010 --> 02:55:23.090
And they came in.

02:55:23.090 --> 02:55:26.870
And they said, oh, so they
already gave Rick the sedative

02:55:26.870 --> 02:55:28.040
because he's ready to go in.

02:55:28.040 --> 02:55:29.540
I don't think you
gave him anything.

02:55:29.540 --> 02:55:32.990
He's like, look at his numbers.

02:55:32.990 --> 02:55:34.120
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I love it.

02:55:34.120 --> 02:55:38.150
Yeah, it's an amazingly
powerful practice

02:55:38.150 --> 02:55:41.090
that I like because
anyone can cultivate.

02:55:41.090 --> 02:55:43.610
RICK RUBIN:
Absolutely, absolutely.

02:55:43.610 --> 02:55:46.040
And there's no good
or bad version.

02:55:46.040 --> 02:55:47.330
It really is just--

02:55:47.330 --> 02:55:53.232
if you learn a technique and
show up and do it, it works.

02:55:53.232 --> 02:55:54.940
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well,
I love that you're

02:55:54.940 --> 02:55:59.200
so willing to share what
you do in your process.

02:55:59.200 --> 02:56:02.240
Listen, I just want to say thank
you for a number of things.

02:56:02.240 --> 02:56:05.710
I want to thank you for
the music you've created

02:56:05.710 --> 02:56:07.840
